85B0721100016F800C0FA003C0A0003AC00D76700D70800057F005C0C004D81180035AE0015A900A77500C86E12C301110003B}}
\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Three modalities used by the BOD in its "scheme" to capture foreign  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  deposits, according to lawsuit\b0\par\par\uc2 1,490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Affected parties of the BOD's foreign banks allege in the lawsuit filed before the TSJ that most of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits of their clients were converted into investments in public debt bonds of the Republic or in securities of \uc2 PDVSA\uc2 . That is why -they argue- that the problems of the Venezuelan entity began when the government had difficulties to honor its commitments with the bondholders and fell into default or non-payment.\par \par \uc2 The case of Grupo Financiero BOD has gained relevance in the last few weeks due to the actions taken by the lawyers of the affected parties, who are seeking the return of their deposits or justice. They state that it is no longer only an issue of responsibility towards the depositors who have sued the bank, but that it extends to other dimensions such as the affectation of human rights.\par \par \uc2 Recently, the lawyers of 200 clients of the BOD who lost their savings with the liquidation and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the banks abroad, went to the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights (UNHCHR), Volker T\u252\'00\'FCrk, to denounce the Venezuelan State for denial of justice. They consider that there is a clear violation of fundamental rights such as access to justice, due to the delay of the Supreme Court of Justice (TSJ) in admitting or rejecting the lawsuit filed in October 2022.\par \par \uc2 Several reports have been made on what happened with the deposits of Venezuelan clients of the BOD to whom the resources were transferred to offshore banks such as Banco Orinoco NV of Cura\u231\'00\'E7ao, BOI Bank of Antigua and Barbuda, All Bank Corp of Panama and Bancam\u233\'00\'E9rica of the Dominican Republic. While executives of the Venezuelan financial group have not given explanations to the public opinion, about what their clients allege, except for the press conference that the president of BOD, V\u237\'00\'EDctor Vargas Irausquin gave in 2019 when the financial authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the entity and its subsidiaries abroad.\par \par \uc2 What is known so far about the case are the allegations contained in the lawsuit before the TSJ against the Venezuelan financial group by its clients, who denounce that they have not been able to have access to their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, which has generated significant losses for them and their families.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers in the case, Carlos Calder\u243\'00\'F3n and Roberto Hung, consider that they are before "one of the most important legal cases in Venezuela, that of the lawsuit for collective rights by depositors of the banks that make up the BPD Financial Group who see their assets at risk".\par \par \uc2 The following are the allegations and the most important points contained in the lawsuit filed before the TSJ against the BOD:\par \par \uc2 -Cartera de Inversiones Venezolanas C.A. is the figure that represents and leads the holding company that constitutes the BOD Financial Group, made up of insurance, stock market and banking entities, in addition to four entities of foreign nationality: Banco del Orinoco N.V. of Cura\u231\'00\'E7ao, BOI Bank Corporation of Antigua and Barbuda, All Bank Corp of Panama and Banco M\u250\'00\'FAltiple de las Am\u233\'00\'E9ricas (Bancamerica) of the Dominican Republic.\par \par \uc2 -The \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of each one of these entities are fully linked to each other, with crossed and recurrent \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that imply the transfer of funds between them, taking advantage of the facilities allowed by the offshore banking condition and the restricted banking supervision applied in the different territories where they are domiciled. They had a common international correspondent bank (\uc2 Novo Banco S.A\uc2 .) of Portuguese nationality, which facilitated international transfer \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , precisely because it was integrated into a single financial group.\par \par \uc2 -In practice, the five entities of the BOD Financial Group are related, especially taking into account that three of the banking entities BOD, \uc2 BDO\uc2  and \uc2 BOI\uc2 , serve portfolios of Venezuelan clients almost entirely, and the remaining two, AllBank and Bancamerica, also have Venezuelan and foreign clients.\par \par \uc2 -The BOD Financial Group activated from both its physical and human structures, the opening of accounts and transfer of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Group's foreign entities; that is to say, from Venezuela, there was the attraction of Venezuelan customers that make up the deposits in foreign banks, as well as the processing of the opening of accounts, transfer of funds, including those originally made in bolivars in the BOD Venezuela; granting of credits in bolivars with guarantees and backing in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits constituted in the Group's foreign banks and many more binding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In fact, the great majority of us and of those affected in general, never went to the headquarters or agencies of those banks outside Venezuela, everything was done from here, in the offices of BOD Venezuela", refers the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 -Different modalities were used by the BOD under the exchange control regime that came into effect in 2003, to capture funds from Venezuelan citizens and companies with the purpose of transferring them to the different banking entities of the group abroad.\par \par \uc2 -It also highlights a modality they used to capture foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had its origin in 2010 until 2013, which was based on the mechanism established by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ) called System of Transactions with Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Securities (Sitme) that allowed individuals and legal entities to acquire foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through duly authorized banking entities (authorized operator as was the BOD), in auctions programmed by the \uc2 BCV\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It was through this mechanism that some of us who subscribed here obtained from the BOD foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was subsequently transferred to the Group's international banks. It is then when the BOD, established as a condition to serve as processing bank and subsequent custodian of the funds in dollars through any of its international banks, that the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  be received by the international entities of the Group where a Fixed Term Deposit (DPF) had to be constituted for a minimum period of one (1) year, a process that was otherwise channeled from Venezuela by the bank's executives", it is stated in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Once the imposed term of the security had elapsed, that is to say, once the one-year term of the security issued had expired, the DPF was automatically renewed despite the timely notice of the beneficiaries to terminate the investment.\par \par \uc2 -From 2015 onwards, the funding mechanism of international banks with deposits from Venezuelan citizens and companies was through loan \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and subsequent exchange in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out in the Group's exchange houses. The BOD granted commercial loans in bolivars with collateral on the DPFs that the clients had in the foreign banks, forced in the international banks. Part of the proceeds of these commercial loans in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was used for new exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  whose proceeds funded the accounts of these clients of the BOD Financial Group in the international banks through foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange houses that are also part of the holding company.\par \par \uc2 -Another outstanding modality used by the BOD as a mechanism to capture funds in Venezuela and transfer them to its "sister" institutions located in the Caribbean, was the issuance of a guaranteed Credit Card (TDC). It consisted of issuing a TDC backed by BOI Bank with a guarantee deposit equivalent to 150% of its credit limit. This means that the customer had to deposit, for example, $15,000 to receive a TDC with a $10,000 limit.\par \par \uc2 -The immense liabilities collected from Venezuelan depositors, estimated at a little more than one billion dollars, were used by all the offshore banking entities that make up the Group, "in high risk investments for the time". The vast majority of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits were converted into investments in public debt bonds of the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela or in securities of state-owned companies, specifically in bonds issued by \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos de Venezuela\uc2  , denominated in the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and generating interest payable in the same \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , taking advantage of the accelerated trend of public indebtedness of the country since 2007.\par \par \uc2 "In November 2017 the wheel got stuck. Venezuela could not withstand the fall of its oil revenues and was unable to pay its debts, which resulted in the consideration by the main world financial risk rating agencies of the existence of a state of selective default (selective default) of its public debt securities. It is then that the problems of the entities in the Caribbean that make up the BOD Financial Group dominated by the holding company Cartera de Inversiones Venezolanas C.A. appear; one by one they begin to show liquidity and insolvency problems, from defaulting among themselves the return of deposits and securities in custody", it was indicated in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 -In September 2019, successive legal and administrative processes began in each of the entities of the Financial Group, including that of the BOD in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which generated situations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and/or bank liquidation.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230728ej7s007a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
