\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Venezuela today: dollarization and a mock "perestroika".\b0\par\par\uc2 1,304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 It's boiling in the afternoon in La Candelaria, in downtown Caracas, where a handful of people are crowding to eat. They don't seem to mind the heat of the long black griddle of the cachapas stand (something like a corn flour pancake), nor the prices in dollars hanging on an advertisement on the dusty awning of the cart. It is the current portrait of the proud Bolivarian Revolution.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela has entered the 21st century, no doubt about it. Gone are the revolutionary political voluntarism (that which in his book on the 20th century Alain Badiou compared to taking a beast by the horns) and its Cuban mirages.\par \par \uc2 The marks of his absence can be perceived throughout Caracas: \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2 's eyes no longer spy the destiny of the country from above, nor does red stain bodies and minds as if it were a bloody uniform. And other emblematic projects of the Revolution, such as socialist areperas, Librer\u237\'00\'EDas del Sur and abastos Bicentenario, today yield their space to more discreet and profitable businesses, such as restaurants, luxury hardware stores and above all bodegones (that is to say, sales of imported delicatessen).\par \par \uc2 Venezuela went from being a Cuba with money to a ruined Russia. Where \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  closes, an imported supermarket opens.\par \par \uc2 The model of a gargantuan and irresponsible country that Chavismo built during a decade and a half now looks abandoned, half-built and half-destroyed, since a sad alternative is already emerging from its entrails, like the creature from Alien.\par \par \uc2 It is the model of contemporary autocracies, whose oligarchies flourish under the blind weight of international sanctions, maintained by a client State. As a fellow writer put it during my visit to Caracas: "Venezuela went from being a Cuba with money, to a ruined Russia".\par \par \uc2 The metaphor is not fortuitous. In a sort of perestroika, the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro has proposed to make the country's collapse profitable through a double doctrine of uncontrolled imports (that which was called pax bodeg\u243\'00\'F3nica, that is, to palliate the shortage with products mostly brought from Miami) and capture the spaces of the impoverished middle class, whose flight in recent years has ceded the commercial terrain to new and more ruthless actors.\par \par \uc2 But the forms change and the spirit remains: where a McDonald's closes, an imported supermarket opens. All within the framework of a gradual de-ideologization of life, from which even the vociferous harangues of the autocrat in Miraflores do not escape.\par \par \uc2 The elderly, the sick and almost 80% of the country's registered poor are facing the total collapse of the public system on their own.\par \par \uc2 The green waveThe general mood is one of learned helplessness, of turning the page, of better talking about something else. Faith in the role of politics has been lost. And although there are not a few who observe the present with paradoxical relief, facing the memory of the so-called "hunger years" between 2016 and 2018, it is difficult to know if things are really better.\par \par \uc2 Far from the slogan that swarms on social networks ("Venezuela got fixed"), the immediate context of the country evidences a brutal economic adjustment.\par \par \uc2 The elderly, the sick and almost 80% of the country's registered poor (ENCOVI 2022) are facing on their own the total collapse of the public system (hours without electricity, days without water, years without internet) and the micro-privatizations that are offered as the only solution: international health insurance, purchase of water tanks, private satellite services.\par \par \uc2 Even so, in his Labor Day speech, last May 1st, Maduro asks to "resist" with a minimum wage of 5 dollars a month (130 Bolivars) to an inter-annual inflation of around 500% (Venezuelan Observatory of Finance), and in exchange he promises bonuses calculated in dollars: 20 for the "war bonus" and 40 for the food ticket, laughable figures in a country whose basic food basket was around 480 dollars in February (Cendas-FVM). The crowd, indignant in front of the podium, opposes, booing him. Nothing happens. The silver thread that linked the impoverished masses to the Revolution has become thinner than ever.\par \par \uc2 In everyday life, meanwhile, the pragmatism of the dollar reigns, which circulates daily alongside the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of two different monetary cones, indistinguishable from each other to such an extent that people recognize the current bills by looking at the date of issue of each one.\par \par \uc2 The old one million bill is worth one; the five hundred thousand, zero fifty. The exchange control and its law of exchange crimes, which prohibited the purchase and sale of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are a thing of the distant past: the official dollar rate, which oscillates around 25 bolivars, is announced daily through the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is replicated on posters and blackboards next to the cash registers of supermarkets and bakeries.\par \par \uc2 All products are offered in dollars, even if a patriotic modesty leads to hide such name with euphemisms such as "reference", "ref." or whatever sign they deem appropriate. The Bolivar, in its third reincarnation and with fourteen zeros less than it had in 2000, serves only as a substitute for the cents of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bank cards offer a loan of a few bolivars, that is, a subway or bus ride. The same goes for traditional insurance, medical and funeral, which cover at most a few hundred dollars.\par \par \uc2 Vintage countryIn this context, consumption is governed by rather suspicious rules. On the same street you can see desolate businesses and the announcement of new ventures, despite the fact that credit is almost non-existent in Venezuela: bank cards offer a loan of a few bolivars, that is, a subway or bus ride. The same happens with traditional insurance, medical and funeral insurance, which cover at most a few hundred dollars.\par \par \uc2 The price dynamics, however, still wounded by past hyperinflationary spirals -the maximum peak was in 2018 with 130,060% year-on-year (\uc2 IMF\uc2 -WB)-, is shown to be more in line with the free market precepts of certain flamboyant "libertarians" swarming in social networks than with the old designs of 20th century developmentalism.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps that is why the nineteenth-century zeal of Chavismo, its patriotic ruralism and its determination to re-found the pro-independence procerato have become a kind of agonizing trademark. Its faded monigots on murals and avenues, its solemn busts in front of a recent baseball stadium, all of it already exudes the nostalgic whiff of vintage, of the obsolete and outdated.\par \par \uc2 In the shadows, however, the old trenches of our civil war by installments make peace: the defenders of the public and those of the private shake hands, while the militancy of both sides pretends to be distracted.\par \par \uc2 Shameless privatizations and discreet state \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are possible in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the 21st century, as long as the clarion call of the parties is silenced and the new owners of the country are not questioned.\par \par \uc2 The government itself will take care of that when it is convenient for it, because as the saying goes, firemen don't step on each other's hoses, and the new oligarchy, born in the heat of the revolutionary anvil, has no time to lose: a country hungry for consumption is lining up in front of its cash register and the money in its anxious hands is starting to dwindle again.\par \par \uc2 The authorGabriel Payares (40) has a BA in Literature, a Masters in Latin American Literature and Creative Writing.\par \par \uc2 Born in London where his parents did their PhDs, he grew up in Caracas, where he lived until he emigrated to Buenos Aires in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Winner of several literary awards, author of the books Cuando bajaron las aguas and Hotel, he published in Argentina Lo irreparable (Corregidor), an exquisite volume of short stories.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230525ej5p007n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
