\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Why did Sudeban authorize lending 30% of deposits in dollars?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Through Circular No. 00335, dated January 18, the National Superintendence of Banking Sector Institutions (Sudeban) officially authorized banks to grant credits in bolivars on 30% of the portfolio of freely convertible deposits, according to the balance existing in these instruments as of December 31, 2007.\par \par \uc2 The mechanism established by the regulatory authority determines that "the Banking Institutions may grant credits in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to their clients with the resources coming from the fund raising in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up to a maximum of resources equivalent to thirty percent (30%) of such fund raising, in such sense the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  corresponding to the referred percentage must be offered in the Exchange Market System (SMC) for subsequent settlement under the different credit modalities foreseen in the Law and the complementary regulations".\par \par \uc2 Now, what are the reasons behind this measure. The economist and head of the firm S\u237\'00\'EDntesis Financiera, Tamara Herrera, commented that it has several purposes, among them, to increase bank financing without reducing the legal reserve and to relieve the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ) of the burden of its cash \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .Furthermore, according to her, "it seeks to promote the private supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange bureaus, since the banks that decide to grant the credits must sell the dollars from those deposits".\par \par \uc2 However, Herrera indicated that the Superintendence of Banking Sector Institutions (Sudeban) has not yet announced or explained "how it will solve the mismatch that the measure may cause in the balance sheets of the banks, which up to now have been quite cautious. In fact, as Executive Vice-President Delcy Rodriguez claimed a couple of days ago, the little use was made of 10% of the deposits in dollars, precisely because the banks are aware that they must replenish those dollars to respond to their depositors", she added.\par \par \uc2 The economist pointed out that this measure is not the best way to increase the private supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We have to bear in mind the prudential rules. The banks have so far acted with this in mind".In her opinion, "it is very favorable to promote the private supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to attenuate eventual upward pressures on the exchange rate, but bear in mind that this supply does not take bolivars out of circulation. The only sale of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has the power to do so is carried out by the \uc2 BCV\uc2 . This must be kept in mind, given that we know that public spending will have to increase soon".\par \par \uc2 Venezuelan economy needs between 8 and 9 times more in credits\par \par \uc2 Economist Luis Oliveros described as "excellent news" that Sudeban approved the granting of credits in bolivars on 30% of deposits in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it was previously at 10%. But, he clarified that the Venezuelan economy needs between 8 and 9 times more in credits than it currently has.\par \par \uc2 "Of course it is excellent news that credit is increasing in Venezuela. It is an issue that is needed for the financing of growth. Private enterprise advocates, asks, begs and needs financing, but here the discussion is that there is still a long way to go to meet the needs of the economy. There is still a long way to go", said Oliveros.\par \par \uc2 The economist and university professor explained that "that 20% increase should be around 300 million dollars and that is not going to come in all at once. This does not mean that tomorrow the banks will automatically lend those 300 million dollars".\par \par \uc2 Credit portfolio growth in 2022 is insufficient\par \par \uc2 The country's credit portfolio closed 2022 at 13,211 million bolivars, equivalent to 755.77 million dollars, at the official exchange rate released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ). An amount higher by US$441.46 million with respect to the credit portfolio of January 2022, when it reached US$314.31 million.\par \par \uc2 "2022 closed with a solid and strengthened financial system, thanks to the successful strategies of the national Government, led by our President Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro and Vice President \uc2 Delcy Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez\uc2 , aimed at the total recovery and economic motorization", said the Deputy Minister of Digital Economy, Banking, Insurance and Securities, Rom\u225\'00\'E1n Maniglia, in his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 For Maniglia, this growth represents an "unequivocal sign" that the nation is "on the right track for momentum and growth in 2023".\par \par \uc2 Despite the "successful strategies" by the Executive, for economists this amount is still insufficient to meet the demand for credit and financing needed by the oil nation to raise national production.\par \par \uc2 "If you compare with what you had at the beginning of the year, which was less than half, it is undoubtedly a significant amount. However, Venezuela needs up to 15 times that amount. So that amount is still very, very low," said Luis Oliveros, and recalled that the country had as much as $14 billion in the credit portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Currently, "Venezuela's credit portfolio is the lowest in the entire region", according to the economist, detailing that approximately two thirds of the total loans granted by the banks are commercial, while consumer credit is barely 3%.\par \par \uc2 Along the same lines, Tamara Herrera explained that when the State reports that the credit portfolio practically doubled in 2022, "of course it sounds like an extraordinary activity, that the percentage element is precisely extraordinary because of the very low level at which it is compared".\par \par \uc2 However, "being "dazzled" by that figure, for Herrera is not the right thing to do, due to the fact that it continues to be "insignificant" for the national economy.\par \par \uc2 "Even when the balance quoted is equivalent to 756 million dollars, that is little less than 1% or 2% of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP). The economies that are duly financed by their banking systems at their efficiency, the least one can aspire to is that this coefficient is in the order of 15%, while what is normal in countries where there is an important dynamic and where this streamlining is facilitated, is that you can be on the scale of 20% of the Gross Domestic Product", the economist pointed out.\par \par \uc2 The head of S\u237\'00\'EDntesis Financiera highlighted that the "biggest headache" of the country's businessmen is the lack of access to financing from the banks and the State, besides waiting for the authorization of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  financing in the local banking market.\par \par \uc2 "That figure is so insufficient. That percentage increase is so misleading, that even when you see at the end of the year that the growth of the amount of money in circulation in the economy was less than that of the monetary base, which is the result of multiplying the monetary base by the credit multiplier. The credit multiplier fell and that is the only thing that explains why the monetary mass has decelerated and has grown less than the monetary base itself that leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2 ", explained Herrera.\par \par \uc2 He added that this represents a weakening of the function of bank credit in the Venezuelan economy and "where there is no credit, there is no production".\par \par \uc2 Herrera emphasized that the economic team of the \uc2 BCV\uc2  "is very afraid of reducing the legal reserve to allow a greater increase in the acceleration of credit for fear that it will lead to a demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and put upward pressure on the exchange rate".\par \par \uc2 This is the reason why the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro has been restricting the credit portfolio of the national banks for four years, as explained by Herrera.\par \par \uc2 In his opinion, it is extremely important for the State to promote an "active and much more agile program for the reduction of the legal reserve, so that the banks can make and create more spaces for the facilitation of production".\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, the economic activity, "whether it is an empanada stand, a bakery, a store or a small, medium or large industrial company, if it does not have financing, everything it has to do to buy inventories, supplies, repairs, expansion of production lines is delayed because they must wait to sell what they have and this is not only subject to the rate of sales, but also to the rate of collection".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230120ej1k0073n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
