\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Maduro again asked the U.S. to lift sanctions against Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In the midst of a growing social unrest, which since last Monday has included protests in the streets of Caracas and the main cities of the country due to the incessant increase in the cost of living, President Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro described as "dramatic" the figures of the Venezuelan economy and once again asked the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to lift the sanctions against the country.\par \par \uc2 The President made special reference to public revenues, which have fallen sharply since Washington and its European allies decreed the embargo on the country's oil exports. "National income fell to dramatic numbers, but it was not that it fell three years ago, today it is in dramatic numbers", insisted Maduro during his annual message to the National Assembly, the Venezuelan parliament.\par \par \uc2 According to what he stated before the legislators, Venezuela only received 1% of the money that should have entered the country without sanctions. "At this hour that I am speaking to you here, only 1% of what should or should have entered the country entered the country," said the president, a situation he attributed to what he described as a "war" of the governments of the United States and its allies who, as he interpreted it, "sought to establish a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the country.\par \par \uc2 Maduro said that since 2015, \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped receiving tax revenues for the equivalent of 3,995 million barrels of oil, which he valued at about US$ 232 billion, occasion on which he added that "imperialism and its allies stripped" the country of US$ 400 million dollars a day in that period. "The real war has been waged against the oil industry to try to generate a total collapse and the conditions for a coup d'\u233\'00\'E9tat and a change of regime", he affirmed.\par \par \uc2 The head of the Miraflores palace detailed that of the sanctions applied to the country, 42% were directed to government agencies; 18.7% to the oil industry; 17% to the economy and finance sector; and 7% to the private sector. Addressing the \uc2 US government\uc2 , Maduro exclaimed: "We do not want more sanctions, lift the criminal sanctions on the Venezuelan people, enough of criminal sanctions".\par \par \uc2 Maduro gave his account rendering to the parliament in the midst of the massive street protests that started last Monday in Caracas and extended to other cities of the country when workers of several unions, as well as retirees, came out to demand salary increases in the context of an economy that closed 2022 with a retail inflation that climbed to 305.7% measured in bolivars, the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and 53.6% in dollars; according to the Venezuelan Observatory of Finance (OVF) and the firm Ecoanal\u237\'00\'EDtica, respectively.\par \par \uc2 According to the Center for Documentation and Social Analysis of the Venezuelan Federation of Teachers (Cendas-FVM), the total basic food basket, which includes food as well as non-food goods and services, stood at a floor of US$ 474.87 last November for a family composed of two adults and two minors, in the face of a minimum salary of 130 bolivars, equivalent at the beginning of this week to about US$ 7.4, according to the exchange rate of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Venezuela has had a growth in 2022 above 15% of the Gross Domestic Product, the highest growth in Latin America and the Caribbean", said Maduro, a dynamic that he explained by the "impact of the diversification of the economy", while he asked public and private banks to grant 30% of their portfolio in dollars to "expand foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  financing" to "sectors that produce goods, wealth and jobs".\par \par \uc2 According to private analysts and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  (\uc2 WB\uc2 ), in coincidence with the studies of the \uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean (ECLAC)\uc2 , the country's economic activity began to fall in 2014, amid the collapse of foreign sales of hydrocarbons and a pressing domestic crisis that worsened after the imposition of a battery of international sanctions in 2019 as part of the crusade led by Washington to seek the fall of the president, after disavowing his reelection in 2018.\par \par \uc2 With an informal dollarization and the relaxation of exchange controls, the economy had a respite and, after years of recession and hyperinflation that pulverized the purchasing power of Venezuelans, recorded five consecutive quarters of growth between 2021 and 2022. "We are going step by step, it is the real economy that is going and must go better and better," Maduro said, despite the fact that many economists have warned that the recovery is likely to reach a ceiling in the absence of deeper measures.\par \par \uc2 Maduro, who did not offer data on poverty and indigence, affirmed that the open unemployment rate dropped from 8.9% to 7.8% between 2021 and 2022. In addition, he acknowledged "some disturbances" registered at the end of 2021 which, he said, "destabilized" domestic prices, although he did not detail figures. The latest official data on inflation published by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is from October, with a year-on-year rate of 155.8%, half of the approximately 300% measured by private consulting firms, which warn about the possibility of the country returning to the hyperinflationary cycle it left behind in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230113ej1d008ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
