\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2 's dispute between federalism and centralism and the challenges for 2023\b0\par\par\uc2 1,114 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 (By Daniel Scar\u237\'00\'EDmbolo) The year of President \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2 's administration concludes with the ruling of the Supreme Court that issued an injunction for the national Government to allocate 2.95% of the volume of co-participable taxes to the city of Buenos Aires, which reopened the unfinished and constitutive discussion in Argentine history between federalism and Buenos Aires centralism. This ruling is not only detrimental to the fulfillment of the Budget 2023, approved last November in the Congress, but also to the President himself, due to the criminal charges and the request for impeachment by some sectors of the opposition, after the President decided to recuse the members of the Court and to file a request for the revocation "in extremis" of the precautionary resolution issued.\par \par \uc2  Beyond this episode, Fernandez worked this year on the post-pandemic agenda, which included changes in the cabinet, with the incorporation of Sergio Massa in the Ministry of Economy; the reconversion of social plans for genuine employment; and the challenges for the last year of government, based especially on the fight against inflation and the recovery of salaries. Massa, who took office last August 3, raised in November the idea of lowering inflation to around 3% by April, at a rate of a decrease of half a point per month. Last month, Fernandez held a bilateral meeting with her Chinese counterpart, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , within the framework of the G20 Summit in Bali, where she obtained the authorization of the "Asian giant" for Argentina to dispose of US$ 5 billion as part of the Swap mechanism for any kind of commercial use, which will go to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , essential to reorder the economy in this fight against inflation. During this tour, the President also obtained the endorsement of the Managing Director of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ), \uc2 Kristalina Georgieva\uc2 , who told him that it is very important for Argentina to maintain the economic course "as it has successfully done" during the last months, and the need to continue working on lowering inflation. Directly related to inflation is the wage recovery. In fact, according to the latest available data from the National Institute of Statistics and Censuses (Indec), the wage index registered an increase of 6.7% in September, five tenths above the 6.2% inflation of that month. And on the 22nd of last month, the Minimum, Vital and Mobile Wage Council, made up of the General Labor Confederation (CGT), the Argentine Workers' Central (CTA) and the business chambers, determined a 20% increase in four tranches to reach a year-on-year increase of 110% in March. This will have an impact on pensions, the Universal Child Allowance (AUH) and all those variables that, by different laws, are tied to the minimum wage. In addition to Massa, on October 10, Victoria Tolosa Paz joined the cabinet as Minister of Social Development; Ayel\u233\'00\'E9n Mazzina as Minister of Women, Gender and Diversity; and Raquel "Kelly" Kismer de Olmos as Minister of Labor. Tolosa Paz replaced Juan Zabaleta, who returned to the mayor's office of Hurlingham; Mazzina replaced Elizabeth G\u243\'00\'F3mez Alcorta, who resigned after questioning the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of federal forces carried out by court order for the eviction of the Mapuche community Lafken Winkul Mapu from the land they occupied in Villa Mascardi, where seven women were arrested; and Olmos succeeded Claudio Moroni, who left the position due to personal issues. On November 1, Santiago Maggiotti took over the Territorial Development and Habitat, replacing Jorge Ferraresi, who resigned and returned to the Avellaneda City Hall. And on the 30th of the same month, Diego Giuliano took over in Transportation to replace Alexis Guerrera, who resigned due to health problems. As regards social plans, last November 3, the President signed a decree to advance in the process of linking such assistance with genuine employment, and as from the entry into force of this measure no new registrations were granted in the programs Potenciar Trabajo, Potenciar Inclusi\u243\'00\'F3n Joven, Nexo and Plus Esencial. The decree "puts a ceiling on the number of Potenciar Trabajo beneficiaries", which is 1,350,000, as indicated by Tolosa Paz in the announcement, and anticipated that there will be about 10,000 monthly withdrawals, whose amounts will go to "the acquisition of machines and tools for the production of goods and services" for the recipients of the plan, or also for the "educational offer and job training". Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez was also this year president pro tempore of the Community of Latin American and Caribbean States (\uc2 Celac\uc2 ), which on January 24 will hold its Leaders' Summit in Buenos Aires and will meet at the San Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Palace of the Foreign Ministry. There, the President will deliver the balance of his year in office and will finish his term of office, and "he is not working on his re-election", government sources told T\u233\'00\'E9lam. "In any case, it will have to be decided how and who will lead \uc2 Celac\uc2  during 2023", they added. As president pro tempore of that organization, he had an interesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the so-called "Meeting on \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 " with his peers from France, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 ; and Colombia, \uc2 Gustavo Petro\uc2 ; in the framework of the 5th Paris Peace Forum, held on November 11 and 12 in the French capital. There, the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela agreed to return to the political dialogue between the government and the opposition, after the support of Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez, Macron and Petro, in order to reach a solution in that country, "without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 " but "bringing Venezuelans closer to finding a way out", according to the Argentine president himself at that time. Negotiations of this type in Venezuela will continue in 2023. As a counterpart to her possible departure from \uc2 Celac\uc2 , Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez assumed in Montevideo, last December 6, the Pro Tempore Presidency of Mercosur, until June 2023, with the challenge of promoting the development of fair and secure supply chains for the productive and economic complementarity of the countries of the bloc, according to sources from the Foreign Ministry. From this position, Argentina will focus on the importance of incorporating science and technology and adding value to exportable products. In addition, it will propose an internal and an external agenda: the first is based on issues such as the customs union, a proactive green program, border integration, migration issues, health and education, among others. The second is based on finding solutions to pending issues with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and negotiations already underway in Canada and Indonesia, in addition to deepening integration in Central America and the Caribbean, and then moving on to Africa. (T\u233\'00\'E9lam)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221226eicq0075s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
