\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Inflation in Venezuela Reached 21.9 % in November, Says Independent Entity\b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Inflation in Venezuela in November was 21.9%, 7.4 percentage points higher than in October, according to data released Monday by the Venezuelan Finance Observatory (OVF), an independent entity outside the country\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The accumulated inflation for the first 11 months of the year rose to 195.7%.\par \par \uc2 The OVF stated that this acceleration of inflation occurred in a context of a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant\u8221\'20\'1D depreciation of the bolivar, estimated at 43%, and a \u8220\'20\'1Closs of reserves of 1,154 million dollars\u8221\'20\'1D between November 2021 and 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis suggests that the \uc2 BCV\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market to stabilize the exchange rate has lost effectiveness and fails to contain the rise in prices, while at the same time generating a drain on the \uc2 BCV\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reserves,\u8221\'20\'1D he added on Venezuela\u8217\'20\'19s inflation.\par \par \uc2 The sectors with the highest increases in November were leisure at 30.4 %; goods and services at 27.9 %; education at 29.4 %; and clothing and footwear at 27.5 %.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, the OVF indicated that the cost of the food basket, calculated for a family of five, was 366 dollars, while the \u8220\'20\'1Cminimum salary remains at 130 bolivares (11.62 dollars)\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe existing salary gap has been impacted by the increase in the exchange rate. Thus, while the average payment in the Metropolitan Area of Caracas stands at 120 dollars per month for the private sector, in the public sector it reaches only 11 dollars per month,\u8221\'20\'1D added the entity, made up of former congress members and economic experts.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela emerged last December from hyperinflation which started in 2017 and went on for four years to reduce the value of the bolivar, the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as citizens\u8217\'20\'19 trust in it, so they unofficially adopted the dollar in an attempt to protect their income.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221205eic500bjv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
