\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The Venezuelan economy in 2023 "hangs on a license".\b0\par\par\uc2 1,330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The decision of the \uc2 US Treasury Department\uc2  to relax \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's license will decide the fate of the Venezuelan economy for next year, and not only because of the impact on oil production but also on inflation, devaluation and consumption. Projections by S\u237\'00\'EDntesis Financiera and Ecoanal\u237\'00\'EDtica coincidentally point to a growth for 2023 of 4% without the license and a maximum of 12% with the approval of OFAC announced on Saturday 26th.\par \par \uc2 Analysts pointed out that in order to estimate the behavior of the Venezuelan economy in 2023, it was necessary to wait for a decision from the \uc2 Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC)\uc2 , a body that depends on the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 , involving a relaxation of the oil sanctions against \uc2 PDVSA\uc2  or at least a new license to \uc2 Chevron\uc2  with extensions to operate.\par \par \uc2 And no wonder. Oil production has been the main engine of the country's economic activity, so after the debacle of \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos de Venezuela\uc2  due to years of disinvestment and diversion of resources, the country was plunged into an eight-year recession, which deepened with the U.S. sanctions against the industry.\par \par \uc2 This is how the government of the U.S. company \uc2 Chevron Corporation\uc2  to expand its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Venezuela, as early as Saturday, November 26, when a new round of dialogue between the administration of \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2  and the Venezuelan opposition began. This Thursday, December 1, \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's General License 8 expires, so expectations on the subject were increasing.\par \par \uc2 This measure of the Treasury Department will decide the fate of the Venezuelan economy by 2023, not only in terms of oil production, but also in terms of inflation, devaluation and consumption.\par \par \uc2 The director of the consulting firm, pointed out that the expansion of \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country would obviously contribute to increase production, but she does not expect it to be a significant figure. Nevertheless, it could lead the Venezuelan economy to an expansion of 8.7% for next year. He maintained that if the license to the North American oil company was not approved, the growth would be 3.5%. These figures, however, are still modest if one takes into account that the country lost 80% of its size in a period of eight years.\par \par \uc2 "With the license extension, the increase in oil production will be slow and moderate. But we believe that the barrels of Venezuelan crude exported by \uc2 Chevron\uc2  will be made without discount," he said in a speech given at a forum of the Venezuelan Real Estate Chamber.\par \par \uc2 The report highlights a 14% result for the local oil sector after the OFAC measure, an average production of 785,000 barrels per day (b/d) and an oil export of 586,000 b/d, for a total amount of income of US$ 11.4 billion at a price of US$ 53 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 S\u237\'00\'EDntesis Financiera's projections are in line with estimates that pointed to 2023 growth of 4% without a license and a maximum of 12% with OFAC approval. "We believe that in the event that the United States grants a license to oil companies that allows them to expand their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country, Venezuela can grow between 10% to 12%. A slowdown will occur if no measures are taken and no agreements are reached regarding the country's oil situation", said recently Asdr\u250\'00\'FAbal Oliveros, director of the local consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 Tamara Herrera recalled that \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's resources obtained from its crude oil exports will very possibly amount to $3.2 billion at a rate of approximately $78 dollars per barrel.\par \par \uc2 If the license had not been approved with new guidelines, the figures would change drastically. In this sense, production would continue the decline it has had in recent years, to 677,000 b/d on average for all of 2023, as well as an export of 516,000 b/d and an income of $9 billion to a barrel heavily punished by the discount at $48.\par \par \uc2 "By extending the license to \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , greater transparency and better prices in the volume exported by this company will be observed, but also possible positive externalities in the Venezuelan oil industry and, consequently, a recovery of expectations and a sequel of licenses to other companies", stated Herrera.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he maintained that if the license had not been granted, "the country's economic activity would slow down or would depend only on the anomalous impulses of the so-called black economy".\par \par \uc2 In his opinion, the national oil industry "has not been able to take advantage" of the current world energy crisis, which has generated high fuel prices.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan economy stopped falling from the end of 2021 and in 2022 it has maintained positive rates, however, for Herrera this year has been a period in which "the set of positive factors that generated the recovery was exhausted and metabolized". Among these factors he points out, the end of the pandemic, the pragmatic easing of the economic policy in practical but not legal terms.\par \par \uc2 "The positive effect of the dollarization process, for example, was halted because the government itself at the beginning of the second quarter introduced the reform of the Large Financial Transaction Tax, which," he said.\par \par \uc2 No license for hyperinflation The global economic environment will also have an effect on Venezuela in 2023. The director of S\u237\'00\'EDntesis Financiera pointed out that the global recession will have an impact on remittances, which have been a determining factor for Venezuelans' income. For next year, this factor of remittances together with the persistent inflation in the local economy, will generate a lower consumption among Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 "The rhythm in the volume of remittances to Venezuela has been slowing down due to several aspects: the terrible conditions in which many migrants are currently, many families have reunited outside the country and therefore the needs of remittances are different and the recession is affecting everyone".\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he emphasized that another important element that is changing is the speed with which the speed of salary increases in the private sector is losing strength, and therefore "consumption, which is the engine that allows to dynamize the economy, is also slowing down".\par \par \uc2 He maintained that a lower income in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the country and a political environment that demands more public spending, will accentuate the monetary financing of the deficit, which is largely responsible for the hyperinflation process experienced by the Venezuelan population. He considers that oil revenues continue to decline and therefore the key tool that has been the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been weakened due to the decline in revenues.\par \par \uc2 "In 2023, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the BCV (\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will be the mainstay of the exchange rate policy, but there are concerns about its sustainability. If the pressure on the exchange rate continues, we would enter a scenario close to hyperinflation", said Herrera.\par \par \uc2 He explained that the anti-inflationary policy of \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2 's administration has exhausted its effectiveness, due to the fact that decelerating public expenditure "has become unfeasible", while tax collection in real terms is growing, but is insufficient. On the other hand, he said that the \uc2 BCV\uc2 's open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hedging Securities) have not yielded positive results and "indexation creates a dilemma for the issuing entity itself".\par \par \uc2 The positive aspect of the license for the exchange market is that by expanding its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  will have to sell part of its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obtained from exports to meet its commitments in bolivars, which will alleviate the pressure on the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, it must be remembered that the process of recovery of the oil fields in which the company manages together with \uc2 Pdvsa\uc2  will be slow, it will also have to deduct from the income from exports what is owed by its partner and the Treasury will not receive royalties or taxes corresponding to the oil activity.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221130eibu007p4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
