\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 How much will the price of the dollar reach by the end of 2022? This is the opinion of economists\b0\par\par\uc2 1,615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 So far in 2022, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ) has injected around 150 million dollars to banks, with the aim of containing the rise of the official dollar, but how feasible and lasting can these exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  be in view of the fall of international reserves and the decrease in oil production.\par \par \uc2 Economists consulted by HispanoPost agree that the lack of official data from the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro makes it increasingly difficult to make estimates. However, they calculate that the dollar could close by the end of the year, between 10 and 15 bolivars.\par \par \uc2 "How much the dollar will close at in 2022, nobody knows, not even the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself knows. I have seen many estimates that speak of between 10 and 12 bolivars, but this is speculation. What is clear to me is that it will go up in the remaining months, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot sustain the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indefinitely, because it has lost a lot of reserves", said economist Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Guerra, member of the Venezuelan Finance Observatory.\par \par \uc2 Even, for economist Manuel Sutherland, it is probable that the dollar rate could double. "It could reach 15 or 16 bolivars, depending on the difficulties that the government has to finish paying bonds and important labor commitments, as well as suppliers for the end of the year".\par \par \uc2 The distortions in the exchange market make economist and director of Ecoanal\u237\'00\'EDtica, Alejandro Grisanti, question whether the \uc2 BCV\uc2  will be able to maintain the exchange rate until December: "It seems that it will devaluate, that it should close in a range of 10 and 13 bolivars per dollar for this year and it will depend on to what extent they will really unleash in the economy".\par \par \uc2 "That is to say, the lower it closes, which is closer to 10 bolivars, we are talking that the exchange rate is going to have greater expressions and greater probabilities of devaluation in the first quarter of next year and to the extent that it closes closer to 13 bolivars, it would go very much in line with what we are expecting in inflation", added Grisanti.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, university professor, economist and member of the National Academy of Economic Sciences, Leonardo Vera, recalled that in Venezuela there is no statistical basis of how the real economic activity has evolved, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  stopped publishing this data since the first quarter of 2019.\par \par \uc2 "It is difficult to make projections, due to the fact that volatility is much higher. Today we are seeing a quotation between 8 and 9 bolivars per dollar in the informal markets and it would not have any time that at the end of the year this could be in 10 and 12 dollars, if there is no special event", commented Vera.\par \par \uc2 Christmas bonus payments: will the dollar have an impact? The devaluation of the bolivar has caused Venezuelans to lose confidence in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and seek refuge in the dollar. Last August the exchange rate, both official and parallel, soared partly due to the amount of bolivars in circulation, following the payment of the holiday bonus due by law to the country's teachers.\par \par \uc2 This led us to ask economic experts whether the payment of Christmas bonuses to be paid by the Maduro government to public employees could generate a new impact on the price of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Leonardo Vera explained that the measure taken by the State to pay Christmas bonuses to public administration workers in four parts "will perhaps help it to lighten the pressure on the exchange market, because let's say that it gradually reduces the pressure that the excess of liquidity may generate in the exchange market".\par \par \uc2 However, for Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Guerra, the payment of the Christmas bonuses will have an effect regardless of whether they are paid in installments or in full. "They are doing this at the cost of an impressive loss of international reserves, between December last year and October 14, 2022, they have lost more than 920 million dollars, which is the highest figure in the last three years".\par \par \uc2 Guerra added that "on the one hand, it increases the demand for products and, on the other hand, it also puts pressure on the exchange rate, because when the pensioner spends, commerce wants to replenish inventories and, obviously, this translates into an expression of the demand for goods and dollars. I do not think it will be much, because the Christmas bonuses are not a significant amount and, in addition, they are being paid in a fractioned manner".\par \par \uc2 Alejandro Grisanti also estimates that this pressure of bolivars that will be seen in the payment of Christmas bonuses in the Venezuelan economy could have an impact on the exchange rate of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "In order to maintain this exchange rate stability, which is not desired, but it seems that the government needs it, it is going to have to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some 600 million dollars in November alone and an additional 400 million dollars in December. But, once again, the question here is whether this is the most efficient use of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or whether it should be used and spent in other areas that may be more important than burning dollars to maintain an artificially low exchange rate", the economist reflected.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland emphasized that "it is evident that there is a very large fiscal gap. In addition, the State does not have enough income and what it does is to issue money without backing, artificial money, which when it enters the national stream, causes inflation because it is a monetary injection without real value, but purely artificial".\par \par \uc2 The economist emphasized that "the economy is extremely small and the amount of bolivars circulating in the economy is extremely low. The entire monetary mass could be equivalent, more or less, to some 1,500 million dollars or a little less than that".\par \par \uc2 For Sutherland "the problem is that this exchange rate, artificially low, creates very big distortions in prices and makes the prices in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  go up, reduces the competitiveness of the industry and national production, prevents the export of anything other than oil, makes oil exports yield much less than they should in Venezuela and only benefits a caste of importers who live off this differential".\par \par \uc2 Are international reserves at risk? Economists warn that the financial policy managed by the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro to maintain the price of the dollar is not enough to alleviate the financial crisis that the country is going through and puts the international reserves at risk.\par \par \uc2 Guerra assured that "the government has a very limited capacity because the liquid reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are very low, of 4.8 billion dollars, because it is worth remembering that they report that they have 9.9 billion dollars, but we have to subtract 5.1 billion dollars which are special drawing rights assigned by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , which are not reserves because they are not liquid. They are not available and a good part of the liquidity is gold".\par \par \uc2 Grisanti described that what has happened in the last three years, where inflation has been much higher than the devaluation, "what you end up having is an increase in the cost of living, an increase in the price in dollars and a lower purchasing power".\par \par \uc2 Vera ratified that as long as oil revenues and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues maintained by the Venezuelan State do not increase significantly and the liquid reserves held by the \uc2 BCV\uc2  remain at such low levels, the economic instability in the country will continue.\par \par \uc2 "This scenario could only change substantially, if and only if, for example, Venezuela obtains the 5,000 million that are trapped in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , by way of special drawing rights and that should, under normal conditions, have been distributed to Venezuela, just as they were distributed to the different member countries of the fund many months ago. This would help to feed the international reserves and with this the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become very creative when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market", said Vera.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Sutherland emphasized that "we are at the historical minimum, without a doubt, in terms of total reserves and import capacity", for which he qualified as an "error that the \uc2 BCV\uc2  adds the special drawing rights, which are in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , as if they were its own international reserves".\par \par \uc2 Economic growth projected for Venezuela Despite the financial circumstances the country is going through, economists project that Venezuela's annual economic growth could be around 7%, as estimated by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and the \uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean (ECLAC)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There are some indications that the growth rate of the first semester could be a little higher than the second semester, because the oil activity has not advanced much more in the second semester of this year and it seems that the advances registered at the beginning of 2022 compared to the beginning of 2021 augured a more or less good recovery of the oil sector and already towards the second semester of this year that is not what is being seen", said Vera.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, Sutherland pointed out that "most of the estimates say that it is quite probable that the Venezuelan economy will grow between 10 and 12 percent. I believe it could be a little less, due to the inflationary rebound, the drop in oil production, among other factors. The economy could close 2022 with a growth between 7 and 9 percent".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221019eiaj006w6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
