\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Plataforma Unitaria blamed Maduro for the devaluation: "it continues to impoverish Venezuelans".\b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Unitary Platform blamed this Wednesday the regime of \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2  for the devaluation of the bolivar registered in the last few days, which "continues to impoverish the Venezuelan family".\par \par \uc2 Economist Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Guerra, member of the Governance Agreement Commission of the Unitary Platform, assured that the devaluation of the bolivar is due to the lack of confidence Venezuelans have in this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Guerra specified that, in view of the accelerated inflation, citizens acquire goods or foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their income. "When a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is devaluating, it is because the public wants to get out of it", he stated.\par \par \uc2 "The policy followed by Maduro's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange bank has resulted in a loss of more than 750 million dollars between January of this year and August 19. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no way to withstand the demand for dollars because it simply has no supply of dollars", he explained.\par \par \uc2 He added that this is a unique case in the world of an oil country that is not accumulating international reserves in the form of dollars but rather is losing them.\par \par \uc2 Guerra pointed out that the problem does not reside in the fact that the payment of bonuses and salaries to workers is pressuring the exchange market, but that the devaluation is due to the "loss of confidence in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; first due to the hyperinflation experienced up to January of this year and then due to the inflation which is still high".\par \par \uc2 Guerra pointed out that the solution to the Venezuelan crisis must be a political change and an economic change, stressing that new policies must be generated to generate confidence in investors, which in turn will reestablish the purchasing power of Venezuelan workers and that employment will flourish.\par \par \uc2 Finally, he suggested that "together with this, a program must be designed to increase oil production. Currently 650 thousand barrels per day are being produced; Venezuela can produce in the medium term close to one million five hundred thousand barrels of oil, increase incentives for agriculture and cattle raising, and above all that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not continue to issue inorganic money".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220824ei8o00bid\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
