\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The government does not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in sales to  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2  and there are no risks: mincomercio\b0\par\par\uc2 1,435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Mauricio Galindo - Economics Editor @galmauGerm\u225\'00\'E1n Uma\u241\'00\'F1a, one of the ministers of the new government, is beginning to move forward with an agenda as rich as the name of his portfolio: Trade, Industry and Tourism. In this conversation, the new minister explains several aspects of these tasks, among which stand out the idea of reviewing trade agreements and recovering the voluminous trade that took place in the past with Venezuela. Uma\u241\'00\'F1a explains the points in which there may be unequal conditions in trade agreements and the possible ways to try to correct those situations in which it is not possible to compete on equal terms with the producers of Colombia's partner countries. He also refers to tourism and how to maintain the momentum of these activities, after the tax reform project contemplates removing the existing tax incentives. In your campaign you proposed to seek changes in the \uc2 FTA\uc2  with the U.S. What is the way forward?\par \par \uc2  We are evaluating the results of internationalization in Colombia. We have to look at it over the last 30 years. Since the opening in the 90's; then came the need for certain countries to propose bilateral negotiations. This negotiation methodology began as a \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  Plus, in which there are at least three major agreements: the first one Colombia signed was what was called the G3, which later became the G2, with Mexico. Then, with the United States, and later, the association agreement with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Where would this evaluation lead to? The result is asymmetrical because with subsidies, domestic support and other measures of equivalent effect that Mexico, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the United States kept alive and apply, there is an imbalance. If one has subsidies and the other does not, there is no price competitiveness. We import millions of tons of food, which is neither bad nor good, but what they cannot tell us is that we are not competitive because it turns out that it is not a problem of competitiveness, but of subsidies. Once these imbalances have been gauged, what is the way forward? We have to analyze what we are going to do, not tariffs, that is not true. That will go to inflation. We are seriously analyzing how to recover a minimum of food sovereignty; how to apply the rules of international trade and its limits in order to put the agricultural sector under equivalent conditions. To strike a balance between imports and domestic production needs under competitive conditions. And that has two components, some internal measures, in consultancy, technical assistance, seeds... and a talk on how we can balance the injustice, with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , with the United States and even with Mexico, although it is not so dramatic. Apart from agriculture, where else are there imbalances? In services, because only those that are the product of the fourth industrial revolution, of transportation, are liberalized. And why is there no liberalization of professional and business services? They should be liberalized because that is our offer. Why have the fundamental rights to education and health remained in these agreements as goods and not as fundamental rights? Everything that has to do with arbitration tribunals, such as the International Center for Dispute Settlement. It cannot be that we are about to be condemned because we denied the environmental license. They first gave the mining license to exploit Santurb\u225\'00\'E1n. In these agreements it is possible to put the exploitation of coal above environmental rights. They are not going to call you a protectionist. They are not going to tell you that you are against the free market. On the contrary, what I am saying is that there are market failures derived from these agreements. How does the assessment you are making affect new agreements? They were negotiating with the United Arab Emirates and, whatever it was, they were going to close it. But despite the fact that Colombia, but especially President Duque, whom I esteem and respect very much, wanted to sign that agreement, the Emirates did not want to sign it. It was an imperfect agreement, with problems especially in the origin. We will have to see how the agreement is, how far they have advanced, and see if it is worthwhile for them and for us to continue with that negotiation. As President Duque said at the time -although he denied it with the Arab Emirates, I don't know why-, no more free trade agreements will be signed until that evaluation is made. What is coming for tourism? We are playing with perfecting and fulfilling the peace agreements. A dream that can become a reality in spite of what has happened in the last four years. And those peace agreements are fundamental in the most forgotten but most promising areas of the country. In the \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , in Guaviare, in Vichada, everything is tied to the possibility that these peace agreements are supported by investment, by crop substitution, by the development of infrastructure, and to be able to combat drug trafficking. One kilometer that we recover from the roads or 100 square kilometers of crop substitution are a thousand square kilometers of territories for sustainable tourism for the indigenous communities, for the black communities, for the peasants. To convert tourism, little by little, in the substitution of many of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generated by the extractive sector. How does the end of tax incentives for certain tourist activities influence? Look, possibly there will be things to improve, but it is not the hotel business that is important. What is important is that the progressiveness of the tax reform will contribute to eliminate exemptions in many sectors that are not allowing inequality to decrease. The tax reform is to be able to respond to social, infrastructure and security needs in conflict zones. All the resources of the ministry, which are not large resources, but are important because of their multiplying effects, are going to be dedicated in the area of tourism to large projects in the peace zones and in the areas with more difficulties. The ministry is going to promote, develop and make clear policies, but not to build big hotels or to give exemptions, but for the development of sustainable tourism. What are your expectations regarding trade with Venezuela, which reached 8,000 million dollars? Of course, we had a trade of US$ 8 billion. In 2020, legal trade dropped to 220 million. Legal trade this year (if the border is not opened, which it will be) will be $650 million. Once the border is opened, we will replace illegal trade with legal trade. Without any problem we can say that this year with Venezuela we will have minimum 800 million dollars in bilateral trade, that is still 10 percent of the best time, but do not forget that the Venezuelan gross domestic product fell between 2015 and 2020 by about 75 percent. In 2021 that gross domestic product recovered, and this year they are going to have growth of 20 percent. To the extent that the demand in Venezuela recovers and we also demand Venezuelan products to balance the trade balance, trade is going to grow and we think, from the analysis we have done, that we are talking for the year 2023 about 2 billion dollars and at the end of the government of our President Petro we are going to reach levels of 4.5 billion dollars, 60 percent of what was the best time, but we really have to be clear that this depends on the recovery and balance of the economy. And at the moment, how are the payment mechanisms for exporters? In the payment mechanisms there is no influence of the government, of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because there are sanctions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What there is is a supply and demand in dollars through the exchange bureaus and there the daily exchange rate is fixed. Seventy-five percent of those dollars go to the domestic market, because the Venezuelan economy has a high degree of dollarization, but 25 percent is for export payments. From the point of view of exchange control it no longer exists, and there are mechanisms for payment, especially those of the banks, in the money desks that they send to their sister banks. It is usually sold in advance, and there is no risk. Of course, entities such as the Colombian-Venezuelan Chamber do due diligence. Now, what is bought and what is sold is fluid.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220814ei8e005c9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
