\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The high cost of reducing inflation in Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 1,815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In the last few weeks, a large part of the opposition and economists with a neoliberal bias in the country went from using Venezuela as the worst possible scenario to which Argentina would be heading to, to taking it as a model of "war won" against inflation. The Venezuelan economy, after four years of hyperinflation and two monetary reconversions, managed to get below the "hyper" threshold and even showed lower price increases than those shown in Argentina today. But it did so at a very high cost.\par \par \uc2 The praise received by Venezuela from the economic establishment is not only for the results but mainly for the extremely contractionary policy applied to achieve them. These adjustment measures are intended for Argentina, even though this has generated in Venezuela the impoverishment of the purchasing power of household income, a collapse of the Gross Domestic Product and has deepened the emigration of around six million inhabitants.\par \par \uc2 "The government recognized in facts that its policy of controlling the economy had failed and now they have gone to the other extreme: extreme neoliberalism. There is no price control, no taxes are paid on exports, there is a massive import bubble even with products that are produced in the country and the dollar went from a situation in which it was illegal to one in which it is the only reference. At the same time, the government is receiving more dollars: a small recovery of oil, remittances from the millions of Venezuelans who left the country and the export of gold. Between this and the sale of reserves, the relationship between the bolivar and the dollar remains stable, it has even dropped a little", explained to El Destape the sociologist Edgardo Lander, professor emeritus of Social Sciences at the Central University of Venezuela in Caracas, one of the Venezuelan intellectuals chosen by the late President Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez to negotiate the No to the FTAA and a referent of a citizen platform that for years has been seeking to propose an opposition not aligned with the United States.\par \par \uc2 The prelude to the hyperThe massive exodus of Venezuelans began in 2015 and three years later, when the number of emigrants traveling the region by plane, bus and on foot already exceeded three million, the \uc2 UN\uc2  included Venezuela among the countries with humanitarian crises requiring urgent assistance. The government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro rejected this qualification and then, in 2019, when the suffocating U.S. sanctions against state-owned companies and the financial system against the South American country arrived, they began to blame the "U.S. blockade", equating it with the one suffered by Cuba for more than half a century. The truth is that although the sanctions decreed by Donald Trump were not the cause of the economic crisis, they did deepen it to unprecedented levels for the country.\par \par \uc2 It was the time of the empty supermarkets, of the giant wads of money worth so little that they were thrown in the streets as a sign of protest, of the denunciations of hoarding and the raids that uncovered warehouses full of products, which businessmen refused to sell due to controlled prices that, according to their arguments, did not allow them to make profits or to guarantee new imports.\par \par \uc2 Maduro's government tried to control the situation with the Local Supply and Production Committees (CLAP) boxes that distributed basic foodstuffs imported by the State to friendly countries that allowed circumventing sanctions, constant increases in the minimum wage and a policy of ever greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. However, he was unable to stop the shortages, the deterioration of the State and of the public services under his charge -health, education, transportation, fixed telephones-, the collapse of oil production, the main source of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and hyperinflation.\par \par \uc2 Maduro was also unable to curb another effect of this economic crisis, the loss of support from the most vulnerable sectors, the backbone of Chavism's electoral base since its origin. "Since five or six years ago, we see a sustained decline of the electoral base of Chavismo. In last year's gubernatorial elections, the opposition candidates as a whole got considerably more votes than those of the ruling party. But the government kept most of the governorships because we are facing a divided opposition, which says that its priority is to defeat Chavismo, but then each force presents its own candidate and they do not reach agreements", Lander pointed out.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan strategy Venezuela managed to get out of the hyperinflation cycle it had been in since 2017. Inflation was 9585 percent in 2019 and 2294 percent in the first five months of 2020. To try to reduce the inflationary impact of monetary expansions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  increased the required legal reserve (part of the deposits tied up in the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and retracted the growth rates of monetary aggregates (circulating and demand accounts) in 2019 and in the first months of 2020. However, and as part of the plan to mitigate the effects of the pandemic, since March 2020 this policy was relaxed and the pace of issuance accelerated that year. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2 , the National Consumer Price Index reached twelve months with a monthly variation below 50 percent, considered by analysts as the threshold of hyperinflation, while accumulating four consecutive months with a single-digit monthly variation. According to the Bolivarian \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last year closed with an annual inflation of 686.4 percent. This is not all good news.\par \par \uc2 The question is how that result was achieved and which of all the measures had an effect. "The process of deceleration of inflation occurs due to the stability maintained by the price of the dollar, due to the recurrent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  made by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  by burning international reserves to maintain the exchange rate without significant oscillations," Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Epstein, political scientist and economic analyst of CEPA (Center of Political Economy of Argentina), told this newspaper.\par \par \uc2 "There was a very strong economic contraction, of 88.6 percent of GDP from November 2014 to August 2020, and a rebound 10 percentage points taking from November 2020 to December 2021. At the beginning of March, workers' base salary is equivalent to 29 dollars. There had been no revision for a year", Epstein explained. As a reference, the value of the food basket in February 2022 reached $365; that is, the minimum wage does not even cover 10 percent of the necessary food intake.\par \par \uc2 According to figures from the \uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean(ECLAC)\uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean\uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America\uc2  and the Caribbean(ECLAC), the frequent revisions of the minimum wage did not allow compensating the growth of prices, so that said wage contracted 37.4 percent in real terms between December 2018 and December 2019, and 30 percent in the first six months of 2020 with respect to the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 "The process of dollarization of the economy and the strong restriction and recession seem to be determinants for the inflationary deceleration," Epstein pointed out, even though that has increased social inequalities between those who receive income in domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with respect to those who do so in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ninety-nine percent of prices are dollarized and only 49 percent of payments in the main urban centers are made in bolivars. Grocery stores, supermarkets and clothing stores now mark their prices in dollars.\par \par \uc2 The image today is different from that of just a few years ago. In Caracas, one can again see the gondolas full, the highways full of cars, many of them new imports, and the restaurants, especially in the wealthier areas, full. "Now merchants are selling because they have the capacity to do so in dollars and buy with their dollars, without asking permission from the government", explained Lander. But that image of recovery is partial, according to the sociologist. "The shortage now is of a different type. It is no longer that of a State that tries to control things and the business community decides not to sell; now it is of a capitalist type. Before, everyone felt the shortage, now it is felt by those who have nothing to pay for it," he said.\par \par \uc2 "The humanitarian crisis has been half compensated by the remittances of six and a half million migrants. In many families the parents left and left the children with the grandparents, or the father left alone...it is a migration in which the relationship with those who stay here is very fluid. It is a cushion that has allowed many people to survive," added the sociologist.\par \par \uc2 The deficit adjustment was in line with the fall in activity. By 2017, the public deficit exceeded 20 percent of the Product, the highest in Latin America, and private estimates currently place it at around 7 percent. Other measures implemented to deepen the adjustment were to eliminate the gasoline subsidy, relax price controls and open the exchange market, which was subject to tight state control for more than 16 years.\par \par \uc2 "The strong contraction of the economy had an impact on the hyperinflationary spiral, but it attenuates the inflationary process by reaching such a significant reduction of the Venezuelan economy", added the CEPA analyst. In short, it seems that the logic that is trying to be imposed in Venezuela and that some analysts intend to copy in Argentina, is to carry out a brutal contraction of the GDP, dollarize the economy, strongly adjust public spending, consumption and salaries; and thus, lower inflation.\par \par \uc2 But even at this high cost, inflation is not a problem of the past in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, it depends on its capacity to continue controlling the price of the dollar by selling reserves -limited by US and European sanctions-, expanding its oil production capacity -also limited by sanctions, the deterioration of its infrastructure and its massive indebtedness with China, with which it has an oil-for-debt agreement- and on a flow of remittances, which will undoubtedly be affected by the crises that the economies of the countries of the region are going through. For this reason, Maduro's government has been making concrete and clear political gestures for some time now to reinsert itself in the international community -led by the United States- and become attractive to receive foreign investments.\par \par \uc2 And on the other hand, for Lander, the effect of the war in Ukraine will also be strongly felt in Venezuela. "In energy, oil and gasoline, not so much because they are highly subsidized. But in food there will be an impact because there is still a very high imported component in this area. Our country does not produce wheat, imports 100% of its flour and is no longer self-sufficient in milk, coffee and sugar", he said.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220321ei3l0020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
