\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The recipe behind Venezuela's leap to economic recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 1,332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 ana rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez braz\u243\'00\'F3n - el tiempo correspondent - caracas@anarodriguez_bThe hyperinflation nightmare is over for Venezuela. In theory, the country has had a year with monthly inflation figures below 50 percent, bidding farewell to a stretch of 37 hard months. Now the path of a recovery is emerging, still viewed with caution. During the last few years, Venezuela recorded an 80 percent drop in GDP. Its main income -oil- was also affected and went from receiving 100 dollars to 34 dollars per barrel. In 2014, a few months after Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro came to power, the situation began to deteriorate and in 2017, with the economic sanctions imposed by the United States, the pit was reached. Still in memory are those days when one had to wait in line for one or two kilos of corn flour, the brawls outside supermarkets and the images of people dying of heart attacks in the lines waiting for their turn to buy.\par \par \uc2  Although this has been overcome, there are still lines to get gasoline, as well as people looking for food in the garbage. However, this 2022 is expected to grow 8.6 percent compared to 2021. This data is provided by the report of the Instituto de Investigaciones Econ\u243\'00\'F3micas y Sociales de la Universidad Cat\u243\'00\'F3lica Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Bello (IIES Ucab), which estimates a better year for the country, despite the fact that extreme poverty closed above 90 percent, according to figures of this same institution. The economic progress is focused on estimating that oil production could reach 950,000 barrels per day. Thus, the oil GDP would grow 39 percent with a five percent increase. Henkel Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, director of the firm Econom\u233\'00\'E9trica, explains to this newspaper that everything started from 2018, when there was a change in the position of the National Government by starting to lower the harassment to entrepreneurship and all its policy of controls. Although for Garc\u237\'00\'EDa this is not enough, at this moment there is evidence of a deceleration of inflation and containment of the exchange rate. "If this trend of fiscal and monetary discipline that the Government has had continues, we could end up with a double digit inflation in 2022", he said. The study of the Catholic University coincides with the appreciations of Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, who participated in the forum 'Analysis of the current situation of Venezuela and perspectives for the year 2022', organized by the web portal La gran aldea. The document details that the measures implemented by Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro's regime, such as the "monetary and fiscal stabilization" carried out by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ), have had positive effects. Researchers believe that inflation would reach 225 percent annually, below the 686 percent it had in 2021, and even lower compared to the 2,960 percent in 2020 and the 9,586 percent in 2019. However, that 225 percent rate is still inflationary. According to analysts, this recovery formally started in the second half of 2021 was also obtained by the increase in oil production and exports to Asian markets, managing to evade sanctions. In this sense, the exchange rate stabilization allowed certain freedom in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, reducing the legal reserve from 85 to 73 %. In addition, tax collection has increased and the fiscal deficit has decreased. If this context remains unchanged, the path will continue to improve. Another aspect highlighted by the research is that remittances sent by migrants, estimated at US$ 2.7 billion in 2021, by 2022 would be US$ 3.5 billion, which represents an increase of 26.1 percent in 2021 and 30 percent in 2022. Recovery without the \uc2 IMF\uc2  "Venezuela was adapting to the US sanctions and found new markets to orient its exports. This allows us to affirm that the Venezuelan economy is beginning to recover," economist \uc2 Francisco Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez\uc2  told EL TIEMPO. However, Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez warned that living standards will continue to be low compared to the past. The also founder of Oil for \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when asked whether or not the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) is necessary taking into account that the Caribbean country did not execute this procedure, the expert highlights that with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  "everything would have been easier". "If a loan had been obtained in this way, it would not have been necessary to resort to private parties". "It would have been positive for the country to have had the support of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  because it would have prevented the contraction of imports from being so strong", emphasizes Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez. For him, the outlook is worse because although there is no debt with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , there is debt with private companies, amounting to US$ 170 billion. This implies that although this debt is in default for reasons related to the sanctions, when the nation becomes part of the world economy and the sanctions end, it will have to solve the problem of debt restructuring. "They are creditors who are willing to sue Venezuela, to take assets from it, to seize payments for oil effects and the debt will accumulate faster", warns Rodriguez. Hence, everyone agrees that these Venezuelan signs of recovery are comparable to a person suffering from a serious illness, who, although showing signs of recovery, is left with after-effects that are not so easy to heal. Uneven with these data and improvements, the purchasing power of the common citizen continues to be a nightmare. However, on Thursday, Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro announced that the minimum wage will be set at half a Petro (digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  created by the Government), which is equivalent to 126 bol\u237\'00\'EDvares (28 dollars), plus a proposed 45 bol\u237\'00\'EDvares in food bonus, which would be about 10 dollars. If this is implemented, as the President did not indicate when it would come into effect, Venezuela would be talking about 40 dollars in salary, which represents an increase of 1,700 percent with respect to the 10 bolivars currently paid to workers. For Garc\u237\'00\'EDa it is important to take into account that if the economic contraction was of 80 percent, in order to recover the level the country had, a 400 percent growth would be needed. Hence, people do not fully feel the improvements that Maduro himself has talked about. "Trade is recovering much more than production in areas such as manufacturing, for example. In that uneven growth we see very depressed wages," says Garcia. The humanitarian emergency still persists. For Susana Raffalli, a public nutritionist, Venezuela is still among the countries with the most intense emergency. In the region, it is accompanied by Guatemala and Colombia, the latter with an increased risk precisely because of Venezuelan migration. And despite the fact that the regime tries to avoid them, the sanctions have also weighed heavily. "Today, 14'000,000 Venezuelans have humanitarian needs. According to the \uc2 UN\uc2 , 7.8 million Venezuelans are undernourished," Raffalli noted. Analysts agree that, in addition to the measures already adopted, it would be beneficial to improve basic services, expand bank credit, increase oil production and exports. "If the law of fair prices is repealed, that would give an institutional floor to the economic opening we are seeing", said Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, who also agrees with the need to relax sanctions. Everything points to the fact that the Venezuelan economy will continue to be based on oil revenues added to tax collection. With those 950,000 barrels per day that production would reach, there would be a 75 percent growth in the country's main industry, which currently does not exceed 543,000 barrels per day. "In addition, exports could reach 760,000 barrels per day," says the Ucab report. For now, the great beneficiary is Maduro, who will have to demonstrate that he has found a way to one of his weakest points: economic stability. Something that would mean political stability. If this progression is maintained, at least until 2024, when the presidential elections will take place, the current administration will find a longed-for stability.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220307ei36000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
