\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Maduro denounces before the \uc2 UN\uc2  the use of the migratory crisis to "justify an invasion" of Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 MADRID (EUROPA PRESS)-Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro denounced on Monday before the \uc2 United Nations Human Rights Council\uc2  the use of the migration crisis to "justify an invasion" of Venezuelan territory.\par \par \uc2 Maduro has thus defended that Venezuela is known for being "historically" a recipient of migrants. "More than 12 million migrants live here, most of them coming from Colombia," he has pointed out during the 49th regular session of the council.\par \par \uc2 "There are some six million Colombians fleeing war, misery, drug trafficking and paramilitarism in Colombia. Today they are resorting to the expedient of the campaign against migrants to justify an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", he asserted before forcefully rejecting "the perverse campaign".\par \par \uc2 Likewise, he denounced the "kidnapping" of Venezuelan gold and capital by foreign banks. "I want to denounce that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , with the support of the \uc2 Government of the United Kingdom\uc2 , is keeping 31 tons of gold of our country, belonging to the historical legal reserves of Venezuela," he lamented.\par \par \uc2 "I must also denounce that the Novo Banco de Portugal has sequestered in the modality of freezing more than one billion dollars (about 893 million euros) of Venezuela, that Citibank transferred 342 million dollars (305 million euros) from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  belonging to the country, to an account of the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  in an illegal manner. With this money they pay \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of international criminal organizations", he said in a telematic session.\par \par \uc2 Maduro has stated that the financial institutions have denied the Caribbean country the "right to use these resources to buy vaccines, medicines, food or supplies in the fight against the coronavirus pandemic". "The misnamed sanctions have deprived our country of 99 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  income from exports," he has stressed.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, he has insisted that these resources are used by other countries to finance criminal actions against Venezuela. "With this money they pay for the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the international criminal organization (...) that attacks our country", he has reiterated.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, he has pointed out that there are currently more than 500 coercive measures to "damage the Venezuelan economy". "Venezuela has been the target of 502 unilateral coercive measures and other criminal and illegal, direct and indirect measures to destroy our economy, destabilize our democracy and put an end to the inclusive social model built by the Bolivarian revolution," he explained.\par \par \uc2 Shortly before, Maduro emphasized that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) has refused to give Venezuela 5 billion dollars (about 4.4 billion euros) as part of the "special drawing rights" to face the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In March 2020, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  rejected the Venezuelan government's request for financial assistance of 5 billion dollars to address the pandemic after considering that the "fund was not in a position to consider such request".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220301ei2s001kx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
