\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Academics consider that reform of tsj law reinforces lack of judicial independence\b0\par\par\uc2 1,745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Caracas - The Academy of Political and Social Sciences of Venezuela rejected the reform to the Organic Law of the Supreme Court of Justice (TSJ) that reduces the number of magistrates, as well as the way in which they are elected by the national Parliament, because they consider that the respective public consultations were not carried out and "the absence of independence" of justice in the country is reinforced. In a communiqu\u233\'00\'E9, issued on Friday 28 and published on its website, the Academy sets out its position on the reform of the Organic Law of the Supreme Court of Justice (LOTSJ), published on January 19, 2022, in Official Gazette No.\par \par \uc2  6.684, Extraordinary, with the sole purpose of modifying the current composition of the highest court of Justice by reducing the number of its members and the appointment of new Magistrates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its organization and \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."Justice in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  lacks independence since magistrates, judges and prosecutors do not have stability and are subject to external pressures. This lack of independence is a guarantee of impunity for crime, especially for serious human rights violations including crimes against humanity, and at the same time, it is used for the political persecution of opponents of the regime", they write in the document.Communiqu\u233\'00\'E9 of the Academy of Political and Social Sciences of Venezuela"This Academy has repeatedly insisted on the defense and the necessary validity of the values and principles that inspire the Venezuelan constitutional system and the necessary independence and suitability of the Judiciary as a guarantee of the fundamental elements of the Rule of Law. Likewise, it has held that the basis and foundation of the administration of justice is compliance with the constitutional rules on the appointment and election of judges and magistrates, in which transparency and broad participation of society is an element of its legitimacy as a Public Power. The Supreme Court of Justice must enjoy independence and autonomy as guarantor of the supremacy and effectiveness of the constitutional norms and principles; it is also responsible for the direction, government and administration of the Judicial Power, the inspection and supervision of the courts and the public defender's offices (art. 267, Constitution). Only a Supreme Court of Justice and courts composed of competent, independent and impartial judges can comply with the constitutional and international standards established in the treaties and other instruments in force. In this regard, it is necessary to recall that according to the UN Basic Principles on the Independence of the Judiciary, persons selected for judicial office shall be "persons of integrity and ability and shall have appropriate legal training or qualifications", that, "No method used for the selection of judicial personnel shall ensure that they are not appointed for improper motives" and finally, that in the selection of judges "no discrimination shall be made", including on the grounds of political or other opinion (Art. 10).).According to international rulings, as well as the decisions and opinions of international bodies, justice in Venezuela lacks independence since magistrates, judges and prosecutors lack stability and are subject to external pressures. This lack of independence is a guarantee of impunity for crime, especially for serious human rights violations including crimes against humanity, and at the same time, it is used for the political persecution of opponents of the regime. For the Academy it has been an ethical priority to defend the Rule of Law and the primacy of the 1999 Constitution, hence publicly through its communiqu\u233\'00\'E9s and meetings it has referred to the outrages committed by the Supreme Court of Justice through its sentences, which rather than interpreting the Constitution has arbitrarily changed its norms, creating defenselessness, lack of protection and legal insecurity. For the aforementioned reasons, in view of the promulgation of the reform of the Organic Law of the Supreme Court of Justice, the Academy of Political and Social Sciences is obliged to pronounce itself, rejecting its text for the following legal reasons:1. The National Assembly did not make the respective consultations on this legislative reform to the other organs of the State, to the citizens, especially to the independent experts in the matter and to the organized civil society, in accordance with the provisions of Article 211 of the Constitution of the Republic. 2. The change of Article 8 of said Law, by which the number of judges of the Constitutional Chamber is reduced to five and the judges of the remaining Chambers to three, was carried out without having accredited the technical, material and substantive reasons for such modification of the Supreme Court of Justice, in accordance with the provisions of Articles 204 of the Constitution, which is applicable to all of the modified articles.3. The amendment of Article 25 adds a clause incorporating the constitutional mechanism of inter-institutional dialogue between the Constitutional Chamber and the National Assembly, which will prevent the Constitutional Chamber not only from exercising the so-called normative jurisdiction, but also from issuing favorable rulings, which constitutes a legislative overreach, all the more serious insofar as it omits the fact that the other Chambers, such as the Civil Cassation Chamber, have also been unconstitutionally modifying several laws (annulling legal norms and changing their legal meaning), in the illegitimate and abusive exercise of a misunderstood diffuse control of constitutionality. 4. In accordance with the provisions of Article 270 of the Constitution, the Judicial Nominations Committee, as a means of citizen participation in the management of public affairs, and especially in the election of judges of the Supreme Court of Justice, is composed of representatives of the different sectors of society, in accordance with the provisions of the law, and therefore, cannot have deputies as members. In other words, the Judicial Nominations Committee is not an organ of the National Assembly, nor of the Citizen Power. Notwithstanding the clear constitutional provision, the reform of Articles 65 and 69 of the LOTSJ modify the correlation of members of the Judicial Nominations Committee, in favor of deputies to the detriment of civil society, going from 54.54% to 47.61%. Likewise, an absolute majority is established as a quorum for constitution and a simple majority as a quorum for deliberation, all in favor of the political control of the judiciary by the parliamentary majority established in Article 65.5. Article 64 of the Reform imposes the incorporation of the Judicial Nominations Committee as an advisory body in the selection of Magistrates of the Supreme Court of Justice, the Inspector General of Courts and the Director of the National School of the Magistracy, as well as of the Judicial Electoral Colleges for the election of judges of disciplinary competence, without it being known what were the technical, material and substantive reasons for its incorporation in this task, other than the parliamentary political control of the judiciary. Pursuant to Article 270, as stated, the Judicial Nominations Committee cannot have deputies as members. This, in short, denaturalizes and politicizes the Committee; and also constitutes a usurpation of constitutional functions since, by incorporating deputies to the Nominating Committee, which is only responsible for selecting the list of candidates for the position, then those same deputies are incorporated to the National Assembly to elect the Magistrates from that same list.6. The reform of Articles 81 and 83 establishes that both the Inspector General of Courts and the Director of the National School of Magistrates will be appointed by the National Assembly, based on the same procedure established for the appointment of the Magistrates of the Supreme Court of Justice, which entails a severe political interference of the Legislative Power in the independence, autonomy and functioning of the Judicial Power (art. 267, Constitution).7. Article 38 of the LOTSJ, as amended, maintains with a different wording, the definitive selection of the magistrates by a simple majority of the members of the National Assembly, if the latter does not achieve, after three consecutive sessions without an agreement for the appointment, the favorable vote of two thirds (2/3) of its members. This rule, in addition to violating the constitutional principle of the appointment of all high authorities of the State with the favorable vote of two thirds (2/3) of the members of the National Assembly, which is even expressly ratified for the removal of the judges of the TSJ (art.265).8. Regarding the incorporation of the second final provision (art. 13), the reform states that the appointment of the twenty new magistrates and their alternates will proceed, adding that the magistrates "who by the date of entry into force of this Law have not completed the term for which they were appointed and designated, may run again for these positions". Consequently, it would be producing for certain magistrates, the possibility of spending more years in the Supreme Court than the fixed 12 years that they can remain in their positions. This is contrary to the express provisions of the Constitution (art. 264) regarding the total duration of the term of office or period of office of the justices: "(...) they shall be elected for a single term of twelve years". Thus, magistrates can only be elected for a single term, the maximum duration of which, in any case, is twelve years.9. The reform that replaces the Unidad Tributaria (Tax Unit) as the reference value for the determination of jurisdiction and the fines provided for in Articles 26, 81, 121, 122 and 123, with the official exchange rate of the highest value \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  established by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2 , generates serious doubts as to its constitutionality. In view of the content of the Law of partial reform of the LOTSJ, it is necessary to conclude that it deepens the political dependence of the appointment of the magistrates of said Court and other instances of the justice system in Venezuela, and therefore, with effects on the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of all the organs of justice. By virtue of this, it is concluded that this Law reinforces the absence of independence of justice in Venezuela, as it is openly contrary to the standards of due process, appointment and stability of judges, contained in international instruments on human rights and the Rome Statute, as well as in the jurisprudence of the courts and other international bodies, especially the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  and the organs and agencies of the United Nations". Julio Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Berrizbeitia, President.Cecilia Sosa G\u243\'00\'F3mez, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220131ei1v008q5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
