\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Venezuela close to leaving hyperinflation behind, but not its aftermath\b0\par\par\uc2 1,045 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In one of the countries with the worst economic prospects in the world, the exit from the hyperinflationary cycle after more than four years looks like a light at the end of the tunnel. Venezuela -according to economists and what the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  itself has announced this week- will reach in the first quarter of 2022 more than 12 months with monthly inflations below 50%, which would technically indicate its exit from hyperinflation. The aftermath of this cycle, however, will still remain a lead in the wing for the economic recovery that the government promises every year.\par \par \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2  belatedly acknowledged hyperinflation, a word rarely uttered officially, although it has been one of the problems that has most afflicted Venezuelans in recent years. Venezuela and Zimbabwe have been the only countries with hyperinflation during the 21st century. The Caribbean nation is also one of the rare cases of oil-producing countries that have experienced this economic disease. And Venezuela will be the only country that has gone through hyperinflation and a pandemic, says Marino Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez, a researcher at Sim\u243\'00\'F3n Bol\u237\'00\'EDvar University in Venezuela and \uc2 La Rioja University\uc2  in Spain.\par \par \uc2 For the academic, who has dedicated himself to studying hyperinflation and its approaches, the Venezuelan hyperinflation has not been an exclusively monetary phenomenon like most of the cases recorded in history. "Unlike other Latin American countries like Brazil and Argentina, here it manifests itself after a great loss of productive capacity experienced by 15 years of controls. The destructive effect of hyperinflation adds to the previous and now pandemic."\par \par \uc2 Looking at some indicators between 2017, when the hyperinflationary fire started, and 2021 shows its effects. From 87% poverty, the country went to 96%, according to the annual Encovi survey. There is another more dramatic data: at the close of 2017 only 1.4 million Venezuelans had left the country, today there are six million out, according to United Nations figures, moved mostly by the impossibility of sustaining themselves economically in the country. "The human capital lost during this time will be difficult to replace," says economist and university professor Hermes P\u233\'00\'E9rez.\par \par \uc2 P\u233\'00\'E9rez points out that Venezuela suffers from the three most serious diseases of an economy: consecutive economic contraction for eight years, hyperinflation and unemployment, which this year reaches half of the active population. "Of course it's good news to get out of hyperinflation, but in 2022 Venezuela will undoubtedly continue to have the highest inflation in the world, even now when all countries are fighting it.\par \par \uc2 Inflation destroys the functions of money, P\u233\'00\'E9rez explains. One scar left by hyperinflation in Venezuela is the virtual disappearance of the bol\u237\'00\'EDvar, which has even changed its face in the one-bol\u237\'00\'EDvar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (which has been stripped of 14 zeros after three reconversions). The so-called Barre bolivar, after Albert Barre, the engraver of the Paris Mint who was commissioned in 1873 to engrave the profile of the Venezuelan hero facing to the left, persisted on the coins until a few months ago. Those that came out in October, with the third monetary reconversion, have the face that Chavismo reconstructed after the exhumation of the remains of the Liberator, ordered by \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2  in 2010, stirring up the historical conspiracy that he was assassinated by his enemies. It is the one repeated on all banknotes since 2018 and, since this year, on coins, with a wider, rounder nose, bushy eyebrows and square jaw, "the mulatto Bolivar," as Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez, who once joked about his resemblance, called him. In any case, today it is the most devalued bolivar.\par \par \uc2 Today, dollars account for 70% of transactions in the country. They are in the hands of informal vendors or at the cash register of any establishment. The de facto dollarization allowed by the Government from 2019, after the repeal of the illicit exchange rates, is the natural response in any process of hyperinflation, but in Venezuela it has ended up throwing confidence in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will be a problem for the future. "Not having confidence in the monetary sign does not help economic recovery," P\u233\'00\'E9rez stresses, and Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez points out, as another peculiarity of the Venezuelan process, that none of the hyperinflations that have occurred in Latin America have ended with dollarization.\par \par \uc2 But for Maduro - and for the country - it has been an escape valve, as he has recently acknowledged, and it is one of the factors that has helped to get out of hyperinflation, four years later just before surpassing the five-year record that Nicaragua experienced between 1986 and 1991, in the first decade of Sandinismo.\par \par \uc2 "The exit from hyperinflation is happening by spontaneous generation, not by joint economic measures to combat it," P\u233\'00\'E9rez responds. In his opinion, in addition to dollarization, other situations have had a greater impact on ending the hyperinflationary cycle in an economy that is a fifth of what it was a decade ago. The destruction of the demand for money, in part because of the six million Venezuelans who are not in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the liberalization of prices, the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with injections of dollars to artificially contain the exchange rate - because despite the sanctions it now benefits from the rise in oil prices - and, the most costly of the measures, according to P\u233\'00\'E9rez, the exaggerated legal reserve imposed on banks to totally restrict commercial and personal credit.\par \par \uc2 Lowering inflation is just one of the conditions for the economy to take off. Different economic firms forecast a moderate contraction by the end of 2021, after falls of up to 39% in a single year, and even some growth for 2022 driven mainly by the private sector. The haze, however, remains over whether these easings will be sustainable and lead to a real economic turnaround. Also about whether the necessary political agreements will occur - today in the freezer - that will create legal certainty for more investors to enter the field and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://elpais.com/internacional/2021-11-28/el-lujo-de-la-elite-chavista-se-da-cita-en-el-hotel-humboldt.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://elpais.com/internacional/2021-11-28/el-lujo-de-la-elite-chavista-se-da-cita-en-el-hotel-humboldt.html}}}\uc2  has led this opening that has made the economy of the poorest country in Latin America - and the most backward in the world,\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211224ehco006pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
