\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2 's US$5 billion that Venezuela doesn't receive and for which the government and opposition are negotiating\b0\par\par\uc2 1,250 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 US$5 billion is a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 Especially for a country like Venezuela, which has been in the throes of a serious economic and social crisis for years.\par \par \uc2 That's the amount that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) is supposed to give the country as part of the special drawing rights that the Fund will allocate to developing countries to help them alleviate the economic blow of the covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But Venezuela is for the moment excluded from that distribution.\par \par \uc2 At least, as long as the opposition and the government, negotiating in Mexico to end the country's long-running political crisis, do not agree on how to handle that money and other Venezuelan state assets under litigation abroad since January 2019, when opposition leader \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2  proclaimed himself interim president of the country and won the recognition of the United States and most European and Latin American countries.\par \par \uc2 Who is Pedro Castillo, the rural teacher who became president of Peru and challenges the elites from the left?Pedro Castillo: His 4 biggest challenges as Peru's presidentIMF: How much money each Latin American country will receive with the largest injection of resources approved by the organization in its historyMoody's downgrade of Peru's credit rating may have consequences for PeruThe latest news about the negotiation in Mexico is not encouraging and Venezuelans continue to pay the effects of lack of agreement between the political actors in their country.\par \par \uc2 Nicolas Maduro made clear his demands for the government to participate in the new dialogue process. REUTERSWhatare\par \par \uc2 Special DrawingRightsSpecial Drawing Rights are a system created in 1969 by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to provide liquidity to world economies and to be able to provide additional reserves to member countries in case of difficulty\par \par \uc2 .Manuel Sutherland, director of the Caracas-based Centro de Investigaci\u243\'00\'F3n e Informaci\u243\'00\'F3n Obrera (CIFO), told \uc2 BBC\uc2  Mundo that it was "an anchor in a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could be used or exchanged as a reserve fund in situations of economic difficulty".\par \par \uc2 When the 2008 crisis erupted they were used to keep the world economy afloat and in 2020 the Fund again resorted to this mechanism to counter the effects of the coronavirus on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  has not yet released the corresponding funds to Venezuela. GETTYIMAGESWhy the money is not coming to VenezuelaVenezuela\par \par \uc2 should be one of the countries most benefiting from this possibility of financing on very advantageous terms, which other countries will begin to receive imminently, but the international dispute over who is its legitimate president, the poor relations of successive Chavista governments with the Fund and the breaches of the transparency requirements it demands by the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro prevent it from receiving the funds\par \par \uc2 .The \uc2 IMF\uc2  agreed this time to release about $650 billion, the largest amount in its history.\par \par \uc2 The refusal of member countries like the United States that do not recognize Maduro is blocking the disbursement of Venezuela's share of that huge pie.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland also notes that "there is also a lot of mistrust from sectors within the country that do not want the Maduro government to directly manage that money.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan opposition has insisted for years that Venezuela is living under "a dictatorship" and Guaid\u243\'00\'F3's so-called "interim government" has sought ways to wrest control of Venezuelan assets abroad from Maduro.\par \par \uc2 Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 claims it is about "protecting" the wealth of Venezuelans, but Maduro's government considers it "an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to plunder and steal the national patrimony."\par \par \uc2 Both parties maintain open litigation regarding Venezuelan strategic assets abroad, such as Citgo, a subsidiary in the United States of the state-owned \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos de \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the company Mon\u243\'00\'F3meros, recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Colombian authorities; or the nearly US$2 billion of Venezuelan gold retained by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and still pending a final resolution by the British courts.\par \par \uc2 Government and opposition negotiate again in Mexico, but for the moment they are still far from an agreement. HECTOR VIVAS / GETTYAlreadyin March 2020, at the start of the pandemic, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  rejected the government's request for a US$5 billion emergency loan. "There is no clarity" on international recognition of Maduro, the Fund said in a statement at the time.\par \par \uc2 But the Venezuelan government's friction with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  goes back a long way.\par \par \uc2 The late President \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2  frequently criticized the Fund, which he considered "an instrument of colonialism" and even promoted alternative international organizations with the support of allied governments in the region.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan government also forced \uc2 IMF\uc2  technicians out of the country and closed the door to advisory and collaboration missions.\par \par \uc2 Usually, the Fund requests the development of reform plans and the supervision of their execution from the countries to which it grants loans, but, as Sutherland recalls, "Chavismo has refused to cede what it calls economic sovereignty and to make the economic changes demanded by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and a large part of civil society".\par \par \uc2 Most experts agree that years of statist economic \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 , corruption and waste led \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its current situation, which has been aggravated by U.S. sanctions and has driven 5.4 million people to leave the country, according to \uc2 U.N\uc2 . figures.\par \par \uc2 What could be done with that money in Venezuela\par \par \uc2 Economist Sutherland said "US$5 billion would seem small for an oil economy like Venezuela's, which once had US$80 billion in average annual export revenue.\par \par \uc2 But the drop in Venezuela's oil exports, its main source of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  income, has drastically reduced its international reserves, now just over US$6.3 billion, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Given that figure, Sutherland estimates the corresponding drawing rights could represent up to ten times the total liquidity the country now has.\par \par \uc2 The aid could serve to alleviate the suffering of Venezuelans when one in three suffers from food insecurity, according to the UN World Food Programme, and the National Survey of Living Conditions carried out by experts from Venezuela's main universities calculates that eight out of ten households live in poverty.\par \par \uc2 GETTY IMAGESSutherlandpoints out that "an agreement at the dialogue table in Mexico "could facilitate obtaining those resources that are so urgently needed"\par \par \uc2 .However, the negotiations appear to be stalled.\par \par \uc2 4 keys to Venezuela's new dialogue between government and opposition in MexicoJorge Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez, head of the Chavista delegation, announced that he will include Alex Saab, a Colombian businessman imprisoned in Cape Verde awaiting extradition to the United States, where he is wanted on money laundering charges.\par \par \uc2 An investigation by the website Armando.info attributed Saab as the mastermind of a massive corruption scheme linked to the government-subsidized food delivery program.\par \par \uc2 Venezuelan analyst Luis Vicente Le\u243\'00\'F3n believes that the attempt to include the controversial Saab as an accredited diplomat reflects "the government's decision to freeze the negotiation process".\par \par \uc2 Requesting the incorporation of Alex Saab to the negotiating team in Mexico is part of the official decision to release him. But given the low probability of it happening, this seems a clear message of the government's decision to freeze the negotiation process.\par \par \uc2 - Luis Vicente Leon (@luisvicenteleon) September 15, 2021Formalnegotiations continue. But, like many other observers, Sutherland views them with skepticism: "They're arguing among political party leaders with very little national rootedness, and they've forgotten about the most urgent issues, like the humanitarian and social crisis." (I)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210921eh9l007vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
