\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2 's US$5 billion that Venezuela is not receiving because the government and the opposition can't agree on it\b0\par\par\uc2 1,274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Getty Images US$5 billion is a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 Especially for a country like Venezuela, which has been in the throes of a serious economic and social crisis for years.\par \par \uc2 That's the amount that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) is supposed to give the country as part of the special drawing rights that the Fund is allocating to developing countries to help them ease the economic blow of the covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But Venezuela is for the moment excluded from that distribution.\par \par \uc2 At least, as long as the opposition and the government, negotiating in Mexico to end the country's long-running political crisis, do not agree on how to handle that money and other Venezuelan state assets\par \par \uc2 under litigation abroad since January 2019, when opposition leader \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2  proclaimed himself interim president of the country and won the recognition of the United States and most European and Latin American countries. The latest news about the negotiation in Mexico is not encouraging and Venezuelans continue to pay the effects of lack of agreement between political actors in their country.\par \par \uc2 What are Special Drawing Rights Special Drawing Rights are a system created in 1969 by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to provide liquidity to world economies and to be able to provide additional reserves to member countries in case of difficulty\par \par \uc2 . Manuel Sutherland, director of the Caracas-based Centro de Investigaci\u243\'00\'F3n e Informaci\u243\'00\'F3n Obrera (CIFO), told \uc2 BBC\uc2  Mundo that it was "an anchor in a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could be used or exchanged as a reserve fund in situations of economic difficulty\par \par \uc2 ". When the 2008 crisis broke out they were used to keep the world economy afloat and in 2020 the Fund again resorted to this mechanism to counteract the effects of the coronavirus on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  has not yet released the funds corresponding to Venezuela Getty Images The \uc2 IMF\uc2  has not yet released the funds corresponding to Venezuela Why the money is not reaching Venezuela Venezuela should be one of the countries that will benefit the most from this possibility of financing on very advantageous terms, which other countries will begin to receive imminently, but the international dispute over who is its legitimate president, the poor relations of successive Chavista governments with the Fund and the breaches of the transparency requirements that it demands by the government of Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro prevent it from receiving the funds\par \par \uc2 . The \uc2 IMF\uc2  agreed this time to release about $650 billion, the largest amount in its history\par \par \uc2 . The refusal of member countries like the United States that do not recognize Maduro is blocking the disbursement of Venezuela's share of that huge pie.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland also notes that " there is also a lot of mistrust\par \par \uc2 from sectors within the country that do not want the Maduro government to directly manage that money. The Venezuelan opposition has insisted for years that Venezuela is living under "a dictatorship" and Guaid\u243\'00\'F3's so-called "interim government" has sought ways to wrest control of Venezuelan assets abroad from Maduro.\par \par \uc2 Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 claims it is about "protecting" the wealth of Venezuelans, but Maduro's government considers it "an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to plunder and steal the national patrimony."\par \par \uc2 Both parties maintain open litigation regarding Venezuelan strategic assets abroad\par \par \uc2 , such as Citgo, a US subsidiary of the state-owned \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos de \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the company Mon\u243\'00\'F3meros, recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Colombian authorities; or the nearly US$2 billion of Venezuelan gold retained by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and still pending a final resolution of the British courts. Government and opposition negotiate again in Mexico, but so far they are still far from an agreement. Hector Vivas / Getty Government and opposition negotiate again in Mexico, but for the moment they are still far from an agreement. Already in March 2020, at the beginning of the pandemic, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  rejected the government's request for a US$5 billion emergency loan. "There is no clarity" on international recognition of Maduro, the Fund said in a statement at the time.\par \par \uc2 But the Venezuelan government's friction with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  goes back a long\par \par \uc2 way. The late President \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2  frequently criticized the Fund, which he considered "an instrument of colonialism" and even promoted alternative international organizations with the support of allied governments in the region.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan government also forced \uc2 IMF\uc2  technicians out of the\par \par \uc2 country and closed the door to advisory and collaboration missions. Usually, the Fund requests the development of reform plans and the supervision of their execution from the countries to which it grants credits, but, as Sutherland recalls, "Chavismo has refused to cede what it calls economic sovereignty and to make the economic changes demanded by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and a large part of civil society".\par \par \uc2 Most experts agree that years of statist economic \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 , corruption and waste led \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its current situation, which has been aggravated by U.S. sanctions\par \par \uc2 and has driven 5.4 million people to leave the country, according to \uc2 U.N\uc2 . figures. What could be done with that money in Venezuela \uc2 Economist\uc2  Sutherland said "US$5 billion would seem small for an oil economy like Venezuela's, which once had US$80 billion in average annual export revenue\par \par \uc2 . But the drop in Venezuela's oil exports, its main source of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  income, has drastically reduced its international reserves, now just over US$6.3 billion\par \par \uc2 , according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2 . Given that figure, Sutherland estimates that the corresponding drawing rights could represent up to ten times the total liquidity the country now has.\par \par \uc2 The funds could help alleviate some of the hardships of Venezuelans. Federico Parra / Getty The funds could help alleviate some of the hardships of Venezuelans. The aid could go a long way toward alleviating the suffering of Venezuelans when one in three is food insecure\par \par \uc2 , according to the UN World Food Programme, and the National Survey of Living Conditions conducted by experts from leading Venezuelan universities estimates that eight out of 10 households live in poverty. Sutherland points out that "an agreement at the dialogue table in Mexico "could make it easier to obtain those urgently needed resources".\par \par \uc2 However, the negotiations appear to be stalled.\par \par \uc2 4 keys to the new dialogue between the Venezuelan government and opposition in Mexico \uc2 Jorge Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez\uc2 , head of the Chavista delegation, announced that he will include Alex Saab, a Colombian businessman imprisoned in Cape Verde awaiting extradition to the United States, where he is wanted on money laundering charges.\par \par \uc2 An investigation by the website Armando.info attributed Saab as the mastermind of a massive corruption scheme\par \par \uc2 linked to the government-subsidized food delivery program. Venezuelan analyst Luis Vicente Le\u243\'00\'F3n believes that the attempt to include the controversial Saab as an accredited diplomat reflects "the government's decision to freeze the negotiation process".\par \par \uc2 Requesting the incorporation of Alex Saab to the negotiating team in Mexico is part of the official decision to release him. But given the low probability of it happening, this seems a clear message of the government's decision to freeze the negoti\par \par \uc2 ation process? Luis Vicente Leon (@luisvicenteleon) September 15, 2021 Formally, negotiations continue. But, like many other observers, Sutherland views them with scepticism: "They're arguing between party leaders with very little domestic support, and they've forgotten about the most urgent issues, such as the humanitarian and social crisis".\par \par \uc2 You can now receive notifications from \uc2 BBC News\uc2  World. Download our app and turn them on so you don't miss our best content.\par \par \uc2 Do you already know our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel? Subscribe!\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210920eh9k004ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
