\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Interview / \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2  / 'The opposition is central'\b0\par\par\uc2 2,231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The loss of democracy begins with the attack on the free press, warned the president of the Venezuelan Parliament, who confirmed that Mexico is among the possible venues for a dialogue with the Government of Maduro\par \par \uc2 What was Venezuela like before Chavismo?\par \par \uc2 Venezuela pre-ninety-eight, before what they called the revolution or socialism of the 21st century, was a Venezuela with inequality, with social debts, but with access to education, with a solid oil industry, with citizen security, with territorial integrity, with the exercise of sovereignty. There was a strong middle class, a relatively low public debt compared to what we have today, inflation was under control.\par \par \uc2 What is it like today?\par \par \uc2 It is a Venezuela in extreme poverty, which has generated the largest migration and refugee crisis the continent has seen in recent decades, today only comparable to Syria, a country that has years at war.\par \par \uc2 (It's) a country where not a single vaccine (against Covid-19) has come in through the COVAX system, where a nurse earns $3 a month, where children grow up dreaming of leaving the country, it's a tragedy, a humanitarian catastrophe. We are looking for alternatives to deal with the emergency, and to restore democracy.\par \par \uc2 It is a Venezuela that is in dictatorship, in autocracy, with the peculiarity that the state disappeared. Today, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not control the interest rate, the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite being the bolivar is no longer the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (but) the dollar, the euro, gold. This sets up a failed state, the absence of the state with a dictatorship pretending to hijack the public powers.\par \par \uc2 The most basic thing is citizen security, somehow minting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and none of that today can be done by (Nicolas) Maduro.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand (there is) a majority of citizens protesting, demanding rights, looking for alternative solutions to the conflict, looking for international allies to be able to attend to the humanitarian emergency and to be able to have free and fair elections, to solve this terrible conflict that when you put it in contrast, what it was in '98, in '99, or the first part of the 2000s , versus what we have today, we see an unprecedented catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 How did the process of institutional destruction take place? How was it tolerated? Is society complicit in what happened, the businessmen, the television companies, the radio stations, the journalists?\par \par \uc2 Democracy is based on the strength of its institutions and society, on NGOs, on the natural counterweights to the State.\par \par \uc2 Democracy is always at stake. In Venezuela, they evidently did not say, as no dictator says: 'We are going to destroy the institutions, we are going to kidnap them, we are going to seize them and we are going to weaken them in order to strengthen the one in power of the day.' In Venezuela came for the first thing, through the media, censorship, persecution of the free press, critical thinking. Then, the positioning of figures affects the dictatorship in positions of power. They always tried to co-opt the institutions.\par \par \uc2 We must be very vigilant about independence, about the autonomy of the powers that be. The National Parliament, for example, must be a guarantee of comptrollership, counterweight, denunciation and voice of minorities at all times.\par \par \uc2 What they call the dictatorship of the majorities, even by one vote, you have to be very careful, that was what (Hugo) Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez exercised from the beginning leveraged in two things: one, yes the popularity at the time, but two, the use of state resources to generate social control to large parts of the population , is what some define populism, that which led to a dictatorship of the majority, and sometimes of the minority, because today Maduro does not have any kind of popular support.\par \par \uc2 How to remove Maduro from power?\par \par \uc2 We have tried literally everything. We have won majorities, founded parties, generated an almost unprecedented movement of civic struggle. In the last 12 years we have had thousands of demonstrations, some massive, millions in number of people, some modest, hunger strikes, citizens' protests, we took parliament from you electorally.\par \par \uc2 In Venezuela, we are facing a transnational dictatorship, with links to terrorism, to drug trafficking, to farc dissidents, to \uc2 the ELN\uc2 . We have suffered political assassinations, persecution, imprisonment, exile... We are resisting, from a vulnerability. The country with the largest oil reserves on the planet has no gasoline. Venezuela, today a country (the fifth or eighth) with gas reserves is now cooking with firewood, in an almost barbaric situation.\par \par \uc2 What do we have to do to achieve democracy, to achieve an orderly transition, which is what we want? What we have been doing for years: building majorities, exercising it in the streets despite the risk, making our struggle visible, seeking the articulation of the international community to accompany our demand, which is a simple one: free and fair elections.\par \par \uc2 Would you support a US military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The choice of international force is controversial, no one wins in a dilemma between war and peace. If you ask any citizen of the planet what they prefer, war or peace, 99 percent would say peace. What we have to evaluate right now in Venezuela is what alternatives we have to stop the suffering, the danger that \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2  represents, not only to Venezuelans, but to the entire continent because of drug trafficking, because of terrorism, because of the migration crisis. In other words: Would you support it or not? I support the democratization of Venezuela, the holding of free and fair elections, the big issue is how to get there. And whether or not the use of force is necessary, what I believe is that it must be evaluated responsibly in order to save lives, to regain legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 We must evaluate the social cost, the cost of a dictatorship in the region, what it represents in drug trafficking and terrorism. \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2 , by the way, with great ties to drug cartels in Mexico, which represents a great threat to the continent.\par \par \uc2 We must responsibly evaluate all the options we have to achieve an orderly transition that generates governability in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 What is most talked about now about Venezuela is this national salvation agreement, what is it? What role is Mexico going to play?\par \par \uc2 We have called it an agreement because the agreement is the objective of a negotiation. In Venezuela there have been seven negotiation processes in the last 8 years, and none has reached an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela does not need another failed negotiation process, it needs an agreement to democratize the country, to regain confidence in Venezuela in international investment, to reactivate the productive apparatus; we went from three million barrels (of crude oil) a day to less than 500 thousand, having invested 300 billion dollars in the industry.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of an agreement is the possibility of saving Venezuela, it is to reverse the migratory flow, it is to have an independent arbitrator who can govern an electoral process and turn the electoral process into a window of solution to the conflict. It is to be able to generate confidence in the multilaterals so that they triple or increase their humanitarian participation in Venezuela. The big question is how to achieve this, understanding that in the past the dictatorship used negotiation processes to defuse international pressure and thus buy time, to mock Venezuelans and the international community.\par \par \uc2 The first thing is that there is this temptation, this risk, and we must prepare ourselves for that as well. The second thing is to use the tools we have, which is the exercise of the Constitution, the clear demand for free and fair elections, international pressure. As Juan Gonzalez (adviser to US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on the Western Hemisphere) said recently: if we do not succeed in a negotiation, multilateral pressure will continue.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, being clear about all these tools is going to allow us the possibility of an agreement, as we have called it, of national salvation. Today in Venezuela we need everything from vaccines, to water, to gasoline.\par \par \uc2 It is said that it will be in Mexico that this national salvation agreement will be signed. Do they require Mexico's endorsement at this juncture?\par \par \uc2 In order to form an agreement, there are many roles for the international community: facilitation, mediators, guarantors of the process.\par \par \uc2 We have recently spoken with the Kingdom of Norway, who already did so in 2019 and are aware of the Venezuelan actors and can facilitate the process. We are evaluating possible venues for the holding of a possible negotiation... it is a possible negotiation.\par \par \uc2 We are assessing Mexico, Norway, some other countries that can independently, impartially and respectfully host a possible negotiation.\par \par \uc2 What has the process been like for Mexico to host? What has been the dialogue with Mexico and how feasible is it that Mexico will be and not another place?\par \par \uc2 It could be any country, Canada, USA, Spain. The democratic alternative is welcome in any country in the world.\par \par \uc2 One of the main variables is where the two parties feel comfortable. Not many countrieswhere the dictatorship can feel comfortable, so that's one of the variables.\par \par \uc2 In 2019 it was in Barbados and Oslo, they were two venues to hold the negotiating rounds. At the moment we are in the phase of exploration, of the possibility of a negotiation, within the technical elements is the headquarters, the physical location, and a possible one is Mexico.\par \par \uc2 From the Venezuelan experience, and what you mentioned about there not many countries that Nicolas Maduro would feel comfortable in, and Mexico is one of them, what do you think of the Mexican opposition's stance that we're on our way to becoming Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 Democracy is not only elections, we saw it for many years in Venezuela, it is independence from power, it is respect for freedom of expression, it is the strength of its institutions, it is the respected counterweights, those are the variables that have to be taken care of a lot as Mexicans.\par \par \uc2 The Opposition of any country is central, respect for the Opposition in any country is fundamental. No ruler of the day should in any way be offended when what is sought is to preserve democracy.\par \par \uc2 From your experience as an ordinary Venezuelan, what do you think of the news and what is happening in Mexico?\par \par \uc2 We, at the beginning, said: We are going to become a second Cuba. And some saw us as exaggerated. For us it is very painful to be the bad example now, now we are the bad example Cuba and Venezuela, that should fill us with strength to continue fighting, to continue fighting.\par \par \uc2 What I do draw attention to as a Venezuelan is that they are attentive to the signals, to the attack on the free press, to the attack on the Opposition, to the desire to trivialize the struggle of minority sectors. These are all signs that do not help to strengthen democracy. Any democrat must accept criticism, he must open up the spaces for the alternative, for minorities, so any signal made by the opposition must be received by the government. Being a democratic government, it must not feel attacked in any way, when what you want to preserve is democracy.\par \par \uc2 The internal political dynamic, of positioning the factors, of the different parties, is a natural political dynamic of any country, it happens in Mexico, in the United States, even in dictatorship in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 I call on both the opposition and the government of (Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel) \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2 , as well as Mexican society, to be attentive to the signs, sometimes the totalitarian drift gradually passes. There is the temptation of populism constantly.\par \par \uc2 I repeat, democracy is not just elections, it is not just the Executive, democracy is the system, the ecosystem, in some way, of institutions, of society, of respect for fundamental human rights, of respect for the public purse, and above all for the natural counterweights in each country.\par \par \uc2 What advice would you give mexico so that we do not follow the path of Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 To the Mexicans, to my brothers in Cuba, in Nicaragua, in Peru, in Colombia, preserving democracy is a daily and constant task, not a gift, it is not a legacy, it is a constant struggle, to preserve institutions, to denounce and be alert to the signs that can lead to democracy on the wrong path, to undermine democracy. , weakening the institutions, attacking the free press, the fundamental pillars of democracy, we must always be vigilant.\par \par \uc2 I call on young people to participate in decision-making, to participate in citizens' organisation, to participate in political parties, to be part of the constant denunciation, but also of the alternatives, to be part of associations, student movements, NGOs, decision-making places, that is fundamental.\par \par \uc2 What democracy is going to guarantee in the future is going to be the strength and strength of its social fabric, how willing we citizens are to defend our rights and to do our duties, because the populist temptation, the totalitarian temptation, is also going to be there forever. The best antidote is citizenship, institutions, mobilization, demand, protest, defense, counterweights, among others. I always put a lot of emphasis on the free press, because it was one of the first to attack in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210603eh63004m2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
