\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The dictionary of the crisis in Venezuela: a 2020 review from A to Z\b0\par\par\uc2 1,485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 2020 ended and Venezuela is facing another year of likely political startle and an economic crisis that seems hard to overcome. The long list of everyday problems that the country lives, and which was exacerbad by the coronavirus pandemic that marked the last nine months, can be reduced in a brief dictionary.\par \par \uc2 A, from Embrace: the dream word and the forbidden gesture. Something so common among family and friends became so important in 2020 that it had never been given before. With the appearance of the covid-19, the distance and veto came to affection. Venezuelans, like the rest of the world's citizens, dream of regaining that custom that has never been given so much value.\par \par \uc2 Bolivar B:the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost 95.50% of its value throughout the year, which made it almost useless with no choice but to give way to the dollar. In early January, 48,709 bolivars could be exchanged for a dollar. Today, 1,066,000 bolivars are needed for the same \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Covid-19 C:The scourge of the year almost all over the world was (and is) in Venezuela one of the problems best controlled by the government of \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro\uc2 . With 113,121 contagions, of which, according to official information, 95% are already recovered, and 1,025 deaths, the Caribbean country is one of the least affected by the virus.\par \par \uc2 Dollar D:The American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , once detailed by Maduro's government, shifted the devalued bolivar and became the country's trading engine, contrary to the more optimistic expectations of the representative, who hoped that the petro - venezuelan cryptocurrency launched in 2018 - would be the life table of an impoverished nation, something that did not materialize.\par \par \uc2 Election e:Controversial legislative elections on December 6 showed that more and more citizens are moving away from chavism, which lost nearly 1,400,000 votes from the 2015 elections. It still happens to the opposition led by \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2 , who did not participate in the contest, but called a popular consultation that showed him that about 1,700,000 people withdrew his trust.\par \par \uc2 F of Fraud:the word chosen by the opposition to develop its electoral counter-campaign in the face of chavism. According to the political sector that closes ranks around Guaid\u243\'00\'F3, the elections were a fraud, which is why they refused to participate and compete in the polls with their opponent.\par \par \uc2 G of Gasoline: Fuel shortages were another obstacle to be overcome. Thousands of Venezuelans were forced to queue to load their tanks, up to 6 days with their respective nights, in kilometer rows of vehicles, in order to get a few litres of gasoline.\par \par \uc2 Hyperinflation H:November closed with cumulative inflation of 3,045.92%, according to data from the National Assembly (AN), a body that provides the information in the absence of reports from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (BCV), an entity to which it is appropriate to give these figures. Several experts estimate 4,000% at the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 I of Pardons: At the end of August and by surprise, Maduro decided to grant measures of grace to 110 people, including 53 prisoners who were released and 57 other citizens who received dismissal of causes or lifting of punishments. For the opposition, it was a propaganda element of the election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Justice J:it remains one of the outstanding subjects in Venezuela and, in 2020, was ratified. Break-ins without a warrant, arbitrary detentions, violations of freedoms, prisoners without trial or disqualifications with dubious arguments. Quarantine served the authorities to strengthen control andRepression.\par \par \uc2 Kilometers K: a measure to which Venezuelans are very accustomed, and not only to calculate the distance of their travels. Kilometers of tail to load fuel, kilometers to find signal on the phones, to look for water, kilometers walking to leave the country clandestinely. And the difficulties that added 2020 added more kilometers in the particular account of each.\par \par \uc2 L de Leopoldo (Lopez): the escape of the opponent, who was in the residence of the Spanish ambassador to \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a guest, since April 30, 2019, copied the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the president and government officials in the last week of October, and generated a wave of reactions of chavismo against Spain, where L\u243\'00\'F3pez settled after his departure from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 M of Migration:Due to the coronavirus pandemic, thousands of people who had left Venezuela fleeing misery were forced to return because they had lost their jobs in host countries, but over the months and the laxity of the closing and confinement measures, the exodus began again.\par \par \uc2 N of Shipwreck: The desire to leave the country led hundreds of Venezuelans to venture out by sea, especially to Trinidad and Tobago, in small and improvised boats that were wrecked, causing the drowning deaths of more than a hundred people during 2020.\par \par \uc2 Or Obstacle:if there is a country in Latin America where every day becomes an obstacle course, it is Venezuela. Opening the pot and crossing your fingers to get water out is probably the first challenge to overcome. And then... will there be light? How much will the bolivar have been devalued? Will the price of flour have doubled? Will you get gas?\par \par \uc2 Parliament's P: the institution that chavism has longed for since it lost in 2015 and which it managed to recover in the December 6 elections. Legislative power was a cause for debate every day of 2020. On 5 January, the new Members will take office and Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 will have to make way for the new generation, although he threatens to hold on to the parliamentary presidency against the wind and tide.\par \par \uc2 Q of Perhaps:in Venezuela there is no certainty of almost anything, not even of the most common issues. Maybe there's water today. Or not. Maybe you'll get gas, or maybe you won't. The light may not go out all day, or maybe it will. You'll probably get the medicine you need at a pharmacy. Or maybe none at all.\par \par \uc2 R of Resilience: Venezuelans' ability to adapt to almost any situation is not something new, but 2020 earned them to break their own record and prove that they are prepared to assume that a pandemic is just one more stone along the way.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions S: WithJoe Biden's victory in the U.S. presidential election, he came to Venezuela in the hope that sanctions blocking Caribbean country funds and property in the American nation will be lifted, at least in part, when the elected representative takes office on January 20.\par \par \uc2 Telework T : abad idea turned into an obligation. To telework, you need at least one stable internet connection, an electrical system without constant outages and good telephone signal. None of the three is guaranteed in Venezuela, let alone if it is a private address. The telework turned 2020 into a "teletortura".\par \par \uc2 U of Usurpation: if there is something that has not changed this year in the South American country it is Guaid\u243\'00\'F3's favorite word. He maintains that Maduro is a "usurpable" for holding on to the Presidency that"assures" it is up to him, even though it has not been measured in an election.\par \par \uc2 Vaccine V: two days before the end of 2020, Venezuela signed with Russia a contract for the purchase of 10 million doses of the Sputnik V vaccine to prevent covid-19 and in it are given all hopes to return to normal.\par \par \uc2 W of Wuhan:the place where it all began, where the germ of the virus that would change the world was gestized, and that would give the final lunge to a Venezuela ravaged by dozens of vicissitudes and plunged into a crisis that many craved eternally.\par \par \uc2 X of Xenophobia:the amendments introduced by the National Electoral Council (CNE) for indigenous voting, including the elimination of direct and individual suffrage, resulted in rejection by the opposition and the detractors of chavism, who crossed out the governing body and the government of xenophobes and accused them of marginalizing minorities.\par \par \uc2 And from Yulimar (Rojas): in a few things there is consensus in such a polarized country, but the athlete, double world champion of triple jump and silver at the Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro in 2016, again achieved unanimous applause, after receiving, last week, the 'Silvestre of the Year' award for the world indoor record she won with a jump of 15.43 meters at the Mitin in Madrid.\par \par \uc2 Zoom Z: The video call app shortened distances, facilitated interviews and allowed work meetings. And it also served to make birthday toasts or family parties that made loneliness more bearable. But, like any technological element, it is far from the ideal system in Venezuela, due to the lousy functioning of the connections.\par \par \uc2 By Sabela Bello, EFE agency\par \par \uc2 Cb?\par \par \uc2 Look too\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210102eh120033g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
