\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Dollarization squeezes Maduro's "betrayal" on his failed socialism\b0\par\par\uc2 1,042 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \par \par \uc2 The unstoppable rise in the dollar in Venezuela has deranged dictator Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro, who to halt the rise of the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country, decided to inject public money through bonds.\par \par \uc2 The "consecutive flood maelstrom weekly" of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  puts pressure on the regime, whose unpredictable behavior led to an increase of 46.6% in the last three weeks, Ecoanalytics says.\par \par \uc2 The figure shows that the market is sensitive. The firm suggests that the dollar price is going through an "uncertainty" that enhances its value.\par \par \uc2 Changes (certain or not) to the minimum wage, the regime's intention to authorize a more sustained easing of local business activity and the coming (and almost inevitable) episodes of gasoline shortages are three factors affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shooting.\par \par \uc2 The "injection of resources" by tyranny into the market is the "element that alters exchange dynamics".\par \par \uc2 According to Ecoanalytics data, chavismo records "interbank transactions with ever-increasing amounts of up to $37 million and a drastic fee cut is an unequivocal sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCV) \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such transactions, either directly or under payment orders issued from official entities."\par \par \uc2 An instant effect Following these actions, the amount of money issued by the BCV (monetary base or primary money) increased by 22.2% in real terms becoming "the largest increase seen in the aggregate since the beginning of 2019, when the minimum wage was tripled in a month," notes the analysis of the finance firm.\par \par \uc2 Although these movements of Maduro's socialism are executed "in the absence of official information on the origin of such an injection", it is clear that the "increase comes expressly from state payments to its employees of these eras, further fueled by the forthcoming legislative electoral processes convened for the coming week."\par \par \uc2 The move is far-fetched, as "for a country to increase its monetary base and monetary liquidity by 30%, it would take 50 weeks or four years," Alejandro Castro, \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager, revealed to La Prensa.\par \par \uc2 Dollarization tightens "The marked dollarization in which the Venezuelan market is still immersed, the persistence of the hyperinflationary picture and the fall in economic activity with official countermeasures" put pressure on Maduro, also says Ecoanalytics.\par \par \uc2 This was "a radical change" in the regime's position on its spending, "not only relative to what has been seen in recent months but to its own stance since 2018 when Maduro initiated its Recovery plan and Economic Prosperity began a marked cut in its disbursements, including payment of direct bonds, cessation of imports and a freeze on implicit wages on its employee payroll."\par \par \uc2 Now "clearly, change seems to denote the dictator's chronic absence of alternatives" "to manage the current crisis, leaving it with no choice but to use the most expensive source of funding not only for the local population, but for itself and for its short-term policy objectives," the study states.\par \par \uc2 The scenario is the photograph that "with the sustained collapse of oil revenues and no access to external borrowing, Maduro seems to have nothing left but to abandon his fiscal discipline in the months to come, betraying at times" the goals set by himself"\par \par \uc2 Hungry climbsThe parallel exchange rate rise has no brake and stands at 1 085 529 bolivars per dollar, La Patilla reported. "This represents that in a month theprice of the dollar doubled in value, because on October 28 it was priced at 508,292.82 bolivars," they stand out.\par \par \uc2 Pensioners and public workers this month lost in just 23 days half of their purchasing power, because now their salary is only equivalent to a dollar with 10 cents a month, "exacerbating poverty," the medium says.\par \par \uc2 "People no longer have to eat, the regime has issued a lot of inorganic money for the payment of bonds and grants for electoral purposes, with the intention of achieving a minimum of participation in its parliamentary fraud," Alfonso Marquina, chairman of the Finance Commission of the National Assembly, released the portal.\par \par \uc2 From his perspective, this year's closing looks even more tragic because, although there is more movement in trade in December because Maduro "is paying more and more bonds via homeland card and that large amount of money is not backed in production," miguel Rojas, an economist at global consulting firm Consulting Group, explained to La Prensa.\par \par \uc2 The worst part of the move is paid by the population who are "stressed Monday through Friday for buying on the market before the black dollar cots at nine in the morning and one in the afternoon."\par \par \uc2 And that scouring, in its view, will deepen because "if the government decides to raise wages in December and continues with the same trend of issuing bonds, the parallel exchange rate could reach two million bolivars in the last month of the year," he said\par \par \uc2 Catastrophe in sight A "flexibilization of quarantine by December" will not prevent a monetary catastrophe in Venezuela's economy, says Marquina, who states that "nothing will improve in the last month of the year, because there is no incentive for the private sector to invest and increase its production, and hyperinflation will continue for many more months."\par \par \uc2 For the MP "until in Venezuela there is no government capable of building trust, establishing fiscal discipline, this parallel exchange rate rise will continue, the public deficit cannot continue to be financed with the issuance of inorganic money. The future consequences will be the same, greater hyperinflation and economic destabilization," he said.\par \par \uc2 Since the Finance Committee of the National Assembly, it estimates that in the last year the economy has contracted by 70%.\par \par \uc2 To think with that number about a new monetary conversion is absurd. He notes that "nothing will solve the loss of the value of the bolivar. Two conversions have already been made and the result is that within a few months those new banknotes in the monetary cone lose their value."\par \par \uc2 The only thing that would change the nefarious course is a "fiscal discipline, call it a devalued bolivar, call it a converted budget, call it a dollar budget. If the government spends more than it does, the same thing is always going to happen," but is it possible?\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201127egbr007pu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
