\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina and Venezuela: what they agree on and what they don't\b0\par\par\uc2 1,034 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The escalation of financial and political dynamics makes the comparison with Venezuela appear more and more in the consultations we receive.\par \par \uc2 Both economies were favored by the jump in commodity prices during the first decade of the '00s and both maximized short-term growth through unsofiable income distribution. Both brought public spending to GDP above 40% and both forced a wage improvement well above their productivity. Both collided when the fall in commodity prices toppled exports, much more aggressively in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 However, there are significant differences in the functioning of the economy and politics that do not extrapolate dynamics. Nor the possibility of living for more than seven years with an exchange gap well above 100% by decoupling domestic prices of the economy as happened between 2007 and 2014. Let alone the feasibility of sustaining "governance" in the face of a drift of the economy with sustained hyperinflation that in two years added five zeros to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In relation to the economy, the productive structure is very different. Venezuela is a food-importing country, and fuel exporter, produced by the state-owned company (\uc2 PDVSA\uc2 ). That is, the export dollars went directly to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the tax was financed directly from the company's profit transfers. The resulting company's decapitalization collapsed oil production, which until 2014 was offset by 19th crude oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Fuels historically accounted for 90% of Venezuelan exports and 50% of tax revenues. In Argentina, the export structure is significantly more diversified. The state "appropriates" part of exports via withholdings and taxes, and settlement is also mandatory within certain timeframes, but production is private. This is a minor difference in answering the question regarding the viability of the exchange rate gap at levels above 100%.\par \par \uc2 In the previous trap, with an average gap of 45%, one sojero producer took half the dollar price, the other half being withholdings and gap. With the 120% exchange gap and current withholdings a sojero producer takes a third of the price in marginal dollars, one-third are withholdings and the rest is a gap. Above these levels the settlement is slowed down and the BCRA loses reserves. It's not an exchange rate competitiveness problem, it's a gap problem.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 Venezuela is an economy that has coexisted with a very rigid trap since 2003 and a exchange rate gap above 100% since 2007 when it loses the fiscal surplus (180% of the exchange rate gap in 2009 and 2010) and a decoupling of domestic price dynamics that remained until 2014.\par \par \uc2 As of 2012 inflation stood at around 20/30% per annum and in 2013 and 2014, inflation stood at 58% and 68% respectively with an exchange rate gap of 474% and 1300% respectively. It is only in 2014 that the moderate inflation regime began to become one of high inflation with a price increase rate of 184%. Three years later Venezuela enters hyperinflation\par \par \uc2 The escalation of the gap in Venezuela occurs while the official exchange rate barely moved. There was a 100% jump in the official dollar in mid-2009 with the international financial crisis (the preferential for food imports remained at 2.15 bolivars and the official increased to 4.3). There was a second jump in 2015 (food import increased 50% to 6.3 bolivars, the officer jumped 200% to 12, and there was one free without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that by then was quoted toalmost 1000 bolivars).\par \par \uc2 Until 2015, the Treasury took advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  gap, placing dollar debt that was exchanged for bolivars through SITME at an exchange rate 50% higher than the official one. This scheme was supplied by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars that held dollar debt prices.\par \par \uc2 This decoupling of inflation from the exchange rate gap observed in Venezuela came against a back of high monetization of a primary deficit that started in 2007 and stood above 7% of GDP from 2009 with a 30% peak in GDP in 2018. Also with a very high current account surplus until 2008, and it was reduced to a level between 0 and 2% until 2014. In 2015, with the collapse in the price of crude oil, exports were cut in half, and the current account went into deficit. At this point it was when the scheme collapsed and Venezuela went to hyperinflation.\par \par \uc2 In relation to politics, while Argentina has alternation, \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  had an authoritarian regime that, after Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez's death, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the ballot box. The regime stands with the Army behind it and an interruption of the democratic system.\par \par \uc2 It is implausible to think that a government is holding up to such an economic dynamic here. The drift in Venezuela includes a 67% fall in GDP since 2004 and a price dynamic that has been inflation of more than 50% per month for three years.\par \par \uc2 In other words, there is no room to live in Argentina with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gap at these levels for a long time without inflationary consequences even with current account surpluses. In the previous trap, the gap never exceeded 80% and on average remained at 45% even with external deficits, but the expectation of political change in 2015 and the feasibility of opening the capital account to recompose reserves; and fundamentally the sale of BCRA futures during 2015 allowed local prices to be decoupled.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, it is unworkable to think of a scenario of monetary disruption that lasts in time without political consequences. The problem is that politics in Argentina reacts after the fire.\par \par \uc2 This crisis was avoidable. Managing the run since 2018 by squandering reserves, non-transition after the sung outcome of THE PASO, the nine-month delay in debt settlement paying with reserves, the pandemic and the handling of an eternal quarantine in a country without credit and without \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  complicated everything.\par \par \uc2 But still, the scope for stabilizing after debt settlement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  willing to sit at the table to negotiate in a liquid world remained. The fiscal signal and escalation of politics, open fights over exchangeless unfolding, and an attempt to tie the trap with wire ended up kicking the board.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201016egaf001zy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
