\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 So Victor Vargas' BOD swindled Venezuelans for millions of dollars\b0\par\par\uc2 1,863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The various institutions of the Chavista banker inside and outside \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities. Photomontage: PanAm Post.The Western Discount Bank (BOD), chaired by tycoon Victor Vargas, better known as Hugo Chavez's banker, is embroiled in a million-dollar scam scandal where the bank decided to attract clients, sell them dollars and subsequently prevent or complicate them from withdrawing their money.\par \par \uc2 Among the hundreds of victims of the million-dollar scam, there are Venezuelans who in the face of the crisis in the South American country decided to invest all their savings, relying on a bank that seemed serious; and there are those who decided to accept third-party payments with dollar deposits held by the bank.\par \par \uc2 To understand the BOD environment, it should be remembered that the financial institution has subsidiaries in different countries. There is the AllBank in Panama, the BOI Bank in Antigua and Barbuda, the Banco del Orinoco in Curacao and Bancam\u233\'00\'E9rica in the Dominican Republic.\par \par \uc2 #Antigua Regulatory Commission imposes special measures on #boibank belonging to the #bod group after #BancodelOrinoco \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pic.twitter.com/IfxZlpFyL5 #AllBank\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Gil & Rosas (@GilRosas_Ve) September 21, 2019\par \par \uc2 AllBank was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on 12 September by the financial authorities of Panama; the Orinoco Bank in Curacao faces a judicial settlement process after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of that country found "serious irregularities". For its part, the Financial Services Regulatory Commission of Antigua issued a number of administrative measures derogators from the BOI Bank; while Bancam\u233\'00\'E9rica is currently going through a difficult period after the secretary and chairman of the Audit Committee resigned.\par \par \uc2 Letter from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Curacao to the employees of the Bank of Orinoco (First Report)The court's judgment in the case of the Banco del Orinoco on 5 September would be linked to the above-mentioned scam against clients of that financial institution; however, the bank decided to close and decree its liquidation.\par \par \uc2 "The court's measure was given two weeks before the bank decided to close, they claimed they had no way to pay us for the sanctions," a witness victim of the millionaire scam that the bank owes at least $2 million owed the bank told PanAm Post.\par \par \uc2 "We initiated the lawsuit in Curacao and when we went to court, the court ruled in our favor and gave the bank two weeks to pay the debt; however the bank decided to close and now we have to wait and see if they are going to fulfill the payment," he said.\par \par \uc2 Venezuelan journalist Casto Ocando published on its web portal Primer Informe, a circular issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Curacao and San Maarten dated September 27 in which the financial institution addressed the employees and customers of the Bando del Orinoco to tell them in a nutshell that "there is nofunds"."Unfortunately, the Bank has so far been unable to locate any substantial good in the BDO. This means that there are currently not enough funds to satisfy BDO creditors, or to pay any salary and/or benefits for employees. As a result, the Bank does not currently have the capacity to pay any wages for this month," reads in the missive.\par \par \uc2 The journalist noted through his social networks that, in addition, the Curacao Prosecutor's Office "studies opening a criminal investigation and filing charges for alleged fraud against the directive of Banco del Orinoco NV, among them V\u237\'00\'EDctor Vargas, Luis Alfonso Borb\u243\'00\'F3n, Santos Alonso and Joel Santos".\par \par \uc2 Sources: Fiscal\u237\'00\'EDa de Curazao studies to open criminal investigation and bring charges of alleged fraud against the directive of Banco del Orinoco NV, among them V\u237\'00\'EDctor Vargas, Luis Alfonso Borb\u243\'00\'F3n, Santos Alonso and Joel Santos.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Casto Ocando (@cocando) September 30, 2019\par \par \uc2 The modus operandi of the scamTo describe how the Western Baco Discount (BOD) operated, PanAm Post relied on direct testimonies from victims, complaints via social media and press reports. He also contacted the administrators of the "BOD Scams" \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @estafadosbod.\par \par \uc2 The Western Discount Bank in Venezuela started the acquisition of customers 8 years ago to sell them dollars at a preferential price, either with the aim of saving or doing some commercial transactions.\par \par \uc2 The complaints, made through social networks, indicate that at least 20,000 Venezuelan customers were scammed by the bank.\u160\'00\'A0The sum of the blocked funds is $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 In 2018 a group of entrepreneurs reported in Caracas that the BOD kept their savings hijacked and that, when they tried to withdraw them, the bank employees informed them that they were blocked because they had been invested by the financial institution.\par \par \uc2 According to a TV News report, one of the defense lawyers for the case, Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Luis Alcal\u225\'00\'E1, noted that "the bank has many difficulties, because they used investors' money to buy bonds from the national government debt, bonds that plummeted market and have failed to recover," says they "lost."\par \par \uc2 The idea was for customers to buy dollars in bolivars and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be deposited into accounts of their subsidiary banks. Some would have their money in Curacao, others in Panama, Antigua or the Dominican Republic.\par \par \uc2 Some customers were offered certificates of deposits, and others were given account numbers; the offer seemed really tempting as they could have an interest rate between 4.0% and 5.5% annualized on the condition that the money be kept there for a renewable 6-month term. If he did not wish to extend the time limit, he or she must inform you in writing about 10 days before the expiration date.\par \par \uc2 Usually these clients, in the case of Banco del Orinoco for example, waited for the 6 months to request that the funds be transferred to other accounts; however, they began to notice that the financial institution began to make excuses for them by preventing each saver from making their respective transfers or withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 There he allegedly initiated the BOD strategy, in many cases when clients sent the notification, the bank ignored it and then said that time had been met to somehow unlock the funds; in other cases customers began to notice "technical failures".\par \par \uc2 TestimoniesVictims who are considered scammed by the bank tell PanAm Post that on all occasions they triedwithdraw your money or make transfers to access the dollars, the bank set about making excuses or slowing down the process.Most witnesses prefer to remain anonymous because the bank has not yet paid them their money; they hope not to retaliate when making the complaint.\par \par \uc2 "We fell into a misleading offer, deposited our savings trying to safeguard our assets because of the situation in Venezuela. They did an excellent lobby for us to turn in our funds and we did. We even sold some properties to do so," one of the scammed Venezuelans told PanAm Post that as it still expects the bank to pay it, it preferred to remain anonymous; he also claims to have received some threats and extortion to stop him from reporting.\par \par \uc2 "It was at the end of 2016 when I moved my capital and my family's for that bank, there I put my savings of 50 years of work and now we are practically with nothing waiting for the BOD to give me my money," he said.\par \par \uc2 In their testimony, the victims of the scam pointed out that at first, when it came to attracting customers, the bank was very diligent and interested in serving them; yet everything changed when they began to claim their assets.\par \par \uc2 "The first two months were when we saw a good development with the bank, but then it got complicated because we started to see technical problems at the transfer level. My money was in Curacao at The Orinoco Bank, and I stumbled upon the surprise that at least 60 people (almost 40 were Venezuelan) had the same problem," the witness said as he traveled to the island to try to get his money back.\par \par \uc2 "Our savings are hijacked, we don't know what status they're in, in my case it's a million two hundred thousand dollars that the Orinoco Bank has withheld.\u160\'00\'A0The first excuses were that there were problems with the Caracas Stock Exchanges, after we had to wait. The answers are short and banal," he said.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Castellanos is another Venezuelan who settled in Bogota and who agreed to give his name to the PanAm Post because the bank paid him after his complaint was made public.\par \par \uc2 'I understand that the scam started in 2012, but I in 2014 sold all the properties I had in Venezuela, at the time I had 24 million bolivars and I did not know what to do with that money or where to deposit it and the BOD offered to open a deposit account in Antigu a. They were very kind, they had immense courtesy and my son and I opened two accounts," he said.\par \par \uc2 "They sold certificates of deposits with maturity of 60 or 90 days, and if you wanted to withdraw the money at the end of that period, you had to send a notification within 10 days of the deadline, so that then the money would be transferred to a checking account and make the transfer i.e. In sending the notification, the bank responded with a "received" and said that they would soon send a form that in some cases arrived, and in another cases was late when the date had expired," he added.\par \par \uc2 "From Panama we came to live in Colombia and I had a family medical emergency and I called the bank because I needed the money and the BOD replied that what I needed it for. The form came to me two weeks later in English and with some tiny letters. Already in 2016 I decided to go to Maracaibo, the city of the bank's headquarters and there they promised me that they would make me a transfer of 30 thousand dollars and give me a visa credit card. The transfer was not made, the card was given to me at two or three months and after two months they suspended it. After making the public complaint and waiting so hard, I finally got the bank to call me and pay me," he said.\par \par \uc2 Thesituation is complicated by the name of the BOD being the banker Victor Vargas, who in addition to these allegations would be involved in dark million-dollar plots at the international level; which is why BOD whistleblowers have serious doubts that they probably won't be able to get their money back.The power of journalism. The importance of truth.Your contribution makes it all possible!When we started the PanAm Post to try to bring the truth about Latin America to the rest of the world, we knew it would be a big challenge. But we were rewarded for the incredible amount of support and feedback from readers that made us grow and improve.\par \par \uc2 Be part of the mission to spread the truth! Help us fight attempts to silence dissident voices and contribute today.\par \par \uc2 Contribute today!\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191002efa200598\par }\page {\par\fs