\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Savage capitalism in Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 After several years of expropriation, hyperinflation, bankruptcies and financial collapse, what is left of Venezuela's private sector could be forgiven for running out of hope.\par \par \uc2 But entrepreneurs say the economic crisis in the South American country accelerated the movements of Nicolas Maduro's government to move away from the full socialism of his predecessor, Hugo Chavez, towards a freer market.\par \par \uc2 "As business people we wanted price freedom and a free flow of dollars for many years. Now prices are effectively released and you can pay with dollars," says a senior consumer goods executive. Venezuela "adopted a savage capitalism," he adds.\par \par \uc2 Ministers did not officially announce policy changes and were not available for interviews. But entrepreneurs say that in recent months, rules restricting hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions have not been enforced, many price controls were abandoned, imports were freed, and Venezuela's shocked economy was struck Quickly.\par \par \uc2 462121As a result, some products that were previously scarce or could not be re-appeared in stores, although their prices are out of reach of the vast majority of shoppers. Inflation fell from its stratospheric levels of more than 100,000% last year, to an expected level of several thousand percent this year.\par \par \uc2 462125"The economy stabilized to some extent," says \uc2 Dimitris Pantoulas\uc2 , an analyst and consultant in Caracas. "It was chaos, with rows everywhere and no food. But now, even though it's much more expensive, you can get whatever you want."\par \par \uc2 "There is a certain orthodoxy," said Asdr\u250\'00\'FAbal Oliveros, director of Ecoanalytics. "But he came very late and in a messy manner. The controls were not lifted, they just don't apply. This is very arbitrary."\par \par \uc2 No one suggests that Venezuela's economy, paralyzed by years of mismanagement and reeling from US sanctions, is in something close to a sustainable position. Gross Domestic Product was more than halved in a few years, in what \uc2 Ricardo Hausmann\uc2 , a former central banker, called the greatest economic collapse in the modern era other than a war or a natural disaster.\par \par \uc2 But some business leaders welcomed the greatest economic freedom in recent months, even if the reasons for the Maduro government are far from pure.\par \par \uc2 "It was not out of conviction but by necessity that the pressure of exchange rate controls was reduced," said Carlos Larraz\u225\'00\'E1bal, outgoing head of Fedec\u225\'00\'E1maras, the country's main trade association. "There is a deep recession in the productive sectors, consumption is falling dramatically, there is no funding.... credit is impossible."\par \par \uc2 To combat inflation, banks were forced to keep 100% of deposits as mandatory reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which suspended loans. Credit card limits failed to keep pace with galloping inflation, which put a brake on consumer lending. And some of the few remaining industries that still work complain that they cannot compete in production costs with cheap, unregulated imports.\par \par \uc2 "Purchasing power plummeted, so sales fell 20% from its previous level," says the owner of a manufacturing plant. "But I still have a factory ready to produce for a much larger market. So, in the short term, this situation is no better. But in the long run, it's good because there's less state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Pantoulas compared \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a warlike economy. Only "10%of the population has access to everything they want, but the other 90% live in hardship," he says.In fact, the annual fedec\u225\'00\'E1maras assembly, held this month at a university in Caracas, had a certain sense of wartime. When the speaker system did not play the national anthem during the opening session, delegates stood up and shouted of "Viva Venezuela!".\par \par \uc2 "The private sector refuses to disappear," Larraz\u225\'00\'E1bal said in a defiant speech to delegates. "This is not an option for us.... we are part of the solution, not part of the problem."\par \par \uc2 When the FT asked him in advance how long the private sector could resist, he painted a bleaker picture: "It's hard to predict... there used to be more than 12,000, almost 13,000 industrial establishments (in 1998), now there are no more than 3,000 left."\par \par \uc2 462120?\par \par \uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190805ef8500219\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
