\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina, Venezuela and the Cold War\b0\par\par\uc2 1,348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Fidel Castro\uc2  is dead. Trump is not Eisenhower or Kennedy. And Russia is not the Soviet Union. However, the conflict that revolves about, with focus on, is much like a flash of Cold War times. That context embraces and protects, at the same time, the government of. Neither the economic heterodoxies authorized by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, freezing of tariffs and some prices) nor the easy access to the main Argentinean offices of the world can be explained without taking into account what happens in the continent. In that sense, the figure of as an electoral option in Argentina did nothing but set off alarms about a possible return of populism in Latin America. And in this landscape the government's efforts in the last hours must also be inscribed to show a majority of political leaders, even the Peronists, far from the Bolivarian axis.\par \par \uc2  The world helps, but claims: political and economic rationality, whether in the figure of Macri or any other centrist leader, must stop an eventual change of political tide in the region. Maduro is still in Caracas because Russia and Cuba (and, to some extent, also Iran) pressed for things not to change in Venezuela. This is the truth, like it or not. Cuba said it had no military in Venezuela, and maybe it did not lie. But Venezuela controls the Venezuelan intelligence system better than Venezuela and has enormous intellectual influence on the military hierarchy surrounding Maduro. The Cuban regime has one of the most effective intelligence services in the world, created precisely during the vicissitudes of the Cold War. Russia provided the dictator of Caracas with his personal guard (about 100 elite forces), because he does not want to lose the 10 billion dollars Maduro owes him, nor the oil investments, nor the gold business, in which he has many interests. Russia is not the Soviet Union, in fact, but it does what the Soviets could not do: it influences crucial elections in the world through cyber attacks (Washington is still being investigated if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Trump's election). The least active in defending Maduro have been the Chinese, to whom Venezuela owes 40,000 million dollars. Beijing only wants to secure the payment of that debt, either by Maduro or any other party. That distance from the Latin American conflict confirms the theory that China is a commercial empire, that it does not aspire to be a political or ideological power. The Latin American left (including Argentina) has placed much emphasis on the participation of the United States in the fall of Maduro. That participation exists and has been practiced with a memorable awkwardness. A secretary of state (he is supposed to be the most important foreign minister in the world) can not say that he advised Maduro to operate the plane and leave Venezuela if the next day the dictator would continue strutting around Caracas, as he really did. Much less can John Bolton, the main adviser of Trump in foreign policy, tell publicly that there was an agreement with Venezuelan officials to overthrow Maduro and that they did not answer the phone at the time of the events. The North American agreements with important personages of the chavismo existed, according to the press of the United States, but nobody knows when and why they broke. Only the version that Cuba insistently pressed on the Venezuelan political and military establishment is hovering. The fall of Maduro would mean an unmitigated economic disaster for Cuba. These errors of American diplomacy take away credibility from Washington as the main political and military power in the world. They serve to let the left gloat on fire in front of their embassies in Latin America and to ignorethe Russian and Cuban participation in the Venezuelan tragedy. The decisive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Cuba in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a certainty confirmed by Venezuelan academics and intellectuals. Maduro is the exponent of a military regime. The photos after the coup that failed, after the liberation of \uc2 Leopoldo L\u243\'00\'F3pez\uc2 , are almost identical to those that the despot of North Korea tends to spread. Maduro pondered, after the frustrated coup, the loyalty of the military and did not say anything about the loyalty (if it existed) of civil society. Being a military government, it is only necessary for the Armed Forces to step aside and declare themselves dispensing with the end of Maduro's experience. The Venezuelan military does not need to resort to the traditional coup in Latin America. The implausible myopia of the left (and of the Argentine Kirchnerism) ends up supporting a military regime and closes to Venezuela any way of access to a true democracy. Maduro was elected in rigged elections, with the opposition prevented from participating and with the absolute domination of the common and electoral courts. Is that the Justice proposed by the intellectuals and some leaders of Kirchnerism? That ninguneo to the institutions of democracy is what explains the adhesion of the Kirchner to the regime of Maduro? Macri, in turn, initiated in 2015 a turn of Latin America towards more centrist and rational governments than those that formed the Bolivarian axis. Then \uc2 Sebasti\u225\'00\'E1n Pi\u241\'00\'F1era\uc2  arrived at the Chilean government; Jair Bolsonaro, the Brazilian, and Iv\u225\'00\'E1n Duque, the Colombian. The only exception was \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  in Mexico, who does everything possible to avoid fighting with Trump. But \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  created a system of government with features very similar to populist, especially for their ways of understanding democratic institutions and their relationship with journalism. The Mexican exception also creates Washington's strategic need for no other exception in Argentina. Macri harvests Venezuelan turbulence outside his country, although also within Argentina the Venezuelan tragedy may remind Argentines that populisms end badly, especially when they have no money. And the economic bonanza that the Kirchners enjoyed during a good part of their three consecutive terms is gone. The guarantee that Macri means is necessary but not sufficient. It was found in recent days when markets reacted very badly after polls were released that gave \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  an electoral advantage over the current Argentine president. The possibility of an agreement with the most rational Peronism then arose to demonstrate that political extremism can be isolated. The Government suspects that the transcended on a document with ten crucial points to guarantee the present and future governance came out of Sergio Massa. Massa says, in turn, that the government is to blame. Massa was one of the first to use for his own benefit -when not- the transcended: he rejected the document that, perhaps, he himself contributed to disseminate ahead of time. Massismo also would have slipped the possibility of a formal act with the signature of all non-Kirchner leaders of Peronism, including most of the governors. That is unlikely, if not impossible. The elections are close and the Peronist governors do not want to openly confront Cristina in their territories. Be that as it may, the Government read that, after the leaks, anything other than the formal act of signing the document would be a failure for the ruling party. Macri went out to play personally and called some of Peronism's referents. The purpose is to publicly display them to those who say no to an agreement of simple ideas, most of which should be in the program of anysensible ruler. Good sense is scarce when many feel close to the presidential chair. The government's most important aspiration now is to extract some statements from governors, legislators and Peronist leaders, in which they show their agreement in full or in part with the document. Entrepreneurs, both industrialists and rural producers, have manifested massively in favor of the ideas of that draft. The policy is more petty, as it was found in emblematic leaders of Peronism. Argentina is, meanwhile, in the midst of a complicated rebalancing of international balances. A game too sophisticated for these poor ambitions.\par \par \uc2 Copyright Grupo de Diarios Am\u233\'00\'E9rica - GDA / La Naci\u243\'00\'F3n / Argentina\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190507ef56002oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
