\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The thousand and one ways of living without light or water in Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 1,808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By the time you read this, surely millions of people in Venezuela continue without hydropower for six days. The tragedy that is lived in that country is understood only, perhaps, by someone who suffers it or has lived something similar a while ago.\par \par \uc2 They are six days without light or water, in much of the country. Without Internet and without metro in Caracas, the main means of transport of the capital used by almost three million people, according to the Ministry of Transport. From Thursday until Monday morning, electric service was almost nil throughout the country. The Internet measurement observatory, Netblocks, published on Saturday that 96% of Venezuela was without this service, after the blackout.\par \par \uc2 The less fortunate saw how their relatives died, as did Elizabeth Diaz, a resident of Valencia, the third most important city in the country. Her 19-year-old daughter suffered from cerebral palsy, and weighed 10 kilos of chronic malnutrition. D\u237\'00\'EDaz narrated a local journalist who, after suffering a medical emergency last Saturday, decided to take her to two public outpatient clinics, but they did not attend her for lack of electricity. Then the city police transferred them to the Central Hospital in Valencia, where the young woman died in the arms of her mother.\par \par \uc2 See more: Why did Migraci\u243\'00\'F3n Colombia not allow Maduro's cousin to enter the country?\par \par \uc2 Entire Venezuela was dark and desolate day and night for three continuous days. The oil country, in just five days, lost 26 confirmed lives, so far, due to the blackout, according to the National Survey of Hospitals. This caused, in addition, by the nonexistence of power plants in 50% of the public hospitals throughout the nation. However, \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2 , president sworn in by the National Assembly and recognized by more than 60 countries, said on Sunday that there may be between 40 and 60 fatalities, unofficially, because before the national blackout, communications in the oil nation have Been complicated and have not been able to confirm more.\par \par \uc2 For everyday life, power plants were transformed into the salvation of those who have them in their homes, but also on the right hand of the media, some private companies, telephone companies and even hotels. During those three days without electricity, there was no way to pay, in a nation where cash is scarce and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is devalued by more than 96%, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (BCV). Where the dat\u225\'00\'E1fonos, called "point of sale" in the South American country, did not work because there was almost no telephone signal. What caused that in specific areas of two of the three highways of the city, these became the meeting point of dozens of people, seeking coverage to communicate.\par \par \uc2 Given this, part of the more than 2.3 million Venezuelans abroad, according to the \uc2 UN\uc2 , when they heard the news about the blackout, quickly entered into despair because they could not contact their family and friends. While on the other hand, Venezuelans in that nation, around 30 million inhabitants, searched during the day to resolve their situation in the face of the crisis. Many left their homes early, for two or three days, to pharmacies and supermarkets that were open, to buy mainly non-perishable food, as well as ice and salt in order to keep their food in good condition. Before this, the dollar bills became an escape to chaos. Mainly, for those who fortunately have foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 See more: Spain offered help in Venezuela's energy crisis\par \par \uc2 However, there were more stores, supermarkets, gas stations and pharmacies that had to close,because there was no electricity. In addition to the fear of their owners before possible looting, events that occurred in a store in Caracas and in Maracaibo, Zulia state, in a shopping center and different stores. They only opened those businesses that had an electric plant.\par \par \uc2 The car and the charger to use in this became the best ally of those who have them. And, although many did not have the same luck, in their cars they could listen to some of the radio stations, which managed to be on the air. "It was hard for us to communicate. There was no access to mobile data. I could not stand this level of misinformation anymore. I did not know anything, or anyone and it was driving me crazy, "said Carlos Rodriguez, 28, from Caracas.\par \par \uc2 The reason for the electrical failure that generated such a crisis in an oil country, was partially known, about an hour and a half after almost the entire Venezuela went off last Thursday afternoon. \uc2 Luis Motta Dom\u237\'00\'EDnguez\uc2 , Minister of Electric Energy and president of Corpoelec, reported that this situation was due to "sabotage", an argument that was later repeated by \uc2 Jorge Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez\uc2 , Minister of Communication, who also added that it was the fault of the "American empire" and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , American senator. However, a day later Rodriguez said it was "a cyber attack by the United States."\par \par \uc2 See more: Diplomatic blackout in Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 During the day, and the partial restoration in some areas and not in others, the streets of the urbanizations and neighborhoods, neighbors were seen to go down to talk among themselves, while the younger ones carried out activities that perhaps because of the existence of the Internet and the technology have been lost in this new century. Several took advantage of their days also to read and even to play board games with the family, without knowing for almost three days what was happening specifically.\par \par \uc2 "This is for a while, we must prepare," said several citizens. Many lost food, because ice and salt were not enough. Others cooked on grills inside or outside their homes, because they do not have a gas stove, but an electric one. "On Friday night we resolved dinner with bread and cheese. The next day, the situation continued, our breakfast remained bread and cheese, fortunately we had. Then we went to buy charcoal, to use a small barbecue in the apartment to solve the cooking of meat, make arepas and coffee. We swallowed smoke, but we managed to change the menu, "said Carmen Diaz, 50.\par \par \uc2 However, the hours without light kept happening and with it the desperation increased. At night, citizens played pots in protest and shouted slogans against Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro. On Saturday, thousands went out to protest all over the country, due to the failures in the electric service, in a demonstration called by \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But it was on Sunday that the president of Parliament, together with deputies to the National Assembly, and the engineer Jos\u233\'00\'E9 de Viana, former president of Hidrocapital (public water utility company in Caracas) and \uc2 Movilnet\uc2  (mobile phone company), assured that the situation was generated by an incident in the interconnection system in the high voltage networks, which transport electricity from the generating plant, to the cities located in the interior and west of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 See more: In \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  years ago there was a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 "An accident of these is terrible and therefore is avoided. There are 2000 kilometers to be maintained and taken care of (pruning and cutting) very well because it has to be without vegetation, "said De Viana. Statement that resembles sources linked to \uc2 Corpoelec\uc2 , who according to Guaid\u243\'00\'F3, confirmed that a vegetation fire on Thursday afternoon affected thethree lines of 765 kilovolts between El Guri - the largest reservoir in Venezuela, which feeds more than 80% of the country's electricity - and the Malena and San Ger\u243\'00\'F3nimo B substations\par \par \uc2 Given this, in Caracas today electric service has returned almost entirely, but not in the surrounding areas. Many homes in the capital have not had electricity for six days, although in the interior of the country, the situation is more tragic. At least 12 Venezuelan states continue without these basic services, including the long lines to get gasoline throughout Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 If there is no light, there is no water During these last six days the water service has also been affected. If the Venezuelan already had daily rationing, imposed by zones, for years now, now nobody receives it.\par \par \uc2 In Caracas, after the light was partially restored after three days in much of the city, the scenes have taken a turn. Now, the situation is to search according to economic possibilities, the way to get water.\par \par \uc2 On the Boyac\u225\'00\'E1 avenue (cota thousand), similar to the Bogota circumvallation, the image of citizens drinking water from El \u193\'00\'C1vila, a mountain located north of the capital, is repeated daily. Entire families get off their cars with pots and containers of different sizes to make long lines and get the liquid. While others come walking, to join that group. The same thing happens on the Francisco Fajardo highway, the main road that connects the entire city, where the Guaire River is located nearby, a well-known waterway, and where people from Caracas go to get a water pipeline that flows into it.\par \par \uc2 See more: The day that Venezuela went out\par \par \uc2 In the case of people with greater purchasing power, they cancel the tank truck service, which in the face of the crisis they charge in dollars in cash. While in the interior of the country, there are images of some Venezuelans who are bathing in sewers. "In the coming hours we will be gradually reestablishing the supply of drinking water in all the big cities of the country (...) I also announce the activation of the 'Blue Tank' plan to provide water reserve tanks to all the homes of the country, "said Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro, on national television on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 However, for Jos\u233\'00\'E9 De Viana, an engineer and specialist in the area, he indicated that the water supply could normalize in seven days "after the Caracas metro starts operating again. When that happens they will know that the electrical system was restored, "he said.\par \par \uc2 See more: Venezuela blames the US UU of the worst blackout in its history\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Tarek El Aissami, attorney general, appointed by the National Constituent Assembly, announced on Tuesday that they were opening a new investigation against \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2 , for his alleged involvement in the "electrical sabotage." While Maduro also designated that day a International Commission, with the support of China, Russia and the \uc2 UN\uc2 , "to investigate thoroughly what happened and establish responsibilities, the world must know the full truth about the attack, high technology," he said on national television.\par \par \uc2 For their part, Venezuelans continue to be dismayed, some unbelievers, about the situation they lived for more than three days. "I never thought to live something like that. I knew that outside of Caracas these things happened, but I never thought that in the capital and for so many days, "said Laura De Freitas, 21 years old.\par \par \uc2 Experts deny Maduro and explain what caused the blackout\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190314ef3d0009a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
