\par\par \b \uc2 Venezuela's Maduro loosens private sector,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  red tape-sources\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2019\par 00:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CARACAS, Jan 31 (Reuters) - As Venezuela grapples with U.S. sanctions on its vital oil industry, socialist President \uc2 Nicolas Maduro\uc2  has made plans to lift some price and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in a bid to revive the country's battered economy, sources said.\par \par \uc2 Since 2014, Maduro's government has directly imported and distributed raw material for industries like food processing and construction. But the government has now informed some companies in private meetings that they can buy their own foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to import what they need, five industrial and financial industry sources said.\par \par \uc2 Until now they have had to go through the government's complex system of exchange controls to obtain scarce foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But business leaders said the shift may have limited impact with Venezuela deep in hyperinflation after five years of recession.\par \par \uc2 TOO LATE\par \par \uc2 "This has come too late," said Jorge Botti, a former leader of Venezuela's largest industry group, Fedecamaras. "Even the most optimistic people, who for months held out for a shift in economic policy, are now betting on a change in government."\par \par \uc2 Venezuela's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that starting in February, banks would be able to sell euros to private companies in "strategic sectors."\par \par \uc2 The banks would be assigned euros each Monday depending on how much local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they had deposited in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coffers.\par \par \uc2 The change comes as Maduro is facing the boldest challenge since taking office in 2013. Opposition leader \uc2 Juan Guaido\uc2  has claimed the presidency, arguing that Maduro's May 2018 re-election was fraudulent. He has won support from the United States and many Latin American countries.\par \par \uc2 The United States imposed sanctions this week that will likely halt exports of crude oil from state oil company \uc2 PDVSA\uc2  to U.S. refineries, cutting off a key source of the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  nation's revenue in order to pressure Maduro into leaving office and allowing Guaido to call elections.\par \par \uc2 Years of heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have prompted several multinational companies to abandon \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The walk back of regulations comes as the government has allowed the bolivar to depreciate to the parallel rate, after years of controls created a black market for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The changes come after sanctions from the United States and \uc2 European Union\uc2  have made it difficult for the government to move money abroad since banks are wary of dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela's official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system has offered very little hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the private sector since 2015, after a collapse in oil prices left government coffers dry.\par \par \uc2 Maduro blames the economic crisis on private companies, which he accuses of conspiring with the domestic opposition and the United States to wage an "economic war."\par \par \uc2 One of the people with knowledge of the policy changes said companies with export capacity would be the only ones that would be able to buy euros from banks.\par \par \uc2 The government would also make its system of price controls, which are intended to contain inflation but have instead contributed to shortages of basic goods, more "flexible" to allow prices to rise in line with the bolivar's depreciation, the sources said. ($1 = 0.8740 euro) (Writing by Luc Cohen Editing by Phil Berlowitz) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-2-1T00:20:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190131ef1v04pr5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
