\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2017\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:36 ET - Venezuela President Maduro's decision to rewrite the constitution is pushing the country's political crisis to the brink "into an all-or-nothing stand-off, from which a stable transition or exit looks increasingly difficult," Teneo Intelligence says. The consultancy boils Maduro's strategy down into three main points: to avoid elections, divide the opposition, and try to weaken international resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "This is a hazardous strategy," Teneo notes because it will radicalize the anti-government protest movement, increasing the risk of serious violent disorder. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com; @kejalvyas)\par \par \uc2 11:35 ET - Foreign investors bought a net $20.6B in emerging-market assets in April, a slowdown from inflows of about $30B in both February and March, IIF data shows. Investors have poured into emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stocks and bonds this year, as worries over higher US interest rates diminished and growth in developing economies picked up. Last month, appetite for riskier assets was dented by the French election and escalating geopolitical tensions with North Korea. Still, flows into EM assets have been positive for five straight months. Developing Asian economies received roughly 80% of April's inflows, IIF data shows. Foreign investors bought a net $13.8B in debt and only $6.8B in equities. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:21 ET - With Venezuela's opposition pledging to ramp up anti-government demonstrations, there is a risk of the protest movement reaching a breaking point, overcoming the capacity of security forces to contain them for several consecutive weeks, says risk consultancy \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2 . So far, Venezuela's armed forces has provided almost unconditional support to President Maduro, but \uc2 IHS\uc2  warns that that loyalty could wither in the face of wider spread looting and an increasing death toll. Another scenario, \uc2 IHS\uc2  says, is that the military convinces Maduro to step down and call for elections later this year instead of 2018, something that the leader has thus far ruled out. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1515 GMT - Greek economic sentiment grew to 94.9 points in April, from 93.4 in March and 90.7 points a year ago, Greek think-tank IOBE said in its monthly report. Business expectations improved in the services and commerce sectors were stable in manufacturing but deteriorated in the construction sector. Greek consumer confidence remained at very low levels, but ended a three-month decline, due to expectations for a swift conclusion of the a second bailout review. (nektaria.stamouli@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:11 ET - Venezuela's opposition begins what it says is a new, intensified wave of street demonstrations against President Nicolas Maduro after the leftist leader's decree to rewrite the country's constitution. His rivals call Maduro's move a nuclear option to cling onto power in the oil-rich country and avoid elections that polls show his ruling Socialist Party would easily lose. Protesters are blocking traffic in the capital Caracas and major cities around Venezuela, facing tear gas and National Guard armored vehicles. At least 29 people have died in violent clashes over a month of protests but the opposition says it will remain on the streets until Maduro allows for general elections and the chance to vote on the presidency. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:56 ET - The urgency for the Fed to raise short-term interest rates has diminished, though June remains "a decent bet with a 70% market-assigned possibility," says Eric Lascelles, chief economist at RBC Global Asset Management. "This week's [Fed] announcement should be fairly sleepy, lacking in pomp with no press conference, no updated dot plots or new growth forecast," Lascelles says. A "slightly softer" statement is likely, given recent macro data showing lower inflation, a lower index of US manufacturing activity, weak 1Q GDP growth and soft March payroll numbers, he says. (Daisy.Maxey@wsj.com; @DaisyMaxey)\par \par \uc2 10:37 ET - The bond market rebounds after several large automakers--including \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Ford\uc2  (F)--report weak sales, another red flag for US growth momentum. Many economists still expect growth to rebound in 2Q following the soft patch in 1Q. The initial GDPNow model on May 1 forecast 4.3% for 2Q. But the model also expected a stronger 1Q earlier this year before revising estimates lower. "If you haven't figured this out by now, I am always skeptical of surges and unless everyone with a buck starts shopping today, I think that 2.0 to 2.5% growth in the 2nd quarter is more appropriate and is more likely," says Kevin Giddis,, head of fixed-income capital markets at Raymond James. The 10-year yield is 2.322% vs. 2.327% Mon. (min.zeng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1431 GMT - \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects the Danish debt management office to revise lower its government bond issuance target for 2017 by DKK10 billion from the current target of DKK65 billion when the next update is due in June. Chief analyst and head of fixed income research, Arne Lohmann Rasmussen says that \uc2 Danske\uc2  in general likes Danish "risk" but given the most recent tightening of Danish bond yields to Germany and to swaps, and also given the somewhat lackluster demand at recent Danish government bond auctions, he only recommends cautious bidding at Wednesday's bond auction. The Danish DMO will tap November 2021- and November 2027-dated bonds at Wednesday's auction. (emese.bartha@wsj.com ; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1419 GMT - A confluence of factors including "boringly good" macroeconomic indicators around the world and consistent tones from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  have helped drive down volatility across equity markets and sent the VIX to its lowest in a decade, according to \uc2 Larry Hatheway\uc2 , chief economist at GAM. People who went long equity volatility last year as a way to try to improve portfolio performance against things like political risk also found it didn't work very well, he adds, while ultra low returns on bonds keep stocks attractive in comparison, driving investors to buy on any dips in the market. (riva.gold@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:19 ET - Deutsche Bank economist Torsten Slock says in a note that accelerating house prices in major urban areas is calling into to question the current outlook for US monetary policy. He writes, "it doesn't look like Fed hikes are having any impact on slowing down the housing market. Given the unbroken uptrend observed in home prices does it make sense for the Fed to be slow and gradual?" (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1409 GMT - With the benefit of hindsight, now that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is pretty much done buying corporate bonds, one can claim that the program's positive impact was felt before the corporate bond purchase scheme was even announced. The market was quick to price in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  after the Brexit referendum, given that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also started its own credit QE program. This pushed sterling corporate bond spreads lower even before the BOE announced its credit QE scheme in early August. After all, "markets always trade ahead," says Andreas Michalitsianos, portfolio manager at JPMorgan Asset Management. Actual purchases began in September and the BOE almost hit the scheme's GBP10 billion ceiling last week. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 1402 GMT - The investor-survey based Sentix Euro Breakup index shows that the fear of Frexit is fast becoming a memory, but it's rather late: the bond market made that clear immediately after Emmanuel Macron won the first round of the presidential election. Spreads between 10-year French and German bond yields shrank to 42.3 bps from 65 bps last Monday, right after the first round. Four days later, the Sentix index indicated that the probability of a French exit from the eurozone is at its lowest since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victory at the U.S. presidential election. Mr. Macron is facing far-right candidate Marine Le Pen in a run-off this weekend. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 02, 2017 11:36 ET (15:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170502ed52002lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
