\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2017\par 17:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:35 ET - The sanctions against Venezuela's vice president, Tareck El Aissami, will only strengthen his loyalty to President Maduro and the administration's strategy of frustrating the electoral process, says Teneo Intelligence. That's what has already happened with several other Venezuelan politicians and military officials who have been slapped with drug charges in recent years. In general, Teneo says, US sanctions have tended to benefit Maduro, allowing him to decry US interference and rally radical supporters of his ruling Socialist Party. While the president's popularity is down amid a punishing economic crisis, the nationalist rhetoric still carries weight among many poor and the military. It also allows him to back his claims of a coup plot against his government led by the US and his political rivals. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com; @kejalvyas)\par \par \uc2 11:07 ET - \uc2 US government\uc2  this week sanctioned Venezuela's vice president for drug trafficking allegations and President Trump followed up by tweeting support for a political prisoner and nemesis of President Nicolas Maduro. While there have been rumblings of a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance from the US towards \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nomura says it's still too early to tell whether Trump will be any tougher than Obama. "Not much political capital is necessary to criticize Maduro," Nomura says, "however there is no obvious motivation to adopt a more aggressive approach considering that Venezuela is not an imminent threat to the US." The measure may weigh on the logistics of the vice president's job but Maduro's response so far has been tepid. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 ET - A combination of Kraft Heinz (KHC) and \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UL) could require a massive amount of debt issuance, but the early reaction of the bond market suggests that it could be manageable. Kraft's 3% notes due 2026 last traded with a 137bps spread to Treasurys, just 7bps wider than its last trade Thursday, according to MarketAxess. That implies that investors think a combined company would retain KHC's low triple-B ratings, enabling it to the access the investment-grade bond market where appetite for new debt has been nearly insatiable. A total of $177B of investment-grade corporate bonds were issued last month, the highest January total on record, according to Dealogic. (sam.goldfarb@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1603 GMT - The relationship between Euro corporate bonds' spreads to interest rate swaps and spreads to \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds appears broken, making a "mockery of people's hedges," say analysts at Citi. They see spreads to swaps as "cleaner," given that it is less exposed to distortions in the bund market. However, the iTraxx Europe index of investment grade corporate credit default swaps has de-correlated from euro corporate bonds' swap spreads this year, but has remained linked to spreads over bunds. This complicates the hedging of credit risk in corporate bond portfolios with the CDS index. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 1542 GMT - The tight gap between euro bond spreads of high yield corporate bonds and their investment grade counterparts reveal a "concerning" picture, according to \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  strategist Christian Weber. Taking a CDS basis-derived mispricing of around 20 bps in investment grade credits, the high yield-IG spread ratio stands at 4.1 times - near its tightest level since the global financial crisis. Each time it reached such levels since 2008, it widened significantly, Dr. Weber adds. Euro high yield bonds have had a strong performance year-to-date, pushing yields on the Bank of America Merrill Lynch Euro High Yield index to all-time lows of 3.69%, according to Factset. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 10:24 ET - Investors are bidding up IHeartMedia (IHRT) securities, wagering on the media company's next move in managing a $20B debt stack. Texas-based IHRT has been taking steps to reduce and extend its debts, including exchanging $738M in debt last week into longer-dated bonds. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts wrote that IHRT's next move "will likely entail a larger effort" to create a more sustainable debt structure. Investors sent prices on a 14% bond maturing in 2021 to 40 cents on the dollar Thursday, a 12% price jump in a week, according to FactSet. A refinancing or exchange offer to extend IHRT's debt maturities would be a boon for investors whose holdings are targeted. IHRT took on much of its debt in a boom-era buyout by \uc2 Thomas H. Lee Partners\uc2  and \uc2 Bain Capital\uc2 . The company has struggled as streaming music and satellite radio providers compete for advertising dollars. IHRT shares fall 4.3% to $1.10. (andrew.scurria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1514 GMT - \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  recommends a trade that seems counter-intuitive at first glance: selling a type of French government debt to bet on far-right candidate Marine Le Pen losing the French presidential elections. The Dutch bank suggests selling French government bonds that carry mandatory collective action clauses, or Model CAC, while buying bonds without such legal provisions in short and mid-dated maturities. Some experts claim that a super-majority of investors need to consent to any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes in Model CAC bonds. This would protect these bonds from redenomination, justifying a price premium over non-CAC issues. But if Le Pen loses, the risk of French government bond redenomination will all but evaporate, reducing the need for extra protection.(tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 9:52 ET - The head of Brazil's state run giant bank, \uc2 Banco do Brasil SA\uc2 , \uc2 Paulo Caffarelli\uc2 , said the bank is concentrated in its digital channels to increase profitability and reduce costs. Caffarelli said, in a teleconference with analysts, that the bank plans raise its profit levels to those of its non-state peers in the next few years. \uc2 Banco do Brasil\uc2  share fal 1.2% to BRL32.50 ($10.51), while the main local stock index, the Ibovespa, was down 0.63%. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1428 GMT - M&A is another component of the growing risk profile of European corporate bonds, says LBBW in a note to investors Friday, distributed just as news emerge on Kraft Heinz eyeing a takeover of \uc2 Unilever\uc2 . The regional bank gives the examples of \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 's interest in GM's Opel subsidiary and private equity appetite for \uc2 Stada Arzneimittel\uc2 . Recent activity shows companies are more "offense-minded," but \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  corporate bond purchases generally masks M&A risks for now, adds LBBW. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 1412 GMT - The macro backdrop in early 2017 has been very supportive of reflation strategy in fixed income, says Chris Iggo, chief investment officer for fixed income at \uc2 AXA Investment\uc2  Managers. This has favored exposure to credit, especially high yield, and to inflation-linked securities at the expense of conventional government bonds, he adds. For example, the U.S. inflation-linked bond index has outperformed the equivalent Treasury index by 64 basis points, while in the U.K. has been a massive 2.9%, he says. In credit, all core credit indices have outperformed their respective government bond curves, even with significant new issuance activity, especially in the U.S., Mr. Iggo says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com ; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 9:01 ET - Treasurys have strengthened as investors respond to reports of possible cooperation between two leftist candidates in France's presidential election. The concern for investors is that a united candidacy could hurt the centrist candidate Emmanuel Macron and help the far right leader Marine Le Pen, who has threatened to pull France out of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Treasurys snapped a five-day losing streak Thursday despite the release of more solid economic data, ensuring yields remain range-bound amid political uncertainty on both sides of the Atlantic. The 10-year yield was recently 2.409% vs. 2.450% Thursday. (sam.goldfarb@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1359 GMT - Cash flow will be supportive of eurozone government bonds next week, with redemptions from Austria, Germany and France that are likely to be reinvested on those market, say Mizuho rates strategists. Elsewhere, Citi rates strategists calculate redemptions of EUR9.9 billion from Austria on Feb. 20, EUR16 billion from Germany on Feb. 24 and EUR16.2 billion from France on Feb. 25. Next week's scheduled supply is very limited with around EUR300 million from Slovakia, EUR1 billion from Germany and an estimated, and yet-to-be announced, EUR2.5 billion to EUR3 billion from Italy. Given the very low scheduled offer, Mizuho says Austria might add a new 10-year bond via a syndicate. (emese.bartha@wsj.com ; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 17, 2017 11:35 ET (16:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170217ed2h002bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
