\par\par \b \uc2 Venezuelan Bonds Down After Presidential Cabinet Change -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2014\par 17:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:40 EDT - Venezuelan bond investors are not thrilled by President Maduro's cabinet changes, especially the moving of \uc2 Rafael Ramirez\uc2 , formerly the top oil and economy czar, to foreign minister. Ramirez had been seen as one of the most pragmatic voices in the socialist government, pushing for reforms and expected to conduct an NYC investor roadshow this month. But last night Maduro made no mention of reforms like simplifying the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and hiking fuel prices, which may disappoint investors, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. Venezuela's benchmark 2027 bond has dropped by more than four points in the last two days to levels last seen in April while yields on some \uc2 PDVSA\uc2  bonds are rising to nearly 15%. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:24 EDT - The Brazilian national treasury is planning to raise at least $500M from the reopen of its 2025 dollar-denominated bonds, according to a person close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  who declined to be named. The treasury is taking advantage of the end of the summer lull and is looking to pay an annual yield in the area of 4%, says the same person. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , \uc2 BTG Pactual\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  are coordinating the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1514 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The ECB will eventually be forced to adopt full-blown quantitative easing involving large-scale outright purchases of government bonds, says Capital Economics. While the latest inflation data have brought the timing of that announcement forward, the ECB might could prefer to wait gauge the impact of the Targeted Longer-Term Refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  before making an official announcement. "But at the very least, President Draghi is likely to use his press conference to stress the Bank's willingness to do more if required," says Capital Economics's Melanie Debono. Adds this message could be reinforced by a token cut in official interest rates or by the announcement of a start date for the programme of QE targeted at private sector asset-backed securities. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:00 EDT - MBS have taken back ground against Treasurys this week as benchmark 10-year Treasury yields bounce off recent lows. Higher yields have reversed some of the recent drop in 30-year mortgage rates, calming MBS investors with positions vulnerable to faster refinancing. Plus, the lower mortgage rates of the past week only resulted in a 1.4% increase in the MBA's refinance index, leaving it bouncing at low levels, according to FTN. Reflecting MBS strength, the spread between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fannie Mae\f28 \uc2 \f2  benchmark MBS rates and the 10-year Treasury yield has dropped below 70 bps for the first time since July 16, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  data show. (albert.yoon@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:58 EDT - Any negative reaction to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision to trim its floor-space expansion this year to 4.4% from 5.2% is more than offset by August sales numbers, which show same-store sales in Mexico up 3.5% from a year earlier, the biggest increase since March 2013. Total sales in Mexico and Central America rose 7.2% to $2.8B. "We continue to think that Walmex self-service formats are outperforming the rest of the industry (as has been the case YTD), and that Sam's is still months away from a significant turn-around," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Walmex V shares recently up 0.9% in Mexico City at 35.55 pesos ($2.72). (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:57 EDT - One rare noticeable addition in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  statement is the more-upbeat comments on Canada's export sector. But Laurentian's Sebastian Lavoie notes that story isn't enough to sway the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking on its next rate move--which is widely expected to be a hike sometime next year. "The so-called rotation in the engines of growth has just started," says Lavoie. For things to continue, he notes Canada must see its export momentum carry forward while increased business capital spending kickstarts a stalling labor market. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:54 EDT - The overall tone of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate statement today is slightly less dovish and more firmly neutral, according to BMO Capital Markets chief economist Douglas Porter. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  abandoned reference to "serial disappointment" with global growth, the statement didn't say closing the output gap "hinges critically" on stronger exports and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped reference to soft landing for housing, he notes. "All these omissions suggest a slightly less dovish stance," Porter says. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1454 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Greece is planning a 7-year bond issue for later this year, as well as the issuance of 12- to 24 months notes, says a senior Greek official, something that would more or less complete the rebuilding of its yield curve. Earlier this year, Greece issued its first 5-year and 3-year bonds following a four year hiatus from the markets. Although the amounts raised were small compared with Greece's overall debt burden--and the interest rate on the bonds are more pricey than the country's bailout packages--the issues have helped create benchmarks for subsequent corporate bond issues. That in turn is helping finance the country's economic recovery, say analysts. (alkman.granitsas@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:52 EDT - Most South American banking-sector outlooks remain negative at mid-year, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Outlooks remain negative for Argentina, Brazil, Ecuador and Venezuela, with positive outlooks only in Costa Rica and Uruguay, according to a new Fitch report." The negative sector outlook for Ecuador, Argentina and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Venezuela\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reflects concern that weaker economies and increasing government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could hinder real credit expansion and the banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," according to Larisa Arteaga, Fitch director for Latin American financial Institutions. For Brazil, main problems are the sluggish economy and recent loan increase of Brazilian federal-owned banks, Fitch adds. (luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:45 EDT - Regulators give big US banks a slight reprieve today with a rule aimed at making sure the firms have enough cash in a crunch. On issues including the treatment of municipal bonds and deposits, regulators made changes banks had asked for -- or promised they would consider changes later. At a time when banks have been facing continually tougher rules, the moves show regulators aren't totally immune to banks' arguments -- a positive harbinger for future rules coming down the pike. (ryan.tracy@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:39 EDT - US regulators will for the first time require banks to hold a minimum amount of safe assets that they can use for cash in a credit crunch like the one that occurred in 2008. The rule is just one of many firsts headed banks' way. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials says they are working on at least three other rules impacting banks' liquidity, targeting short-term wholesale funding and other issues. The rules are worth watching because they will reshape the way Wall Street manages its cash, making firms more stable in regulators' eyes but also potentially increasing their costs. (ryan.tracy@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:35 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' Bricklin Dwyer notes that little has changed since the last \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  monetary policy report. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's view of the domestic economy was largely in line with prior forecasts, risks to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's view remain balanced and it continues to maintain a neutral policy bias. Dwyer adds that without growth accelerating above 2% and inflation expectations well-grounded along with subdued wages, there shouldn't be any new changes from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  for quite some time. Dwyer continues to see the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  moving its policy rate sometime after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  gets the ball rolling first. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com, @itsdgc)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 03, 2014 11:40 ET (15:40 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140903ea93002gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
