\par\par \b \uc2 Latin America Fares Poorly As Place to Do Business, Says EIU -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2014\par 17:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1502 GMT [Dow Jones]--Latin America fares poorly as a place to do business, according to an Economist Intelligence Unit index published Wednesday. Argentina (70th place), Ecuador (71st), Cuba (78th) and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Venezuela\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (82nd) "continue to share a tendency for unpredictable state \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionism\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that has undermined economic performance and discouraged foreign investment," the EIU says. Chile stands apart, located in 13th place, on a par with developed economies in North America, Europe and Asia, EIU says. To a lesser extent, Mexico (32nd) and Costa Rica (40th), also outperform in the region, it says. (matthew.cowley@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 11:00 EDT - Many economists expect the ECB next month to slash its deposit rate from zero to negative in a bid to discourage saving and spark lending. Yet Robert Sinche, global strategist at \uc2 Pierpont Securities\uc2 , argues that a cut to negative 0.1%-0.25% is unlikely to be effective in easing monetary conditions or encouraging a significant increase in bank lending. He notes data out earlier today showed another sharp drop in the ECB Deposit Facility, with deposits down to EUR17.5B, the lowest since July 2011. "With deposits at such a small level, even if the negative deposit rate encouraged all banks to turn excess deposits into loans, the impact on aggregate lending and the economy would be tiny." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1458 GMT [Dow Jones]--The biggest risk faced by emerging markets at the moment is an earlier than expected rate hike by the U.S. Federal Reserve, says BBH, which has a significantly bullish view on the asset class. The first hike is currently forecasted for the second half of 2015. The Fed will eventually start to tighten and this will likely disrupt global markets, but "we continue to think that EM has several quarters ahead of relatively smooth sailing," says Win Thin, EM strategist at the firm. Other risks include negative headlines risk from Russia and China. "Our bullish EM call is predicated on the assumption that the Ukraine crisis will fade and that China does not experience a hard landing with systemic risks," says Mr. Thin. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1456 GMT [Dow Jones] Spain's 2.75% April 2019 and 3.80% April 2024 bonds, both at auction on Thursday, have cheapened "considerably" versus swaps over the past few sessions, reaching record cheap levels since late March, says Newedge economist Annalisa Piazza. Such a movement, coupled with spread widening versus core euro-zone debt, "might be seen as a potential buying opportunity for the Spanish debt this week," she adds. Cross-country, Spain has underperformed the core euro-zone debt since early May but showed some outperformance versus Italy that seems to have more potential political troubles going into the EU elections, she adds. "Given our baseline scenario of a smooth outcome of the latter, we suspect Spain will regain ground versus euro-zone core debt starting from next week but it might lag behind Italy in the 'recovery' path," she adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1450 GMT [Dow Jones]--The \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's survey for May records a third straight rise in consumer confidence, so we know that the Russia/Ukraine crisis isn't much of a worry for many Europeans. At minus 7.1, the measure is now back to where it was in Oct 2007, prior to the financial crisis. But consumer spending hasn't been anywhere near as strong as that would suggest, and that may have something to do with the low rate of inflation, which makes people feel both a little richer, and less inclined to buy now. A pickup in spending may be on the way, but that's unlikely to stand in the way of ECB action in June. (paul.hannon@wsj.com, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @PaulHannon29)\par \par \uc2 1440 GMT [Dow Jones]--The small upside surprise in Brazil's May mid-month inflation doesn't change the good season of inflation prints, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . May IPCA-15 increased by 0.58% m/m and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says the number "poses downside risks to our May IPCA forecast (currently 0.40%) and, moreover, should lead the BCB to pause the tightening cycle next week." The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  has been hinting heavily that it would like to pause a year-long cycle that has seen the key rate lifted to 11% per year. Next meeting is set for May 27-28. (matthew.cowley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:32 EDT - Michael Feroli, chief US economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , says NY Fed Dudley "already previewed all the interesting discussions" that might appear in the minutes due at 2pm. Investors will zero in on clues about the long-run equilibrium fed funds rate; when the Fed intends to cease reinvesting principal payments of its securities holdings; and details to how the Fed intends to employ its new reverse repo facility. "We think there's a good chance each of these topics will be addressed in the minutes," he says, adding that "the sense of the Committee as a whole may not be too different from Dudley's views." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:27 EDT - NY Fed President Dudley turns his focus to his region's economy, noting New York City's "robust" recovery and steady growth across Northern New Jersey, until this past winter. The topic of today's briefing focused on the types of jobs that have been created in the NY Fed's region. Dudley notes that as is typical during periods of economic recovery, a "good number" of higher- and lower-skill jobs have been created, while middle-skilled work has continued to shrink. That includes posts in construction, machine \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and administrative support. "Many middle-skilled workers displaced during the recession are likely to find that their old jobs will never come back." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:15 EDT - \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  thinks there's scope for CAD to become the outperformer in the commodity bloc, especially as positioning in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still short. Firm is relatively positive on CAD's commodity-bloc cousin AUD in the medium-term, but believes extended long positioning in AUD is vulnerable to recent macroeconomic disappointment in Australia and a reassessment of \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  policy expectations. It's recommending a short AUD/CAD position at current level of 1.0070, targeting a move to 0.9700, the pair's Feb. 4 low, with a stop loss at 1.0215. BNP also recommended a short position in EUR/CAD. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1404 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  is already running a short EUR/CAD position, and now adds a short AUD/CAD recommendation. While being positive towards AUD medium-term, the bank says the changing macro dynamics could weigh in the near-term. CAD, on the other hand, has still further to rally, given that market short positioning is still sizable. BNP sells AUD/CAD at 1.0070, targeting 0.97 with a stop up at 1.0215. The cross printed a fresh two-month low of 1.0053 earlier Wednesday. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1355 GMT [Dow Jones]--The April reading of the EMU consumer confidence release came in at -8.6, the highest since October 2007 and a sharp improvement on the -9.3 print seen in March, notes \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . While not quite matching that degree of improvement, the bank expects the flash May reading to come in at -8.0. EUR/USD looks like it could do with some upbeat data, currently trading at 1.3660, around 0.5% lower on the day. Data due for release at 1400 GMT. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:54 EDT - After earlier losses, the greenback jumps up 0.2% against the yen, to Y101.36 from Y101.11, following a small uptick in US yields, says Brad Bechtel, managing director at Faros Trading. The pair's 200-day moving average of around 101.20 continues to offer support. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  showed no signs at its meeting overnight that it was ready to shift policy until it has a clearer picture of the effects of last month's sales tax increase, keeping the yen strong overall. But with investors still expecting the BOJ to provide more stimulus eventually, the dollar has erased all the day's losses against the yen. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 21, 2014 11:02 ET (15:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140521ea5l003h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
