\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Venezuela Chavez Aims To 'Attack'  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Black Market\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2010\par 01:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 9 Jan 2010 19:42 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Use \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Fight \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Black Market\par \par \uc2 9 Jan 2010 19:43 EDT *DJ  Venezuela's Chavez Cites Weak USD Amid Reasons For Devaluation\par \par \uc2 9 Jan 2010 20:07 EDT DJ  Venezuela's Chavez Aims To Fight \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Black Market\par \par \uc2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--Venezuela plans to battle the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  black market with "heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," President Hugo Chavez said Saturday, one day after announcing a devaluation of the bolivar.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to attack the parallel market with heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Chavez said during a televised address.\par \par \uc2 In announcing the devaluation Friday, Chavez indicated the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the thriving and legally murky black market, but provided few details.\par \par \uc2 The black market rate currently stands at about VEF6.25 for $1.\par \par \uc2 Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could entail flooding the market with dollars to weaken the greenback and bring down the rate, perhaps until it reaches the official VEF4.3 rate. That could lessen the black market's relevance.\par \par \uc2 The president of this oil-rich nation also on Saturday cited a "weak" U.S. dollar against international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as one of the reasons for his decision, announced Friday night, to cut in half the official value of Venezuela's bolivar against the greenback, to 4.3 bolivars per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Every day our barrel of oil is worth less because of the weak dollar, because oil is priced in dollars," Chavez said.\par \par \uc2 The devaluation will help offset this, Chavez said, because now every time state oil company \uc2 Petroleos de Venezuela\uc2 , or \uc2 PdVSA\uc2 , goes to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change its dollars for bolivars, it will get VEF4.3 per dollar rather than VEF2.15.\par \par \uc2 "This will have a huge impact on the national fiscal situation, and will strengthen our oil industry," Chavez said.\par \par \uc2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 58-212-284-5651; dan.molinski@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 9 Jan 2010 20:35 EDT =DJ  UPDATE:Venezuela's Chavez Aims To Fight \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Black Market\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Dan Molinski \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--Venezuela plans to battle the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  black market with "heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," President Hugo Chavez said Saturday, one day after he announced a devaluation of the bolivar's official rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're going to attack the parallel market with heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Chavez said during a televised address.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In announcing the devaluation Friday, Chavez indicated the government would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the thriving and legally murky black market, but provided few details.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The black-market bolivar rate currently stands at about VEF6.25 for $1. On Friday night, Chavez announced he was cutting in half the official value of the bolivar against the dollar, to VEF4.3 per dollar from VEF2.15 previously.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the devaluation, receiving the official rate will still require government approval that has been hard to obtain, so it is doubtful the black market will disappear overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the black market could entail flooding it with dollars to weaken the greenback and bring down the rate, perhaps until it reaches the official VEF4.3 per dollar level. That could lessen the black market's relevance, and in the meantime would allow the government to get more bolivars for its dollars than it would get at the official rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The president of this oil-rich nation also on Saturday cited a "weak" U.S. dollar against international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as one of the reasons for his decision to devalue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Every day our barrel of oil is worth less because of the weak dollar, because oil is priced in dollars," Chavez said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The devaluation will help offset this, Chavez said, because now every time state oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroleos de Venezuela\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or \f28 \uc2 \f2 PdVSA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , goes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to change its dollars for bolivars, it will get VEF4.3 per dollar rather than VEF2.15.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This will have a huge impact on the national fiscal situation, and will strengthen our oil industry," Chavez said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said the devaluation will spur national production and reduce imports. "We've been selling dollars cheaply for too long."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to the VEF4.3 official rate, another, secondary rate of VEF2.60 will be available, with authorization, for those buying key "necessary" imports such as basic foods and medicine that Venezuela doesn't produce enough of.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 58-212-284-5651; dan.molinski@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 09, 2010 20:35 ET (01:35 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100110e61a0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw181\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2715\pichgoal600
