\par\par \b \uc2 Moody's\uc2  concludes review of Venezuelan bank ratings; downgrades deposit ratings; confirms Banvenez BFSR\b0\par\par\uc2 1,814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2009\par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009   \par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service downgraded both the long term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rating of Mercantil, C.A., Banco Universal (Mercantil) to Ba3, from Ba2, and that of \uc2 Banco de Venezuela\uc2  (Banvenez) to Ba2, from Ba1. The outlook is stable for both these ratings. At the same time, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  confirmed Banvenez's bank financial strength rating at D-, with a negative outlook.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit ratings is driven by the rating agency's reassessment of a government's ability to support its banks during a protracted, systemic event, such as during the ongoing global crisis. The likelihood of such support is an important part of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  credit analysis, and it provides uplift to deposit ratings above the floor implied by a banks' own financial strength. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rating downgrades bring both banks' ratings closer in line with that of the Venezuelan government's own B1 local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond rating. These rating actions conclude the review for possible downgrade of the deposit ratings initiated on July 1, 2009.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  noted that the rating action on Mercantil's deposits does not reflect a change in the bank's intrinsic financial strength. In fact, on July 1, 2009, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  had affirmed Mercantil's D- bank financial strength rating and its Ba3 unsupported baseline credit assessment to reflect the adequacy of the bank's balance sheet. The bank financial strength rating measures a bank's intrinsic safety and soundness and, as such, excludes external credit-support elements. Mercantil's Ba3 deposit local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating benefits from no uplift due to systemic support.\par \par \uc2 The confirmation of Banvenez's D- bank financial strength rating reflects \uc2 Moody's\uc2  view of its potentially enhanced importance in the system, which could result from its intended role as a consolidator of other Venezuelan public sector banks, under the newly created Corporaci\u243\'00\'F3n de la Banca P\u250\'00\'FAblica.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  believes Banvenez's new role is also likely to result in changes in the bank's risk management and thus its asset allocation, as greater emphasis is placed on executing public policy at the expense of risk-adjusted returns. Over the medium term Bavenez's new public policy role may have a negative effect on the bank's financial metrics, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said - particularly asset quality, profitability and capitalization - which it assesses in its analysis of a bank's intrinsic financial strength. The rating agency therefore has assigned a negative outlook to Banvenez's financial strength rating, indicating that it would monitor the bank's financial fundamentals closely during the next several quarters.\par \par \uc2 Banvenez's new Ba2 deposit rating reflects full systemic support for the bank's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations as a government-owned bank, which adds one notch of uplift to the bank's unsupported baseline credit assessment.\par \par \uc2 Deposit Rating Changes Reflect Revised Approach to Systemic Support\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has refined its assessment to better reflect the capacity of governments to support their banking systems as a whole in the event of a systemic crisis. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  continues to believe that most governments are likely to support their banking systems during a crisis to avoid a meltdown of the local payment system.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the agency also views the capacity of a country and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the nation's banks to be more closely aligned with the government's own creditworthiness, especially in times of crisis like these. (The revised approach to systemic support is outlined in the special report entitled "Financial Crisis More Closely Aligns Bank Credit Risk and Government Ratings in Non-Aaa Countries," published in May 2009.)\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said that the new systemic support indicator for Venezuela has been established at Ba2, thus replacing the current Baa1 local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit ceiling. Despite the downgrade, the Ba2 systemic support indicator remains two notches above the government's B1 local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond rating.\par \par \uc2 The two-notch differential reflects \uc2 Moody's\uc2  assessment of the Venezuelan government's high willingness and ability to support its banking system. This attitude is demonstrated by the following: (i) a history of no local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit defaults in the past 20 years; (ii) the small size of the banking system in relation to GDP - a very modest 19%; (iii) the virtual lack of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations by banks, given that foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending or deposit-taking is not allowed in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; and iv) a very high level of central government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bank industry, which suggests a high likelihood that the government will continue to support the banking system as a whole.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  also pointed out that Venezuelan banks have for some time successfully managed their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a highly unpredictable legal, political, and economic environment. Continued high inflation, coupled with negative economic growth, will cause lower business volumes and declining asset quality, thus continuing to stress the banking system's performance.\par \par \uc2 Previous Rating Actions and Principal Methodologies\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  last rating action on Mercantil and Banvenez was on July 1, 2009. At this time, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit ratings of both banks on review for possible downgrade. At the same time, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  affirmed Mercantil's D- bank financial strength rating with a stable outlook and placed Banvenez's D- bank financial strength rating on review for possible downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The principal methodologies used in rating these banks were "Bank Financial Strength Ratings: Global Methodology" (February 2007) and "Incorporation of Joint-Default Analysis into \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Bank Ratings: A Refined Methodology" (March 2007) and available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.moodys.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.moodys.com}}}\uc2  in the Rating Methodologies sub-directory under the Research & Ratings tab. Other methodologies and factors that may have been considered in the process of rating this issuer can also be found in the Rating Methodologies sub-directory on \uc2 Moody's\uc2  website.\par \par \uc2 The following ratings were affected:\par \par \uc2 Mercantil, C.A., Banco Universal\par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rating downgraded to Ba3, from Ba2\par \par \uc2 Banco de Venezuela\par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rating downgraded to Ba2, from Ba1\par \par \uc2 Bank financial strength rating of D-, confirmed with a negative outlook\par \par \uc2 CREDIT RATINGS ARE MIS'S CURRENT OPINIONS OF THE RELATIVE FUTURE CREDIT RISK OF ENTITIES, CREDIT COMMITMENTS, OR DEBT OR DEBT-LIKE SECURITIES. MIS DEFINES CREDIT RISK AS THE RI