\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ Venezuela Law May Push  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To Play Bond,  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Darcy Crowe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2009\par 22:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be gearing up to become a major player in the market for government-backed dollar-denominated bonds.\par \par \uc2 The institution could become an enforcer for the government's plans to tame a booming unofficial market for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while allowing the administration of President Hugo Chavez to continue its massive debt issues next year.\par \par \uc2 A new law approved by the National Assembly allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy and sell bonds issued by state oil company \uc2 Petroleos de Venezuela\uc2 , or \uc2 PdVSA\uc2 , in the primary or secondary market, giving it a new weapon to influence the price of Venezuelan bonds and the unofficial \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate.\par \par \uc2 The new rules come as the government is ramping up the sale of dollar-denominated bonds to plug its budget, which is hurting from a decline in oil revenue, and to manipulate the country's unofficial rate for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It recently sold a total of $11.26 billion, including debt sold by \uc2 PdVSA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bond issues help quench Venezuelans' thirst for dollars. The securities are bought with bolivars and can then be sold abroad for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , providing a backdoor alternative to the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -exchange mechanisms.\par \par \uc2 Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be able to buy those bonds back from overseas investors and sell them again to Venezuelans to mop up more bolivars.\par \par \uc2 If this is the case, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "could buy around $6 billion in \uc2 PdVSA\uc2  paper," said Alejandro Grisanti, an analyst with \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help increase demand for the bonds.\par \par \uc2 Although the final text hasn't yet been published, it seems the bill wouldn't allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tally the dollar-linked bonds as part of its international reserves.\par \par \uc2 The changes "create a potential time-bomb within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Russ Dallen, head trader at Caracas-based brokerage firm BBO Financial Services. The new law "could create a huge liquidity crunch at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if and when an economic downturn occurs," he added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Nelson Merentes didn't returns calls seeking comment. In an interview published Monday with a local newspaper El Nacional, he said the new law expands the ability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the primary and secondary debt markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a rate of VEF2.15 and residents or companies that can't buy dollars at that rate must turn to the unofficial market.\par \par \uc2 Merentes said the government's goal is to bridge the gap between the peg and the unofficial rate to 60%, meaning that a dollar should fetch no more than VEF3.55 in the parallel market. The sale of dollar-denominated bonds has already helped the government to lower the parallel rate by 35%, Merentes said in the report.\par \par \uc2 The unofficial rate on Tuesday weakened against the dollar to VEF5.35 as many investors await the chance to examine the details of the new law and discover the true scope of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new role.\par \par \uc2 Purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the secondary market could also help the performance of the bonds, which have been pummeled by what analysts see as a supply glut by the increased bond sales.\par \par \uc2 "This will have an effect on prices" for Venezuelan bonds, said Asdrubal Oliveros, a director at Caracas-based economic research firm Ecoanalitica. "They want to influence the parallel rate," he said. Purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help increase demand for the bonds, he said.\par \par \uc2 The state-run oil company has issued $6.2 billion this year and may issue as much as $5 billion more before the end of the year, according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . On Tuesday, Oil Minister Rafael Ramirez told reporters that \uc2 PdVSA\uc2  was considering selling debt in the short term.\par \par \uc2 The bond sales are expected to continue next year. Barclay's Grisanti expects \uc2 PdVSA\uc2  to sell $6 billion next year, while the central government could issue as much as $10 billion.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 PdVSA\uc2 , the bond sales not only allow the oil company to satisfy pent-up demand for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also to squeeze more bolivars from every dollar of oil revenue. \uc2 PdVSA\uc2  by law has to sell its dollar income to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the official rate while selling debt allows it to receive a higher rate.\par \par \uc2 "The government is looking to maximize its oil income without devaluing the bolivar," said Andreas Faust, an economist with Caracas-based Banco Mercantil. For \uc2 PdVSA\uc2 , which faces mounting debts with service providers, selling dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the official rate "doesn't make much business sense," he added.\par \par \uc2 -By Darcy Crowe, Dow Jones Newswires; 58-414-249-6821; darcy.crowe@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 03, 2009 16:15 ET (21:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091103e5b3006lk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
