\par\par \b \uc2 Venezuela's Increasingly Statist Economy Starts To Sputter\b0\par\par\uc2 By Darcy Crowe   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2008\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--A sharp slowing in Venezuela's economy in the first quarter suggests that output could become sluggish throughout the year and even worsen in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Despite sky-high oil prices, economists blame the government's highly \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies for the petroleum-rich country's flagging growth.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gross domestic product, the broadest measure of overall economic activity, increased an annualized 4.8% in January through March. It was the lowest figure after four years of double-digit growth.\par \par \uc2 Forecasts from four private sector economists now have the economy growing anywhere from 4% to 5.5% this year, less than the government's 6% target. And all of them see it cooling off even more next year.\par \par \uc2 "We're in for a sharp slowdown," said Orlando Ochoa, an economist with Andres Bello University in Caracas.\par \par \uc2 Stifling bureaucracy and fast-expanding regulatory regimes, including a gamut of price, interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls constrain private enterprise, which has been badly shaken by nationalizations in the energy, telecommunications, power and mining sectors. President Hugo Chavez's harsh anti-market rhetoric also hasn't helped.\par \par \uc2 The restrictions on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the U.S. dollar at 2.15 bolivars, make it difficult for companies to obtain the greenbacks for their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Price controls on several food items mean that some farmers aren't covering their production costs, leading to shortages.\par \par \uc2 Chavez's socialist rhetoric has scared off foreign investment. Foreign direct investment receded by $475 million, "despite the vast opportunities in the oil and gas sector," wrote \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' economist Alberto Ramos. "In the meantime, FDI flows into Brazil, Chile, Colombia and Peru are booming," he noted.\par \par \uc2 Some key sectors like manufacturing and construction almost came to a standstill in the first quarter after posting double-digit growth last year.\par \par \uc2 More alarming than that, said Pedro Palma, an economist with local research firm Metroeconomia, was the 12.6% contraction in the car-making industry, despite government imposed quotas on car imports that seek to boost domestic production.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan mining industry has also failed to take advantage of the worldwide commodities boom, retreating an eye-popping 20.5% in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 Economists have long warned that extremely loose monetary and fiscal policies would likely be less effective this year than in previous years, given that they have fueled a boom in consumption, but not investment - due to the administration's statist bent.\par \par \uc2 The government has been streaming record oil windfalls into social programs, said Andreas Faust, an economist with Caracas-based Banco Mercantil. "We're seeing higher consumption with very little investment," he added.\par \par \uc2 The result has been the highest official inflation in the region: April's level stood at 29.3% on year. "This is the Achilles' heel of the Venezuelan economy," said Andres Bello University's Ochoa.\par \par \uc2 Inflation could accelerate even more in coming months as the Chavez administration is likely to ramp up government spending ahead of regional elections in November.\par \par \uc2 The GDP figures released Tuesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also somewhat of an embarrassment for the government's economic team.\par \par \uc2 Planning Minister Haiman El-Troudi, an electric engineer who has inked a handful of socialist treatises, on several occasions publicly boasted that growth for the first quarter would surpass 6%. El-Troudi is the head of Chavez's economic team and also sits on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board of directors.\par \par \uc2 "The government was clearly surprised by this," said Mercantil's Faust, who added that he was baffled by El-Troudi's public predictions considering that he had access to detailed and updated economic growth data.\par \par \uc2 -By Darcy Crowe, Dow Jones Newswires; (58) 212 905 6304; darcy.crowe@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 28-05-08 2118GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080528e45s00108\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
