\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Pres: Venezuela, Argentina To Jointly Issue Bond  \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2006\par 20:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--Venezuela and Argentina plan to jointly offer a bond to Venezuelan investors, another step in the economic cooperation between both nations, Argentine President Nestor Kirchner said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 During a televised address in Venezuela, Kirchner said both nations seek to create a "bond of the South" in 60 to 90 days.  \par \par \uc2 Kirchner's announcement confirms rumors vented in the Argentine press that both neighbors seek to offer a "bi-national" bond, or that Venezuela could offer a guarantee for Argentine debt.  \par \par \uc2 A Venezuelan lawmaker confirmed the plans as well.  \par \par \uc2 Officials from both countries "are putting the final touches on a regional bond that would give investors exposure to risk from both Argentina and Venezuela," said Rodrigo Cabezas, head of the congressional finance commission.  \par \par \uc2 Cabezas couldn't provide many details about how the instrument would work or how much of it would be sold. He did say he expects the government to sell the dollar-denominated bonds to local investors in exchange for bolivars.  \par \par \uc2 It is unclear how much of the proceeds from the bond sale would go to Argentina.  \par \par \uc2 By selling a new bond to domestic buyers, the government of Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez would create a new way to impact the price of the dollar in the local black market for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without increasing domestic debt.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the unofficial market for the dollar by selling public debt denominated in dollars in exchange for bolivars.  \par \par \uc2 Investors buy these bonds and sell them overseas for much-needed U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Chavez imposed strict capital controls in early 2003 and has forced businesses and ordinary Venezuelans to seek government permission to buy dollars for various needs.  \par \par \uc2 The controls have spawned a black market for the dollar that Venezuelans follow closely to determine future costs and set prices for their products.  \par \par \uc2 His administration, however, has left a window open for investors to obtain the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by using complex stock and bond transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Chavez has made it a priority to keep inflation under control, especially this year as he seeks reelection in December.  \par \par \uc2 As of early March, Venezuela had purchased $2.5 billion in Argentine debt and sold off $1.1 billion of that amount to local banks.  \par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether the government has helped reduce liquidity by recycling the debt of its South American neighbor.  \par \par \uc2 In the fight against inflation, Chavez's government has faced difficulties keeping excess liquidity out of the economy in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 A hamstrung \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to issue a costly amount of short-term debt to soak up the bolivars in circulation resulting from heavy government spending.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry are looking for new ways to limit the amount of cash in circulation without hurting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bottom line.  \par \par \uc2 Chavez has vowed to gradually move the Venezuelan economy away from capitalism and adopt a new socialist system for the 21st Century.  \par \par \uc2 -By Raul Gallegos, Dow Jones Newswires; 58-212-564-1339; raul.gallegos@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 