\par\par \b \uc2 Venezuelan GDP Sprints As Oil Income Pumped Into Economy  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Raul Gallegos  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2006\par 20:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 CARACAS (Dow Jones)--Booming oil revenues are fueling \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high economic growth, but heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy make some critics doubt whether the good times can last over the long run.  \par \par \uc2 Planning Minister Jorge Giordani Wednesday touted the country's 9.4% annualized gross domestic product growth in the first quarter, and said more is to come.  \par \par \uc2 "Venezuela is now experiencing sustainable economic growth and has been for the last 10 quarters," he said in televised remarks from Libya, where he accompanied President Hugo Chavez on a state visit. Growth this year will exceed the 6% rise originally forecast, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Chavez himself took credit during his televised remarks, declaring, "Venezuela is experiencing solid and sustainable growth."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts don't doubt that Venezuela's economy will expand beyond 6% in 2006, given a high tide of oil revenues, aggressive government spending and loose monetary policy. Capital controls that trap liquidity in the country and interest rate controls ensure that interest rates remain low. Authorities also require banks to allocate about a third of their lending to favored sectors at sweet-heart rates.  \par \par \uc2 All the liquidity made for a broad-based economic expansion in the non-oil sector, even as Chavez focused much of his government's effort on redistributing wealth to the poor. He is seeking another six-year term in December elections.  \par \par \uc2 Fueled largely by government spending and consumption, household incomes have benefitted. Private consumption rose 19.3% year over year and government consumption was up 9.2% during the same period. Construction was also up sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Chavez's subsidies and social spending programs have given the poor more spending power, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  noted. Through off-budget programs called "missions," the Chavez administration provides health, education and subsidized food products, among other services.  \par \par \uc2 To counter the enormous inflationary pressures from the huge economic stimulus, the government has imposed price controls on scores of goods. Under Chavez the way inflation is measured has also been adjusted to include subsidized prices at the state-owned grocery chain Mercal--resulting in artificially lower readings.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the price controls, Venezuela suffers double-digit inflation.  \par \par \uc2 "Significantly loose fiscal and monetary policies are contributing decisively to leverage the non-oil economy up, although through mechanisms that are inherently unsustainable," Goldman Sachs said in a Wednesday report. "The large wealth transfer from abroad (oil revenues) through terms of trade gains has been recycled back into the economy via profligate fiscal spending."  \par \par \uc2 Venezuela has a history of boom and bust cycles dependent on oil, with little saving and varied populist measures. Detractors claim Chavez is more of the same, and will face tough times in a few years when the fortunes of the oil market turn.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, as social spending has increased in recent years, investment in the oil sector has dwindled.  \par \par \uc2 Oil activity declined 0.2% year on year during the first quarter, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data show. Government oil production declined 0.3% during the quarter while private production was down 4.5%, according to a UBS Investment Research report. Analysts pointed out that first-quarter foreign direct investment numbers showed a $1.6 billion outflow, reflecting private capital outflows given the government's continual, unilateral contract changes in its quest to garner more rent from oil companies.  \par \par \uc2 Some observers caution that the lack of investment and saving could hurt the country's economic viability in the long run. But others point out oil prices will likely stay high for years.  \par \par \uc2 "This government has a considerable degree of freedom. Stress scenarios show government spending is sustainable for another three to four years at least," said Luis Zambrano Sequin, an economics professor for the Andres Bello Catholic University.  \par \par \uc2 Zambrano points out the government can easily continue to tap local banks for cash when needed, and can also resort to weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally so it can convert dollars from oil sales into more bolivars.  \par \par \uc2 Chavez has said he will continue to spend heavily in hopes of creating a new socialism for the 21st Century in Venezuela. He has vowed to remain in politics until 2030.  \par \par \uc2 -By Raul Gallegos, Dow Jones Newswires; 58-212-564-1339; raul.gallegos@dowjones.com [ 18-05-06 1819GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060518e25i0012c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
