\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Venezuela eyes controls on commercial bank rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2003\par 23:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CARACAS, Venezuela, Feb 16 (Reuters) - Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez on Sunday proposed introducing controls on commercial bank lending rates for the first time in seven years as the embattled leader looked to tighten his grip on the private sector. \par \par \uc2  The proposal, if approved by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would follow the government's creation of new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and price controls in the past month. Those controls have closed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and forced retailers to sell everything from tomatoes to funeral services at government-set levels. \par \par \uc2  Explaining the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in commercial rates, Chavez complained \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's poor were not offered affordable credit. He said commercial bank lending rates should fall by about 10 percentage points and be set at 30 percent or less. \par \par \uc2  "I would fix lending rates at a maximum of 30 percent," Chavez said. \par \par \uc2  "I want rates to be fixed. I want interest rates to be cut and I want the BCV (Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to fix interest rates," Chavez said during his state-sponsored Sunday television program "Alo Presidente." \par \par \uc2  Venezuela's economy is suffocating under a grueling strike by oil workers against Chavez's government. Oil shipments from the world's fifth largest exporting nation have fallen dramatically since the strike began in December. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks angered the populist president in December by slashing operating hours to support the strike meant to force Chavez from power. Most of the private sector has reopened. But the strike continues in Venezuela's vital oil industry, strangling an economy already reeling from a 8.9 percent contraction last year. \par \par \uc2  Any decision on fixing rates would have to come from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is largely independent but has supported Chavez's recent initiatives - including the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and price controls which are strongly opposed by the private sector. \par \par \uc2  Opponents warned that the new price controls would further damage shopkeepers, many of whom shuttered their businesses for nearly two months as part of the general strike abandoned earlier in February. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls have also hurt the private sector, starving businesses of the much-needed U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a nation that imports more than 60 percent of its goods. \par \par \uc2  Venezuela has allowed commercial banks to set their own lending rates since 1996, after seven years of government rate-setting programs. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030216dz2g00upn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
