\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk/Bolivar -2: Only  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Modestly \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2002\par 16:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Director Domingo Maza Zavala said Wednesday that the bank won't any longer burn its foreign reserves to keep the bolivar afloat against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The bank won't sacrifice its reserves to keep defending the bolivar and that policy is a fundamental difference from the past week," Maza told the Globovision network in a televised interview. \par \par \uc2  However, Maza said that the bank won't stay completely on the sidelines if the bolivar keeps on falling at the same rate as it is doing now. "...in that case the bank would consider the possibility of the convenience to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and ease the fluctuation," Maza said, who added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil is likely to ease down after a couple of days. \par \par \uc2  Currently, the bolivar is collapsing after the government decided to scrap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system and implement a free-float exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Around 1450 GMT (9:50 a.m. EST), the bolivar sank to VEB909 a dollar traded in the over-the-counter market, according to traders in Caracas, who added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  "mildly" at VEB820. \par \par \uc2  According to another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with hefty dollar sales. In the announcement Tuesday, it wasn't clear to what amount the bank would keep supplying dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the last time it was traded, the bolivar closed at VEB792.50 to the dollar. The market was closed Monday and Tuesday for the Carnival holiday. \par \par \uc2  After spending several years trading in a band, Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez announced late Tuesday that the bolivar would freely float. He also announced a major overhaul of the $34.34 billion budget and vowed to cut government spending for 2002 by 7%. Analysts, however, have said the program lacks credibility. \par \par \uc2  Low oil prices and assorted fiscal pressures had made the band - the central parity of which the bolivar was allowed to move within a 7.5% range - untenable. \par \par \uc2  Capital flight was estimated at $9 billion to $10 billion last year, and was rapidly eroding \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves this year. Reserves now stand at $10.34 billion, not including a $6.24 billion windfall oil-revenue fund. \par \par \uc2  -By Fred Pals, Dow Jones Newswires; 58212-5641339; fred.pals@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020213dy2d000ub\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
