\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2 's Bolivar Ends Stronger As Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2001\par 19:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  CARACAS -(Dow Jones)- The Venezuelan bolivar closed slightly stronger against the dollar Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to control the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar ended at a midrate of VEB699.75, VEB0.25 stronger than Wednesday's VEB700.00 close. \par \par \uc2  "There were some offshore dollar sellers and then the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in and sold some more," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $35 million. Total market volume was estimated at some $100 million. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA and is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate ended with an average of around 5% on VEB100 billion in volume, according to dealers' estimates. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the overnight rate traded between 4% and 10%, averaging 4.5% on market volume of about VEB102 billion. \par \par \uc2  -By Jehan Senaratna; Dow Jones Newswires; 582-564-1339; jehan.senaratna@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx1p00doe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
