\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT: Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Mexico Peso Slightly Firmer \b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 1999\par 22:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Mexican peso ended slightly firmer against the dollar Tuesday. It staged a brief rally after the U.S. Federal Reserve decided to keep interest rates steady, while maintaining a neutral bias. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso (MXN) closing at a midrate of MXN9.3120 per dollar, stronger from its open of MXN9.3150 and stronger from its close of MXN9.3190 Monday. \par \par \uc2  As reported, the Federal Open Market Committee Tuesday afternoon announced it had left interest rates unchanged, while surprising financial markets by maintaining its neutral stance on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-making panel held the federal funds rate at 5.50%. \par \par \uc2  One trader said that highly illiquid market conditions and the absence of investors ahead of the holiday season prevented the Mexican peso from strengthening substantially on the FOMC news. \par \par \uc2  But another trader attributed the lack of enthusiasm to an underlying hawkishness in the FOMC statement. \par \par \uc2  In the statement, the FOMC said it remained "concerned with the possibility that over time increases in demand will continue to exceed the growth in potential supply." \par \par \uc2  "What you got here is a neutral directive with a non-neutral text," the second trader said. \par \par \uc2  Despite any positive news from the FOMC decision, Brazil's real (BRR) closed slightly weaker against the dollar as capital outflows put pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Participants said the Fed's decision to keep interest rates unchanged, with a neutral bias, was positive for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Today's losses were just a question of flows, sentiment is still positive as can be seen by the strong rally on the stock market," a local trader said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Bovespa stock exchange closed at a record high, rising 3.2%. \par \par \uc2  The real closed at a midrate of BRR1.8260 to the dollar, compared with Monday's closing of BRR1.8120. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the real fell to a low point of BRR1.8320 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took action, calling several financial institutions asking for exchange rate spreads, which put some pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  However, traders believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  Among smaller Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bolivar ended weaker as the nation continues to cope with the devastation inflicted from mudslides and flooding. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar ended at a midrate of VEB644.25 per dollar, weaker than Monday's close of VEB643.75 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  One trader at a large local bank estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about $20 million, its normal average. \par \par \uc2  Below is a summary of regional exchange rates in late trading, compared with one U.S. dollar: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                           Tuesday(close)    Monday(Close)\par Brazilian real (BRR)         1.8260             1.8120\par Mexican peso (MXN)           9.3120             9.3190\par Venezuelan bolivar (VEB)   644.2500           643.7500\par Peruvian sol (PEN)           3.4925             3.4830\par Ecuadorean sucre (ECS)  17,880.0000 *      17,785.0000\par Colombian peso (COP)      1903.0000         1,876.7000\par Chilean peso (CLP)         537.8000           536.2000\par    *1730 GMT\par    Note: The Argentine peso is fixed 1:1 against the U.S. dollar under\par a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board regime.\par    -By Angela Pruitt; (201) 938-2269;\par    angela.pruitt@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1093 1835 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvcl08kx5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
