\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2  analysts blast  \highlight22\uc2 interventionist \highlight\uc2  proposals. \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 1999\par 23:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CARACAS, July 13 (Reuters) - Venezuelan analysts and businessmen on Tuesday blasted President Hugo Chavez's plans to boost the flagging economy through major public investment schemes and a forced reduction of interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Chavez has come under heavy criticism recently for failing to act on Venezuela's worsening economic plight, concentrating instead on his controversial programme of political reforms. \par \par \uc2  But his recent announcement of a $934 million job creation programme and calls for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to set lower interest rates were greeted as a worrying sign of renewed state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the oil-based economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's the typical Keynesian investment plan which boosts the non-transferable sector of the economy, construction and generates employment, but also boosts inflation," said private economist Maxim Ross of the job-creation scheme. \par \par \uc2  The Venezuelan economy, ravaged by a slump in oil prices earlier in the year, is currently undergoing its worst recession this decade and is forecast to contract by at least 3 percent this year, according to government officials. \par \par \uc2  Unemployment has surged to 15 percent from 11 percent at the start of the year. Chavez took office in February. \par \par \uc2  In announcing the job creation plan Monday, Chavez reiterated his rejection of the "extreme, savage neo-liberal model" and insisted that "the state has an obligation... to orient the country, to be a motor that drives the economy." \par \par \uc2  Chavez, a 44-year-old former paratrooper, also warned Tuesday that interest rates might be unilaterally lowered to allow small businesses greater access to credit. \par \par \uc2  "What's lacking here is, if banks do not agree to lower interest rates, then the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set rates which are more reasonable," he said at a news conference in the western city Maracaibo. \par \par \uc2  Annualized bank lending rates currently average about 33 percent even though inflation over the next 12 months is forecast to be less than 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  But bankers and analysts said setting rates would bring more problems than benefits. \par \par \uc2  "In the past, when there have been interest rate controls, it hasn't led to a growth in lending nor has it boosted the economy," said financial analyst Jose Grasso. \par \par \uc2  Banking Council President Hector Mantellini, meanwhile, warned that a forced reduction in interest rates could have the effect of reducing the incentive for saving and thus spark a run on the local bolivar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((-Silene Ramirez and Ana Isabel Martinez, Caracas newsroom 582 505 2600, fax 582 861 3621, caracas.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7d04llo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
