\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuelan Bolivar Closes Weaker Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1998\par 19:49\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed weaker against the dollar Thursday in heavy trading despite heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  as people stocked up on dollars ahead of the Dec. 6 presidential election,traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar closed at a midrate of 574.50 bolivars per dollar, down from Wednesday's close of 573.75. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 574.00/575.00 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the general public, such as small savers and investors, were snapping up dollars Thursday in anticipation of the election as well as out of concern that the bolivar's recent weakness may be precusor to worse times ahead. \par \par \uc2  After rallying 1.9% between Sept. 25 and Nov. 19, the bolivar has weakened 1% in the last two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, corporations and banks continued their week-long movement into dollars, traders added. \par \par \uc2  Though some of the demand is seasonal, such as people planning vacations abroad and retailers paying for Christmas imports, the bulk appears to be being purchased out of concern about the election and the bolivar's outlook, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  In an effort to relieve pressure on the bolivar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, dumping dollars onto the market, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold roughly $100 milion during the course of the day. \par \par \uc2  The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold this many dollars was on Aug. 24, when it was at the end of an aggressive defense of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amidst a speculative attack. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA, is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. It sells roughly $30 million to the market in a typical day. \par \par \uc2  -Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  03-12-98 \par \par \uc2  1849GMT \par \par \uc2  (Corrected At 2355GMT) \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917duc3006uf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
