\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela bolivar off slightly, 567.25/568.50/dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1998\par 20:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CARACAS, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The Venezuelan bolivar closed slighty off at 567.25/568.50 per dollar Wednesday in a market some traders described as mostly erratic. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar had closed Tuesday at 567.50/568.50 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Offer was met by demand," one trader said, noting there was some corporate demand to pay for dividends' repatriation. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director Armando Leon, for his part, said "We felt today a major impact by companies offering dollars on the local market." \par \par \uc2  As a consequence, he told Union Radio, "The whole morning and part of the afternoon, we had the exchange rate at between one bolivar and half a bolivar below yesterday's close." \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and only "provided the necessary supply." \par \par \uc2  Leon stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had behaved "quite normally" during the past 10 days, with the political climate having no apparent bearing. Venezuelans will vote on Sunday in congressional and gubernatorial elections. \par \par \uc2  ((-Yelitza Cova, Caracas newsroom, 582 505 2600, fax 582 861 3621, caracas.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dub4055lu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
