\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuelan Bolivar Closes Weaker As Dollars Scarce \b0\par\par\uc2 227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 20:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--A lack of dollar-supply combined with good buying demand from local banks and corporations to push the bolivar lower Monday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar (VEB) closed at a midrate of VEB585.35 to the dollar, weaker than Friday's close of VEB584.60 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was VEB584.85/585.85 to the dollar, with the spreads of the major banks between VEB584.75/585.75 and VEB585.30/586.30 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There is some good buying interest for the dollar, but it seems to be short-term covering," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Following the announcement of a package of cuts and revenue-raising measures Friday by the Venezuelan government, traders said the tone of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had calmed slightly. \par \par \uc2  "This is only for the short term," said one, adding that any further bad news could sharply increase the pressure on the bolivar. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  only periodically throughout the session. It placed a mere $40 million, traders estimated. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by\par state-run oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest\par supplier of dollars to the market.\par -By Daniel Flynn; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-09-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1808GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9707oak