\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuelan Bolivar Weaker Vs Dollar At Midday \b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 17:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar was weaker against the dollar at midday Thursday in heavy trading as devaluation fears spurred strong dollar-buying, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At around 1510 GMT, the bolivar was quoted at a midrate of 574.00 bolivars per dollar, down from Wednesday's close of 573.25. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 573.50/574.00. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remained spooked after AFP-Extel News Ltd., or AFX, reported early Wednesday afternoon that an unnamed presidential spokesman had said that the government could devalue the bolivar by 17%-20%. \par \par \uc2  Though the report was later categorically denied by Planning Minister Teodoro Petkoff and Finance Minister Maritza Izaguirre, the financial market remains wary about this possibility, causing many to increase their dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "It's mostly a defensive position by clients. Most don't believe that a devaluation is going to happen soon, but they also don't want to take the chance," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  With dollar-demand up sharply, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  swung into action and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet the strong wave of dollar-buying, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold roughly $70 million to $80 million to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the late morning. On a typical day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only sell roughly $30 million to the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  Despite the strong dollar-buying, traders don't expect the dollar to push much further beyond 574.00 bolivars during the rest of the session. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to support the bolivar until devaluation fears ebb," another trader said. \par \par \uc2  -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1527GMT \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k073rb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
