\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuelan Bolivar Closes Weaker Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 20:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed weaker Thursday against the dollar in heavy trading, though heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the end of the session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar closed at a midrate of 574.50 bolivars per dollar, down from Wednesday's close of 573.25. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 574.00/575.00 bolivars. Spreads for major banks ranged from 573.75/574.75 to 575.25/575.50. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market still remained spooked after AFP-Extel News Ltd., or AFX, reported early Wednesday afternoon that an unnamed presidential spokesman had said that the government could devalue the bolivar by 17%-20%. \par \par \uc2  Though report was later categorically denied by Planning Minister Teodoro Petkoff and Finance Minister Maritza Izaguirre, it was enough to trigger wide-spread fear of a devaluation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  However, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to take some pressure off the bolivar, at least in the last hour of trading, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between $100 million to $150 million to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. It sells roughly $30 million in a typical day. \par \par \uc2  "The willingness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet dollar-demand had a definite soothing effect on the market," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Though the market appeared calmer at the close, traders expect to see continued pressure on the bolivar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People are going to continue buying dollars like crazy until things\par calm down next week. We'll probably see the dollar rally to around\par 576.50 bolivars on Friday," another trader said.\par    -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1813GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k073wp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
