\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuelan Bolivar Slightly Weaker In Thin Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 18:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar weakened against the dollar midday Monday in thin trading as the local market continued its downward trajectory against a backdrop of budgetary concerns. \par \par \uc2  The devaluation of the Russian ruble Monday had no immediate effect on the Venezuelan market, but traders suggested it was still too early to say. \par \par \uc2  "The market has yet to react," said one trader. "It has been extremely quiet this morning. There is no (dollar) demand." \par \par \uc2  At 1400 GMT, the bolivar was at a midrate of 571.50 to the dollar, compared with Friday's close of 570.50. The average bank spread was 571.00/572.00 bolivars to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the morning to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It sold an estimated $20 million during the morning, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-run oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  -By Daniel Flynn; 582-564-3291 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1622GMT \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8h06xu8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
